window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gmch-2024"] = {"id": "gmch-2024", "title": "GMCH Chandigarh 2024", "fullTitle": "GMCH Chandigarh 2024, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "100 real questions · official GMCH key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "In the Simon-Binet test of intelligence, a person with a chronological age (CA) of 40 years has a mental age (MA) of 20 years. The person is intellectually:", "options": {"a": "Below average", "b": "Average", "c": "Above average", "d": "Genius"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: GMCH's own key marks above average, because an adult's chronological age is capped in the ratio IQ. The 'Simon-Binet' test in the stem is the scale that Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built in Paris to find children who needed special teaching. Their 1908 revision graded tasks by age, so a person's mental age (MA) is the age level of the tasks he or she can pass. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA) to get a mental quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet: ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. That works for children, whose mental age grows with real age. It breaks for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so Terman capped the adult chronological age at 16 (the 1937 revision used 15). A 40-year-old is therefore scored as if CA were 16: 20 / 16 x 100 = 125, above average. The 1916 Stanford-Binet's own 'average adult' level was a mental age of 16, so a mental age of 20 sits above it. Example: Arjun, 40, passes the tasks of the 20-year level; with the adult cap his ratio IQ is 125, not 50. Option c is right because for an adult the divisor is the capped age, and a mental age above the adult norm means above-average ability. Exam trap: dividing by the full 40 gives 20 / 40 x 100 = 50, below average (option a); it looks like the obvious sum but ignores the adult cap, the very problem that led David Wechsler (1939) to the deviation IQ. Key note: this mock used to mark a, the plain 20 / 40 sum. GMCH's own key for this paper marks c. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH; if a question gives a child's ages, do the plain MA / CA sum.", "topic": "Ratio IQ calculation", "fig": "Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon"}, {"q": "MMPI is a test of:", "options": {"a": "Intelligence", "b": "Attitudes", "c": "Personality", "d": "Interest"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the MMPI is a test of personality, and in particular of psychopathology. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by the psychologist Starke Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. It is an objective, self-report inventory: the person marks hundreds of statements as true or false, and the answers are scored against fixed norms rather than interpreted freely. Its items were chosen by empirical criterion keying. An item stayed on a scale only if patients with a given diagnosis (for example, depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result is 10 clinical scales, such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia and Schizophrenia, plus validity scales (L for lying, F for rare or infrequent answers, K for defensiveness) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items and is still among the most used personality tests in clinics. For example, when Kabir is referred for low mood and unexplained bodily complaints, his MMPI-2 profile can show raised Depression and Hypochondriasis scales, and the validity scales tell the clinician whether he was faking good or faking bad. Option c is right because the MMPI describes personality traits and clinical patterns, not ability, opinions or vocational likes. Exam trap: the word 'multiphasic' makes some students think of a multi-ability battery; it only means many scales of personality. Also, the MMPI is an objective test, so do not group it with projective tests such as the Rorschach or the TAT.", "topic": "MMPI as personality test", "fig": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "The empty chair technique is one of the techniques of:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic psychotherapy", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Gestalt therapy techniques", "d": "Behaviour therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the empty chair technique belongs to Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; the founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman. It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy. It holds that people carry unfinished business (feelings never expressed, such as grief or anger) and disown parts of themselves, and that healing comes from full awareness in the present moment. In the empty chair, the client imagines a person, or a part of the self, sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly. The client may then switch chairs and answer as that person. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'top dog' (the demanding inner critic) and the 'underdog' (the excuse-making side). For example, Meera's father died before she could tell him how hurt she felt. In the empty chair she speaks to him, says what she never said, and feels the grief finally move. Option c is right because Perls made the technique famous as a way of turning talk about feelings into direct, present experience. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology, founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka, studied perception and the idea that the whole is more than the sum of its parts; the therapy borrowed the word, not the research. Also, Jacob Moreno's psychodrama (1921) used similar role-play, but in exams the empty chair is Gestalt therapy.", "topic": "Gestalt empty chair technique", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "A behavioural psychologist is interested in the behaviour that:", "options": {"a": "Can be observed", "b": "The organism chooses to perform", "c": "Can be inferred", "d": "Is part of the conscious experience"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a behavioural psychologist studies behaviour that can be observed. Behaviourism began with John B. Watson (1913), whose paper 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' argued that psychology should be an objective natural science whose goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. Thoughts, images and feelings cannot be seen or measured by an outside observer, so Watson ruled them out and studied only what an organism does: responses that others can see, count and record, linked to the stimuli that produce them. B. F. Skinner (1938) carried this further in radical behaviourism, explaining behaviour by its consequences in the environment rather than by inner mental causes. For example, a behavioural psychologist working with Arjun, a child who disrupts class, would not ask what he is secretly thinking. She would count how often he leaves his seat in an hour, note what happens just before and just after (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) and then change the consequences. Option a is right because observability is the defining rule of behaviourism: if it cannot be observed and measured, it is not data. Exam trap: neobehaviourists such as Edward Tolman (1932) and Clark Hull (1943) did infer inner 'intervening variables' such as cognitive maps and drive, which makes option c tempting, but the classic textbook definition of the behavioural approach is the study of observable behaviour. Skinner also counted thoughts as private behaviour, yet he still studied people only through observable responses.", "topic": "Behaviourism and observable behaviour", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies helps patients to accept their illness experiences without avoidance?", "options": {"a": "Relaxation", "b": "Stress inoculation", "c": "Acceptance and commitment therapy", "d": "Mindfulness meditation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) teaches patients to accept painful thoughts, feelings and illness experiences without avoiding them. ACT was developed by Steven Hayes from the 1980s, with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (book 1999), and belongs to the 'third wave' of cognitive-behavioural therapy. Its core idea is that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance: the effort to escape or control unwanted inner experiences. Avoidance brings brief relief but narrows life and often makes the distress stronger. Instead of fighting symptoms, ACT builds psychological flexibility through six processes: acceptance (making room for feelings), cognitive defusion (seeing thoughts as just words, as in 'I am having the thought that I am useless'), contact with the present moment, self as context (the observing self), values (what matters to the person) and committed action (doing what matters while discomfort is present). For example, Kabir has chronic back pain and has stopped meeting friends to avoid flare-ups. ACT does not promise to remove the pain. It helps him notice the pain, stop struggling with it, and still go to his cousin's wedding because family matters to him. Option c is right because acceptance without avoidance is the named target of the therapy and the first word of its name. Exam trap: mindfulness meditation (option d) also teaches non-judgemental acceptance, and ACT uses mindfulness exercises, but mindfulness is a practice, not the therapy built around accepting illness experiences and acting on values.", "topic": "Acceptance and commitment therapy", "fig": "Steven Hayes"}, {"q": "Anhedonia is another word for:", "options": {"a": "Weight loss", "b": "Low energy level", "c": "Low frustration tolerance", "d": "Inability to experience pleasure"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: anhedonia is the inability to experience pleasure, or a marked loss of interest and enjoyment in activities that used to be pleasurable. The French psychologist Théodule Ribot (1896) coined the word from Greek roots meaning 'without pleasure'. It is a core symptom of a major depressive episode: DSM-5 requires either depressed mood or markedly diminished interest or pleasure for the diagnosis. It is also one of the negative symptoms of schizophrenia, alongside flat affect, alogia, avolition and asociality. Researchers now separate anticipatory anhedonia (not looking forward to things) from consummatory anhedonia (not enjoying them in the moment). For example, Riya used to love painting and meeting friends; now she does both without feeling anything, and soon she stops. Option d is right because it is the literal meaning of the term. Exam trap: anhedonia is often confused with avolition (lack of drive to start activities), apathy (lack of feeling or concern) and alexithymia (difficulty naming one's own feelings). Low energy and weight loss are separate depressive criteria, which is why they sit in the options as near misses.", "topic": "Meaning of anhedonia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Punishment is effective if it is applied:", "options": {"a": "Immediately", "b": "With a slight delay", "c": "With marked delay", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: punishment works best when it follows the behaviour immediately. Punishment is any consequence that lowers the future rate of the behaviour it follows. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding); negative punishment removes something pleasant (losing phone time). Edward Thorndike (1898) first showed with his puzzle-box cats that consequences shape behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1938) studied them in the operant chamber. Reviewing the animal research, Nathan Azrin and William Holz (1966) listed the conditions under which punishment suppresses behaviour well: it should be immediate, delivered every time the behaviour occurs, strong enough from the start rather than raised gradually, and combined with reinforcement of an acceptable alternative. Timing matters because learning depends on contiguity. The longer the gap, the more other behaviours happen in between, so the consequence becomes linked to whatever the person was doing when it arrived. For example, if Kabir's mother sees him hit his sister and scolds him at once, the link is clear. If she waits until his father comes home in the evening, Kabir may learn only that his father's arrival means trouble. Option a is right because immediate delivery is the best established rule for effective punishment. Exam trap: do not confuse punishment with negative reinforcement, which also involves something unpleasant but increases behaviour by removing it. The immediacy rule applies to reinforcement as well.", "topic": "Timing of punishment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The biomedical model assumes that:", "options": {"a": "All illness can be explained on the basis of aberrant somatic bodily processes", "b": "All illness can be explained on the basis of social factors", "c": "All illness can be explained on the basis of psychological factors", "d": "All illness can be explained on the basis of evolutionary factors"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the biomedical model assumes that all illness can be explained by aberrant somatic (bodily) processes. This model has dominated Western medicine since the nineteenth century. It treats disease as a deviation from normal biological measures, caused by things such as germs, genes, injuries or chemical imbalance. It is reductionist, because it reduces illness to processes in cells and organs, and dualist, because, following René Descartes (1641), it separates mind from body and treats the body as a machine to be repaired. In psychiatry it appears as the medical model: mental disorders are diseases of the brain, to be diagnosed and treated with drugs. The model has great strengths, such as vaccines and antibiotics, but it struggles to explain why two people with the same lesion fare so differently. George Engel (1977) argued in the journal Science for the biopsychosocial model, in which biological, psychological and social factors interact in every illness. For example, Meera has frequent headaches. A purely biomedical doctor orders scans and prescribes painkillers; a biopsychosocial clinician also asks about her exam stress, her sleep and conflict at home. Option a is right because the biomedical model places the cause of all illness in the body alone. Exam trap: the biopsychosocial model is Engel's challenge to the biomedical model, not a version of it, and the 'medical model' in psychiatry is the same disease-of-the-body approach under another name.", "topic": "Biomedical model of illness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to the two-factor theory of emotion, emotion equals:", "options": {"a": "Arousal plus cognition", "b": "Arousal plus intelligence", "c": "Attribution plus explanation", "d": "Attribution plus cognition"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) two-factor theory, emotion equals physiological arousal plus a cognitive label. The theory says arousal itself is general and much the same across emotions: a racing heart, sweating and trembling. What turns it into a particular emotion is how we interpret it, using cues from the situation. Both factors are needed. Arousal without a label is just a stirred-up feeling, and a label without arousal is cold thought. In their classic study, participants were injected with epinephrine (adrenaline) and told it was a vitamin. Some were correctly told it would make their hearts race; others were misinformed or told nothing. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. Those with no explanation for their arousal tended to take on the stooge's mood, while those who knew the drug's effects did so less, because they already had a label for what they felt. For example, Arjun's heart pounds before a cricket final. If he reads it as excitement he feels thrilled; if he reads it as danger he feels afraid. Option a is right because it names the two factors exactly. Exam trap: the theory is also called the cognitive arousal theory. Contrast it with the James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885), in which bodily change is the emotion, and the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927), in which arousal and emotion occur together. Other 'two-factor theories', such as Charles Spearman's (1904) on intelligence and O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) on avoidance, are different topics.", "topic": "Two-factor theory of emotion", "fig": "Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer"}, {"q": "'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance.' Which type of hypothesis is this?", "options": {"a": "Null and directional", "b": "Alternate and directional", "c": "Null and non-directional", "d": "Alternate and non-directional"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' is an alternate and directional hypothesis. A hypothesis is a testable prediction about the relation between variables, and researchers classify it in two ways. The first is null versus alternate. The null hypothesis (H0), named by Ronald Fisher (1935), states that there is no effect or no relation: 'reinforcement has no effect on performance'. The alternate (alternative) hypothesis (H1), formalised by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), states that an effect or relation exists. The second is directional versus non-directional. A directional hypothesis says which way the effect goes (higher, lower, improves, reduces) and is tested with a one-tailed test. A non-directional hypothesis predicts only that there will be a difference ('reinforcement will affect performance') and is tested with a two-tailed test. For example, Meera predicts that students praised after each correct sum will solve more sums than students given no praise. She expects an effect (alternate) in a stated direction (more sums), so she would use a one-tailed test. Option b is right because the statement claims that an effect exists and specifies that the effect is positive. Exam trap: students sometimes label any confident research prediction 'null'; the null is always the 'no difference' statement, and it is the one actually tested statistically. Also, 'positive effect' signals direction just as clearly as 'greater than' does.", "topic": "Types of hypotheses", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The 'fruitful metaphor' concept was given by:", "options": {"a": "George Kelly", "b": "Snygg and Combs", "c": "Medard Boss", "d": "Viktor Frankl"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the 'fruitful metaphor' concept was given by George Kelly (1955). In The Psychology of Personal Constructs, Kelly proposed that we can usefully look at every person as if he or she were a scientist. He called this picture of 'man the scientist' a fruitful metaphor: not a literal claim, but a way of seeing people that produces useful ideas. Like a scientist, each person builds theories about the world, uses them to predict events, tests the predictions in daily life and revises the theories when they fail. Kelly called these theories personal constructs: bipolar ways of construing events, such as friendly versus hostile or clever versus dull. His fundamental postulate states that a person's processes are psychologically channelled by the ways in which he or she anticipates events. Because the same events can always be construed in more than one way, he called his philosophy constructive alternativism. He measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep test) and treated clients with fixed-role therapy, in which the client acts out a new character for a few weeks. For example, Meera construes people as either 'with me or against me'. When a friend's honest criticism turns out to help her, the prediction fails, and she revises the construct. Option a is right because the scientist metaphor is the root of Kelly's whole theory. Exam trap: Kelly's theory is cognitive, yet it is often taught beside humanistic theories, so students wrongly credit the metaphor to phenomenologists such as Donald Snygg and Arthur Combs or to existential thinkers.", "topic": "Kelly's person as scientist", "fig": "George Kelly"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cognitive distortion?", "options": {"a": "Apprehension", "b": "All or None", "c": "Catastrophising", "d": "Personalization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: apprehension is not a cognitive distortion. It is an emotion, an anxious feeling of unease about what might happen. Cognitive distortions are habitual errors in thinking that make events seem worse than they are. Aaron Beck (1963) described them while studying the thinking of depressed patients, and David Burns (1980) popularised a list of ten in Feeling Good. They include all-or-none (dichotomous) thinking, overgeneralisation, mental filter, disqualifying the positive, jumping to conclusions (mind reading and fortune telling), magnification or catastrophising, emotional reasoning, should statements, labelling and personalisation. In Beck's cognitive model, a situation triggers automatic thoughts, the distortions bend those thoughts, and the bent thoughts then produce feelings such as sadness or anxiety. Therapy teaches clients to catch the thought, name the distortion and test it against the evidence. For example, Kabir scores 55 in one test and thinks, 'I am a complete failure and everyone will laugh at me.' That thought shows all-or-none thinking and catastrophising; the apprehension he feels the next morning is the emotional result, not the error itself. Option a is right because apprehension sits on the feeling side of the cognitive model, not on the thinking side. Exam trap: emotional reasoning ('I feel apprehensive, so something bad must be coming') is a distortion, which can make a feeling word look like a thinking error; but the distortion is the faulty conclusion drawn from the feeling, not the feeling itself.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive distortions", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not an IQ test?", "options": {"a": "Bhatia battery", "b": "WAIS", "c": "IDEAS", "d": "MISIC"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: IDEAS is not an IQ test. IDEAS is the Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale, published by the Indian Psychiatric Society (2002) to rate the disability caused by mental illness. It is used in India to certify psychiatric disability so that patients can receive government benefits. The clinician rates the person's functioning in four areas (self-care, interpersonal activities, communication and understanding, and work) and adds a score for the duration of illness; the total converts to a percentage of disability. It measures how much an illness limits daily life, not intelligence. The other three are intelligence tests. C. M. Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence (1955) has five subtests (Kohs Block Design, Alexander Pass-Along, Pattern Drawing, Immediate Memory and Picture Construction) and suits people with little schooling. The WAIS, by David Wechsler (1955), is the standard adult intelligence scale. MISIC is Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, Arthur J. Malin's (1969) Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children. For example, Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for six years, is assessed with IDEAS for a disability certificate, while his younger cousin with learning problems is tested with MISIC to obtain an IQ. Option c is right because IDEAS yields a disability percentage, not an IQ. Exam trap: the acronym looks as if it measures 'ideas' or thinking ability. Also do not confuse it with other Indian tools such as the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA), which is not an IQ test either.", "topic": "IDEAS versus Indian IQ tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"People control basic anxiety by moving toward, away from, and against others.\" Which group of personality psychologists theorised this statement?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic", "b": "Neo-Freudian", "c": "Social Learning", "d": "Cognitive"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the statement comes from Karen Horney (1945), a neo-Freudian. Neo-Freudians kept Sigmund Freud's (1856 to 1939) ideas of unconscious conflict and the importance of childhood, but replaced his sexual and aggressive instincts with social and cultural forces. Horney argued that a child raised with indifference, inconsistency or domination develops basic anxiety: a feeling of being alone and helpless in a potentially hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that she first listed as ten neurotic needs (1942) and then grouped, in Our Inner Conflicts (1945), into three trends. Moving toward people is the compliant type, seeking love and approval. Moving against people is the aggressive type, seeking power and control. Moving away from people is the detached type, seeking independence and privacy. A healthy person uses all three flexibly as the situation needs; a neurotic person relies rigidly on one, which creates inner conflict. For example, Riya, whose parents were cold and unpredictable, always agrees with her friends and cannot say no (moving toward), while her brother Kabir picks fights to stay on top (moving against). Option b is right because Horney is a classic neo-Freudian, alongside Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), Erich Fromm (1900 to 1980) and Harry Stack Sullivan (1892 to 1949). Exam trap: option a tempts because Horney trained as a psychoanalyst, but 'psychoanalytic' in exam options means Freud's instinct-based theory, which Horney openly criticised, for example by rejecting penis envy.", "topic": "Horney's neurotic trends", "fig": "Karen Horney"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a method used in descriptive research?", "options": {"a": "Case study", "b": "Experiment", "c": "Survey", "d": "Naturalistic observation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: an experiment is not a descriptive method. Descriptive research aims to describe behaviour as it is: what happens, how often and in whom. It does not manipulate anything, so it cannot show cause and effect. Its main methods are the case study, the survey and naturalistic observation. A case study is an intensive study of one person or a small group, often over a long time. A survey asks a large sample questions through questionnaires or interviews, as in Alfred Kinsey's (1948) studies of sexual behaviour. Naturalistic observation records behaviour in its usual setting without interfering, as Jane Goodall (1960) did when she began watching chimpanzees at Gombe. An experiment is different in kind. The researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls other factors, ideally assigns participants to conditions at random, and measures the effect on a dependent variable. It is the only method that can establish causation. For example, Meera wants to study students' screen time. Watching them in the canteen or surveying 500 students describes their habits. Randomly assigning half of them to a no-phone week and comparing their sleep with the other half is an experiment. Option b is right because experiments test causes by manipulating variables, while the other three only describe. Exam trap: correlational studies are sometimes grouped with descriptive research because they also manipulate nothing, and a 'field experiment' takes place in a natural setting but is still an experiment, not naturalistic observation.", "topic": "Descriptive research methods", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The therapeutic method of systematic desensitization is believed to work by means of:", "options": {"a": "Implosion", "b": "Paradoxical intentions", "c": "Counter conditioning", "d": "Reactive inhibition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is believed to work by counterconditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the technique on the idea that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time. Deep relaxation is incompatible with anxiety, so if relaxation is paired again and again with a feared stimulus, the old conditioned response (fear) is replaced by a new one (calm). Replacing a conditioned response with an opposite one is counterconditioning, and Wolpe called the underlying process reciprocal inhibition. An early example came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), who freed a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate food he liked. The therapy has three steps: training in progressive muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929), building an anxiety hierarchy of situations ranked from least to most frightening, and then imagining each step while relaxed, moving up only when the current step causes no anxiety. For example, Kabir fears dogs. He starts by picturing a photo of a puppy while relaxed, and weeks later he can imagine stroking a large dog and stay calm. Option c is right because the method replaces fear with relaxation through repeated pairing. Exam trap: reciprocal inhibition (Wolpe) is not reactive inhibition (Clark Hull). Later research suggested that exposure itself, through extinction, may do much of the work, but the classic textbook mechanism is counterconditioning.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation mechanism", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "According to the Indian Lunacy Act, who can be detained in an asylum without a medical certificate?", "options": {"a": "A minor", "b": "A criminal lunatic", "c": "Only a male lunatic", "d": "An elderly lunatic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: under the Indian Lunacy Act 1912, a criminal lunatic could be detained in an asylum without the usual medical certificate. The Act brought together the earlier colonial lunacy laws and governed mental health care in India for most of the twentieth century. It was custodial: its main purpose was to detain 'lunatics' in asylums and protect society, not to treat patients or protect their rights. The usual civil route was a reception order made by a magistrate on a petition, and a petition needed medical certificates confirming that the person was of unsound mind. A criminal lunatic was different. This was a person found to be insane in a criminal proceeding, for example an accused person found unfit to stand trial because of unsoundness of mind. Such a person was sent to the asylum on the order of the court or the government, and that order took the place of the medical certificate. For example, if Ramesh is charged with assault and the court finds him insane, it is the court's order, not a doctor's certificate, that sends him to the asylum. Option b is right because a legal order from the criminal court or the government was itself the authority for detention. Exam trap: students expect the 'criminal' case to need more paperwork, not less. Remember too that the 1912 Act was replaced by the Mental Health Act 1987 (in force 1993), which dropped the word 'lunatic' for 'mentally ill person', and then by the rights-based Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (in force 2018).", "topic": "Indian Lunacy Act 1912", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which subtest is not a subtest of the WAIS-IV?", "options": {"a": "Kohs Block", "b": "Digit Span", "c": "Pass Along test", "d": "Visual Puzzles"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Pass-Along test is not a WAIS-IV subtest. The WAIS-IV (2008) is the fourth edition of the adult intelligence scale of David Wechsler (1939, Wechsler-Bellevue), for ages 16 to 90. It has 15 subtests, 10 core and 5 supplemental, grouped into four index scores. Verbal Comprehension: Similarities, Vocabulary and Information (core), with Comprehension. Perceptual Reasoning: Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles (core), with Picture Completion and Figure Weights. Working Memory: Digit Span and Arithmetic (core), with Letter-Number Sequencing. Processing Speed: Symbol Search and Coding (core), with Cancellation. The Pass-Along test belongs to a different tradition. W. P. Alexander (1932) designed it for his performance battery: the person slides coloured blocks along a tray, without lifting them, until they match a pattern on a card. C. M. Bhatia (1955) later included it in his Indian Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence. For example, Meera, a farm worker who cannot read, might be given the Bhatia battery with Pass-Along and Kohs blocks, while a college student would be given the WAIS-IV with Digit Span and Visual Puzzles. Option c is right because no WAIS-IV subtest uses the Pass-Along task or anything like it. Exam trap: Kohs blocks and Pass-Along sit side by side in the Bhatia battery, so students assume both are Wechsler tasks. Key note: the Kohs Block Design test (Samuel Kohs, 1920) is not literally a WAIS-IV subtest either, but Wechsler's Block Design is built directly on Kohs blocks, so the paper treats it as present. The Pass-Along test has no WAIS counterpart, and c stands.", "topic": "WAIS-IV subtests", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "'Poverty of content' in schizophrenia is when:", "options": {"a": "Speech appears to be detailed in terms of numbers of words, but is grammatically incorrect", "b": "A tendency to jump from one topic to another within a sentence", "c": "Poor use of vocabulary", "d": "Poor use of grammar"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: poverty of content of speech is speech that is adequate in amount, often with plenty of words, but that conveys little information. Nancy Andreasen (1979) defined it in her Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication (TLC). The speech is vague, over-abstract, over-concrete, repetitive or stereotyped, so a long answer can be summed up in a few words. The listener comes away thinking, 'he said a lot but told me nothing.' Andreasen contrasted it with poverty of speech, in which the amount of speech itself is reduced: brief, one-word replies and little spontaneous talk. Both are forms of alogia, a negative symptom of schizophrenia. For example, asked what he did yesterday, Kabir talks for two minutes about 'the forces of life and the way things go on in the general sense of the universe', yet never says what he did. That is poverty of content. If he had simply said 'nothing' and fallen silent, that would be poverty of speech. Option a is right because it is the only option that describes plenty of words carrying little meaning. Exam trap: poverty of content (empty meaning) and poverty of speech (few words) sound alike but are opposite on quantity. Key note: option a gets the core idea right, but its 'grammatically incorrect' tail is off, since in poverty of content the grammar is usually intact. It is still the only option that describes adequate quantity with empty content, and a stands.", "topic": "Poverty of content of speech", "fig": "Nancy Andreasen"}, {"q": "Which one of the following does not measure aptitude?", "options": {"a": "GATB", "b": "TAT", "c": "TAMANNA", "d": "DAT"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the TAT does not measure aptitude; it is a projective test of personality. Aptitude is the potential to learn a skill or succeed in an area given training, such as mechanical, clerical or numerical ability, and aptitude tests predict future learning. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was created by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. The person is shown ambiguous pictures and asked to tell a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The examiner looks for themes of needs (such as achievement or affiliation) and press (forces in the environment), following Murray's (1938) need-press theory. There are no right answers, so the test reveals motives and conflicts rather than abilities. The other three are aptitude batteries. The GATB (General Aptitude Test Battery) was developed by the United States Employment Service for job counselling. The DAT (Differential Aptitude Tests) was published by George Bennett and colleagues (1947) for school guidance. TAMANNA (Try And Measure Aptitude And Natural Abilities) was developed by NCERT with CBSE to help Indian students in Classes 9 and 10 choose their subjects. For example, Riya takes TAMANNA in Class 10 to see her numerical and verbal strengths; if a psychologist wants to understand her fear of failure, she might tell stories to TAT cards. Option b is right because the TAT measures personality dynamics, not the potential to learn. Exam trap: the abbreviations look alike, and examiners rely on students not knowing what each one stands for.", "topic": "Aptitude tests versus TAT", "fig": "Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan"}, {"q": "Re-educative and reconstructive psychotherapies usually focus on the ______ of defences an individual has:", "options": {"a": "Alteration", "b": "Facilitation", "c": "Restoration", "d": "Adoption"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: re-educative and reconstructive psychotherapies usually aim at the alteration of a person's defences. Lewis Wolberg (1954), in The Technique of Psychotherapy, grouped psychotherapies into three classes by their depth and goals. Supportive therapy (reassurance, guidance, persuasion, changing the environment) aims to strengthen existing defences and restore the person to an earlier balance, without trying to change the personality. Re-educative therapy (for example behaviour therapy, directive counselling and relationship therapy) aims at a deliberate readjustment of attitudes and behaviour, modifying how the person copes while largely leaving unconscious conflicts alone. Reconstructive therapy (psychoanalysis and psychoanalytically oriented therapy) aims at insight into unconscious conflicts, so that the character structure itself changes and new growth becomes possible. Both deeper groups therefore set out to change defences rather than shore them up: re-educative therapy modifies them, and reconstructive therapy rebuilds them from the roots. For example, Arjun breaks down after losing his job. Supportive therapy would calm him and help him return to his old way of coping. Re-educative therapy would teach him new ways of handling criticism. Reconstructive therapy would explore why failure feels like annihilation to him, trace it to his childhood and change that pattern at its source. Option a is right because alteration of defences is exactly what separates these two deeper approaches from supportive work. Exam trap: restoration (option c) is the aim of supportive therapy, the near miss that examiners place beside the deeper approaches.", "topic": "Wolberg's levels of psychotherapy", "fig": "Lewis Wolberg"}, {"q": "A fixed interval schedule is one that is based on a:", "options": {"a": "Varying number of responses", "b": "Varying period of time", "c": "Set number of responses", "d": "Set period of time"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: a fixed interval schedule is based on a set period of time. In a fixed interval (FI) schedule, the first response made after a fixed amount of time has passed is reinforced; responses during the interval earn nothing. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) described the four basic partial schedules in Schedules of Reinforcement. Ratio schedules depend on the number of responses and interval schedules on time; 'fixed' means the requirement stays the same, while 'variable' means it changes around an average. FI produces a typical scallop on the cumulative record: a pause just after each reinforcer, then faster and faster responding as the next one becomes due. For example, Meera has a class test every Friday. She barely studies on Saturday and Sunday, but her studying speeds up sharply by Thursday night, a classic FI pattern. Option d is right because the defining requirement of FI is a constant amount of time, not a number of responses. Exam trap: on FI a response is still required, since time alone earns nothing; giving the reinforcer after a fixed time regardless of behaviour is a different schedule, called fixed time. Remember the rule: 'ratio' means number, 'interval' means time. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks c, a set number of responses. That key is wrong: a set number of responses defines a fixed ratio schedule, while a fixed interval is defined by time, so d stays.", "topic": "Fixed interval schedule", "fig": "Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Individuals prone to depression often make ______ and ______ attributions for their problems:", "options": {"a": "Internal, stable", "b": "Internal, unstable", "c": "External, unstable", "d": "External, stable"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: people prone to depression tend to explain their bad events with internal and stable attributions. Learned helplessness was first described by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967): dogs given shocks they could not escape later stopped trying to escape even when they could. To explain why only some people become helpless after setbacks, Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated the theory in terms of attributions, the causes people give for events. They proposed three dimensions. Internal versus external: is the cause me or the situation? Stable versus unstable: will it last or pass? Global versus specific: does it affect everything or just this one area? A depressive attributional style explains bad events as internal ('it is my fault'), stable ('it will always be like this') and global ('I ruin everything'). Internal causes lower self-esteem, stable causes make helplessness last, and global causes spread it to every part of life. For example, when Riya fails her driving test, she thinks, 'I am hopeless and I will never learn anything.' Her friend Meera, who also fails, thinks, 'The examiner was strict today and I was nervous', an external, unstable and specific explanation that protects her mood. Option a is right because internal and stable are the two offered dimensions that match the depressive style. Exam trap: Bernard Weiner's (1985) third attribution dimension is controllability, while the third dimension of Abramson and colleagues is globality; and the self-serving bias is the opposite, non-depressive pattern of blaming outside causes for failure.", "topic": "Depressive attributional style", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "Which is NOT a principle of nonverbal communication?", "options": {"a": "Communication occurs even when language is not used", "b": "It is possible not to communicate nonverbally", "c": "Nonverbal communication is ______ (the word is missing in the printed paper)", "d": "Nonverbal communication is culture-bound"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'it is possible not to communicate nonverbally' is not a principle; the real principle is the reverse. Paul Watzlawick, Janet Beavin Bavelas and Don Jackson (1967), in Pragmatics of Human Communication, stated the first axiom of communication: one cannot not communicate. Every behaviour in the presence of another person carries a message, and there is no such thing as non-behaviour. Silence, looking away, sitting with folded arms or leaving the room all say something, whether or not the person intends it. Nonverbal communication is the exchange of messages through facial expression, eye contact, gesture, posture, touch, use of space and tone of voice. Textbooks list several principles: it occurs even without words, it is continuous, it is often ambiguous, it is culture-bound, it carries much of the emotional and relational meaning, and when it contradicts the words, people usually believe the nonverbal message. For example, Kabir sits silently through a family meeting, staring at his phone. He says nothing, yet everyone reads his boredom or anger. Option b is right because it denies the most basic principle of nonverbal communication. Exam trap: the item asks which is NOT a principle, so the three true principles are the distractors. Also, Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) showed that some basic facial expressions are universal, but display rules and gestures vary by culture, so 'culture-bound' remains a true principle.", "topic": "Principles of nonverbal communication", "fig": "Paul Watzlawick"}, {"q": "Helping someone with a 'severe disorder' of functionality, personality, and aberrations of thoughts, feelings and behaviour is known as:", "options": {"a": "Counselling", "b": "Psychotherapy", "c": "Advising", "d": "Guiding"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: helping someone with a severe disorder of functioning and personality, with marked disturbance of thoughts, feelings and behaviour, is psychotherapy. Psychotherapy is a planned, professional treatment in which a trained therapist uses psychological methods to relieve symptoms, change disturbed patterns of behaviour and promote personality growth, much as Lewis Wolberg (1954) defined it. It is used for clinical conditions such as major depression, personality disorders and obsessive-compulsive disorder. It often goes deeper and lasts longer, and it may aim to change long-standing personality patterns. Counselling, in the classic textbook contrast, serves essentially normal people facing developmental, situational or adjustment problems: choosing a career, exam stress, a break-up. It is usually shorter, focused on the present and on conscious problems, and draws on the person's existing strengths. For example, Meera, who is anxious about her board exams, is well served by a few counselling sessions. Arjun, who has had severe mood swings, self-harm and unstable relationships since his teens, needs psychotherapy. Option b is right because the stem stresses severity, personality-level problems and disturbed thoughts, feelings and behaviour, which are the province of psychotherapy. Exam trap: the line between the two is blurred in practice, and Carl Rogers (1942) used the words almost interchangeably in Counseling and Psychotherapy; but in exams, normal problems map to counselling and severe disorders to psychotherapy.", "topic": "Counselling versus psychotherapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "One important issue in therapy for specific phobias is to address:", "options": {"a": "Ensure the individual never comes in contact with the phobic event or situation", "b": "The phobic beliefs that sufferers hold about their phobic event or situation", "c": "The individual has ample opportunity to talk about the phobic event or situation", "d": "That any dreams about the phobic event or situation are analyzed"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: therapy for a specific phobia must address the phobic beliefs the sufferer holds about the feared object or situation. A specific phobia is an intense, irrational fear of one object or situation (spiders, heights, injections) that the person knows is excessive yet still avoids. Cognitive models say the fear is kept alive by beliefs about danger: a spider phobic may believe a spider will run straight at her, crawl inside her clothes, or that she will lose control or faint with fear. These beliefs overestimate both the likelihood and the cost of harm, and they drive avoidance. Modern treatment therefore combines exposure with cognitive work. Joseph Wolpe (1958) used systematic desensitisation, and Lars-Göran Öst (1989) showed that a single long session of guided exposure can cure many specific phobias. In both, exposure works partly as a behavioural experiment: the client tests the belief and finds it false. Example: Meera is terrified of lizards and believes one will leap onto her face. Her therapist asks her to rate how strongly she holds this belief (90 per cent), then stays with her near a lizard for twenty minutes; it never leaps, and her belief drops to 20 per cent, along with her fear. Option b is right because changing these danger beliefs, through questioning and through exposure that disconfirms them, is the core of effective phobia therapy. Exam trap: the option about never meeting the phobic stimulus sounds kind, but avoidance is exactly what maintains a phobia (O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory, 1947): each escape relieves fear, so avoidance is negatively reinforced and the false belief is never tested.", "topic": "Treating specific phobias", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many chromatic cards does the Rorschach Inkblot Test have?", "options": {"a": "5", "b": "2", "c": "3", "d": "10"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test has five chromatic (coloured) cards out of its ten. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in Psychodiagnostik. It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order from Card I to Card X. Five are achromatic, printed only in black and grey: Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Two are black with bright red patches: Cards II and III. Three are fully multicoloured in pastel shades: Cards VIII, IX and X. Chromatic simply means carrying colour, so the chromatic cards number 2 plus 3, which makes 5. This is why textbooks describe the set as five black-and-white and five coloured. Colour matters in scoring: responses driven by colour (FC, CF, C) are read as signs of emotional responsiveness and control, and a sudden slowing, rejection or disorganisation on the first red card (Card II) or the first all-colour card (Card VIII) was classically called colour shock. Example: Arjun answers Card I fluently, then falls silent for forty seconds at Card II; the examiner notes the delay as a possible reaction to the red. Option a is right because Cards II, III, VIII, IX and X all contain colour, giving five chromatic cards. Exam trap: 3 counts only the fully coloured cards and forgets the red in II and III; 2 counts only the black-and-red pair; 10 is the total number of cards. Key note: some books call only the three pastel cards chromatic and treat II and III as a separate black-and-red group, which is why 3 tempts; but Cards II and III carry bright red, and the usual count of chromatic cards is five, so a stands. GMCH's own key for this paper marks d, 10. That key is wrong: 10 is the total number of Rorschach cards, and five of them (I, IV, V, VI and VII) carry no colour at all, so a stays.", "topic": "Rorschach card colours", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "The therapeutic method of systematic desensitization is believed to work by means of:", "options": {"a": "Implosion", "b": "Paradoxical intentions", "c": "Counter conditioning", "d": "Reactive inhibition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is believed to work by counterconditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the technique on the idea that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time. Deep relaxation is incompatible with anxiety, so if relaxation is paired again and again with a feared stimulus, the old conditioned response (fear) is replaced by a new one (calm). Replacing a conditioned response with an opposite one is counterconditioning, and Wolpe called the underlying process reciprocal inhibition. An early example came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), who freed a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate food he liked. The therapy has three steps: training in progressive muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929), building an anxiety hierarchy of situations ranked from least to most frightening, and then imagining each step while relaxed, moving up only when the current step causes no anxiety. For example, Kabir fears dogs. He starts by picturing a photo of a puppy while relaxed, and weeks later he can imagine stroking a large dog and stay calm. Option c is right because the method replaces fear with relaxation through repeated pairing. Exam trap: reciprocal inhibition (Wolpe) is not reactive inhibition (Clark Hull). Later research suggested that exposure itself, through extinction, may do much of the work, but the classic textbook mechanism is counterconditioning.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation mechanism", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Kohlberg is known for his theory of:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive development", "b": "Social development", "c": "Physical development", "d": "Moral development"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Lawrence Kohlberg is known for his theory of moral development. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), in his doctoral work at the University of Chicago, extended Jean Piaget's (1932) ideas on children's moral judgement into a stage theory of moral reasoning. He posed moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma (should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife?), and scored the reasons people gave, not their yes or no answers. He described three levels with two stages each. At the preconventional level, right is what avoids punishment (Stage 1) or serves one's own interest (Stage 2). At the conventional level, right is what wins approval (Stage 3, good boy-nice girl) or upholds law and order (Stage 4). At the postconventional level, right rests on social contract and individual rights (Stage 5) and on universal ethical principles (Stage 6). The stages are said to follow an invariant order and to depend on cognitive growth. Example: asked about Heinz, Arjun (8) says he should not steal or he will go to jail (Stage 1), while his sister Riya (16) says he should steal because a good husband looks after his wife (Stage 3). Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory as biased towards a male ethic of justice and proposed an ethic of care. Option d is right because Kohlberg's stages describe how moral reasoning develops. Exam trap: because Kohlberg was a cognitive-developmental theorist who built on Piaget, cognitive development (a) is the near miss; but the cognitive stages (sensorimotor to formal operations) are Piaget's, and Kohlberg's own theory is about morality.", "topic": "Kohlberg's moral development", "fig": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, {"q": "Orientation, attention span, recent memory and remote memory are included in which part of the mental status examination?", "options": {"a": "Appearance", "b": "Behavior", "c": "Cognition", "d": "Thought processes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: orientation, attention span, recent memory and remote memory are tested in the cognition section of the mental status examination (MSE). The MSE is a structured, here-and-now description of a patient's mental state, recorded at interview much as a physical examination records the body. It usually runs: appearance, behaviour (and speech), mood and affect, thought (form, stream and content), perception, cognition, insight and judgement. The cognitive part checks the higher functions in a set order: level of consciousness; orientation to time, place and person (time is usually lost first, for example in delirium); attention and concentration (digit span, serial sevens from 100, months backwards); memory, immediate (repeat three words), recent (recall them after five minutes, or describe yesterday's meals) and remote (childhood events, past personal history); general information and intelligence; and abstract thinking (proverbs, similarities). Bedside tools such as the Mini-Mental State Examination (Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975) package many of these tests. Example: Kabir, aged 72, cannot say the date or the name of the hospital and recalls none of three words after five minutes, yet describes his school days in detail; his recent memory is impaired and his remote memory preserved, a pattern typical of early dementia. Option c is right because orientation, attention and memory are cognitive functions, grouped under cognition (often called the sensorium) in the MSE. Exam trap: thought processes (d) sounds mental enough to attract candidates, but it describes the form and flow of thinking (flight of ideas, loosening of associations, thought block), not orientation or memory.", "topic": "MSE cognition section", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ is a strategy or course of action enabling a person to live an acceptable life with a problem that may be intractable and incapable of solution.", "options": {"a": "Investment", "b": "Solution", "c": "Congruence", "d": "Resolution"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: a resolution is a strategy or course of action that lets a person live an acceptable life with a problem that may be intractable and incapable of solution. In counselling, problems are of two broad kinds. Some can be solved: a solution removes the problem altogether, as when a student who failed an examination through poor study habits learns a study plan and passes. Others cannot be removed: a chronic illness, a permanent disability, a bereavement, a family member who will not change. For these, insisting on a solution only breeds frustration and a sense of failure. The counsellor instead helps the client find a resolution: a way of understanding and managing the problem so that life is still satisfying, through new coping skills, changed expectations, support and acceptance. The problem remains, but its hold over the person's life shrinks. Example: Riya, aged 30, has type 1 diabetes. No counselling will cure it, so the goal is not a solution. With her counsellor she builds a routine for insulin and meals, joins a support group and plans her work around clinic days; she now describes her life as full, with diabetes in the background. That is a resolution. Option d is right because the stem's key words, living an acceptable life with a problem that is incapable of solution, define resolution, the counterpart of solution. Exam trap: solution (b) shares the stem's subject, problems, and is the word most people reach for, but the stem itself says the problem is incapable of solution, so the strategy cannot be a solution.", "topic": "Solution versus resolution", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An IQ ranging between 70 and 80 is:", "options": {"a": "Mild mental retardation", "b": "Low average IQ", "c": "Borderline IQ", "d": "Average IQ"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: an IQ of about 70 to 79 falls in the borderline range. A modern IQ is a deviation IQ: David Wechsler (1939) compared each person with others of the same age, on a scale with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Score bands are named by their distance from the mean. In the Wechsler classification, 130 and above is very superior, 120 to 129 superior, 110 to 119 high average, 90 to 109 average, 80 to 89 low average, 70 to 79 borderline, and 69 and below extremely low, the range where intellectual disability is considered. A score of 70 lies 2 standard deviations below the mean, at about the 2nd percentile, so the borderline band sits just above the cut-off for intellectual disability. ICD-10 grades intellectual disability (formerly mental retardation) as mild (50 to 69), moderate (35 to 49), severe (20 to 34) and profound (below 20), and both ICD and DSM-5 also require deficits in adaptive behaviour. Example: Kabir, aged 12, scores 75 on the WISC. He reads two years behind his class and needs extra help, but he dresses, travels and shops on his own; he has borderline intellectual functioning, not intellectual disability. Option c is right because 70 to 79 is the borderline band, just below low average and just above the range for mild mental retardation. Exam trap: the stem's upper end, 80, really belongs to the low average band (80 to 89), which tempts candidates towards b; read the range as 70 to 79, which is borderline.", "topic": "Wechsler IQ bands", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The apparent lack of empathy and the tendency to exploit others for self-benefit have led psychologists to compare narcissistic personality disorder with which one of the following?", "options": {"a": "Histrionic Personality Disorder", "b": "Antisocial Personality Disorder (APD)", "c": "Paranoid Personality Disorder", "d": "Schizotypal Personality Disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: narcissistic personality disorder is often compared with antisocial personality disorder (APD), because both show a lack of empathy and a readiness to exploit others for personal gain. Both are Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, erratic) disorders in DSM-5. Narcissistic personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy; two of its nine criteria are being interpersonally exploitative (taking advantage of others to achieve one's own ends) and lacking empathy. APD is a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others since age 15, with deceitfulness, impulsivity, aggression, irresponsibility and lack of remorse. The overlap is strong enough that Otto Kernberg (1984) described malignant narcissism, a severe form in which narcissism mixes with antisocial behaviour, aggression and paranoid traits, and Robert Hare's psychopathy checklist (PCL-R, 1991) includes a grandiose sense of self-worth. The difference lies in motive and style: the narcissist exploits mainly to protect an inflated but fragile self-image and win admiration, while the antisocial person exploits for material gain or thrills, often breaking the law. Example: Vikram takes credit for a junior's report because he believes he deserves the spotlight (narcissistic); his cousin forges his employer's signature to take money, then shrugs it off (antisocial). Option b is right because lack of empathy and exploitation are core features shared by narcissistic and antisocial personality disorders. Exam trap: histrionic personality disorder (a) is also Cluster B and also craves attention, but its hallmark is emotional, seductive display, not cold exploitation, so it is only the near miss.", "topic": "Narcissistic versus antisocial PD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If we talk about the nature of psychotherapies, which of the following is not true?", "options": {"a": "It requires a systematic application", "b": "It is a dynamic process", "c": "It has a fixed procedure for all clients", "d": "Only trained professionals can practise it"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychotherapy does not follow a fixed procedure for all clients; it is tailored to each person. Psychotherapy is a voluntary, structured relationship in which a trained therapist uses psychological methods to help a client change troubling thoughts, feelings and behaviour. Standard descriptions of its nature (for example in the NCERT Class 12 psychology text used across India) list several features: it involves the systematic application of principles drawn from a theory of therapy; only persons who have received practical training under expert supervision should practise it; the therapeutic situation involves a therapist and a client who seeks help; and the heart of the work is the therapeutic relationship, which changes over the course of treatment, making therapy a dynamic process. What stays fixed is the framework (confidentiality, goals, boundaries); the techniques, pace and focus are chosen to fit the client's problem, history, culture and readiness. Gordon Paul (1967) put the guiding question well: what treatment, by whom, is most effective for this individual with that specific problem, under which set of circumstances? Example: Riya, who has panic attacks, and Arjun, who is grieving his father, see the same counsellor; Riya gets interoceptive exposure and breathing work, while Arjun gets space to mourn and help rebuilding his routines. Same therapist, different procedures. Option c is right because a single fixed procedure for every client contradicts the individualised, dynamic nature of psychotherapy. Exam trap: in a NOT item, the systematic application in a can be misread as a rigid, fixed procedure; systematic means guided by principles, not identical for everyone.", "topic": "Nature of psychotherapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term IQ was given by:", "options": {"a": "William Stern", "b": "Alfred Binet", "c": "David Wechsler", "d": "Terman"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term IQ (intelligence quotient) was given by William Stern. Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision grouped items by age, so a child's level could be expressed as a mental age (MA). A mental age alone is awkward: being two years behind means much more at age 4 than at 14. William Stern (1912), a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), calling the result the Intelligenzquotient, the intelligence or mental quotient. Lewis Terman (1916), revising the scale at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove decimals and made the abbreviation IQ famous: ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. A child of 10 with a mental age of 12 therefore scores 120. David Wechsler (1939) later replaced the ratio with the deviation IQ, comparing a person with others of the same age (mean 100, standard deviation 15), because mental age stops growing in adulthood. Example: Meera, aged 8, passes the items typical of 10-year-olds; Stern's quotient is 10/8 = 1.25, and Terman's ratio IQ is 125. Option a is right because Stern coined both the quotient and the term in 1912. Exam trap: Binet is the classic distractor because he created the test and the mental-age idea, but he never used an intelligence quotient. Key note: Terman is the near miss, since he added the x100 and popularised IQ in English, which is why some books credit him; but the concept and the word were Stern's, so a stands.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "Gillian works as a cognitive therapist. She is always very conscientious about attending to the client's experience and responding to the client's needs in the moment. Most of her clients would describe her as warm and supportive. Gillian most likely attends to which ONE of the following constructs associated with cognitive therapies?", "options": {"a": "The importance of learning", "b": "The importance of cognitions", "c": "The importance of therapeutic empathy", "d": "The importance of operational definitions and functional analysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Gillian is attending to the importance of therapeutic empathy. Cognitive therapies are often pictured as cool and technical, but Aaron Beck and his colleagues, in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979), stressed that the therapist's warmth, accurate empathy and genuineness are necessary, though not sufficient, for change. These qualities come from Carl Rogers (1957), who named empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence as the core conditions of therapy. Therapeutic empathy means sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own and communicating that understanding, moment by moment. In cognitive therapy it has a purpose: a client who feels understood trusts the therapist, discloses painful automatic thoughts and joins the collaborative work of testing them. Research on the working alliance shows that a warm, attuned relationship predicts better outcomes across therapies. Gillian's behaviour (attending to the client's experience, responding to needs in the moment, being seen as warm and supportive) describes empathy, not any technique. Example: when Riya starts crying while filling in a thought record, her therapist pauses the exercise, reflects that this brings up a lot of sadness, and returns to the record only when Riya is ready. Option c is right because warmth, attunement and responsiveness to the client's immediate experience are the marks of therapeutic empathy. Exam trap: the importance of cognitions (b) is the defining construct of cognitive therapy, so it looks like the safe pick, but nothing in the stem mentions thoughts or beliefs; the stem is about the relationship.", "topic": "Empathy in cognitive therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which function of communication is illustrated when a person rolls his or her eyes while making a negative comment about another?", "options": {"a": "Substituting", "b": "Accenting", "c": "Regulating", "d": "Contradicting"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: rolling the eyes while making a negative comment illustrates accenting, a nonverbal cue that emphasises part of a verbal message. Nonverbal behaviour (face, eyes, gesture, posture, voice) works alongside words. Mark Knapp (1972), building on Paul Ekman's work in the 1960s, listed six functions it can serve in relation to speech. Repeating restates the words (saying 'go straight' and pointing ahead). Complementing adds to or fits the words (a sad face while apologising). Substituting replaces words (a nod for yes, a shrug with no speech). Accenting stresses or underlines the words (banging the table on 'never', a raised tone on a key word). Regulating manages the flow of conversation (nodding to keep someone talking, raising a hand to take a turn, looking away to signal the end). Contradicting sends a message opposite to the words ('I'm fine' said through tears). Eye-rolling during a negative remark points the same way as the words and intensifies their scorn, so it accents them. Example: Kabir says 'Rohan's presentation was a disaster' and rolls his eyes on 'disaster'; the roll does not replace, regulate or contradict his words, it underlines his contempt. Option b is right because the eye-roll emphasises the negative message the person is already giving. Exam trap: contradicting (d) is chosen because eye-rolling is famous as a sign of sarcasm; it would contradict only if the words were positive, such as 'great idea' with an eye-roll, but with a negative comment the two channels agree.", "topic": "Functions of nonverbal communication", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Jackie was passed over for a promotion in favour of a colleague with more seniority and greater experience. She is upset because she thinks her boss does not consider her qualified. What would a CBT therapist do to help Jackie?", "options": {"a": "Empathize with Jackie because she has been unfairly treated", "b": "Help her generate realistic alternative explanations", "c": "Agree with her that her boss deemed her not qualified", "d": "Advise her to find a better job elsewhere"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: a CBT therapist would help Jackie generate realistic alternative explanations for being passed over. Cognitive behavioural therapy, rooted in Aaron Beck's (1976) cognitive model, holds that our interpretation of events, more than the events themselves, shapes how we feel. Jackie's event is the lost promotion; her automatic thought is 'my boss thinks I am not qualified'; her emotion is hurt and upset. The thought is an example of mind reading, a form of jumping to conclusions in David Burns's (1980) list of distortions: she is sure what her boss thinks without evidence. The therapist neither argues with her nor simply reassures her. Through Socratic questioning and collaborative empiricism, the therapist helps her examine the evidence: the colleague had more seniority and greater experience, the company may promote by length of service, and her boss may rate her highly but have followed those rules. Jackie then weighs these alternatives, settles on the most realistic one, and notices how her mood changes. She might even test the thought by asking her boss for feedback, a behavioural experiment. Example: in a similar case, Priya rates her belief 'I'm not good enough' at 85 per cent; after listing other possible reasons for the decision, she rates it at 30 per cent and feels calmer. Option b is right because generating and testing realistic alternative explanations is the core cognitive restructuring step in CBT. Exam trap: empathising (a) sounds caring, and empathy does matter in CBT, but option a also declares that she was treated unfairly, which the facts in the stem do not support; CBT empathises with the feeling while questioning the interpretation.", "topic": "CBT alternative explanations", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Shell shock was later termed:", "options": {"a": "OCD", "b": "ADHD", "c": "DPD", "d": "PTSD"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: shell shock was later termed posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). During the First World War, soldiers exposed to heavy shelling developed tremors, mutism, paralysis, nightmares, panic and an exaggerated startle. Charles Myers (1915), a British psychologist, brought the term shell shock into the medical literature, at first thinking the blast itself damaged the nerves; but many cases arose in men never near an explosion, showing the cause was psychological trauma. The condition was later called war neurosis, then combat or battle fatigue in the Second World War, and Abram Kardiner (1941) described The Traumatic Neuroses of War. DSM-I (1952) listed gross stress reaction, and after studies of Vietnam veterans, DSM-III (1980) introduced PTSD. DSM-5 (2013) moved it into a new chapter of trauma- and stressor-related disorders, with four symptom clusters (intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in mood and cognition, and arousal) lasting more than a month. Option d is right because PTSD is the modern diagnosis for the trauma reaction once called shell shock. Exam trap: DPD can stand for depersonalisation disorder, which involves feeling detached and can follow trauma, so it tempts; but it is a dissociative disorder, not the successor of shell shock.", "topic": "Shell shock to PTSD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A delinquency offense can be defined as:", "options": {"a": "An act committed by a minor, for which an adult could not be prosecuted in a criminal court", "b": "An act committed by a minor, for which an adult could be prosecuted in a criminal court", "c": "An act committed by a minor as a result of disequilibrium of homeostasis", "d": "An act committed by a minor with the absence of any rational motive"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: a delinquency offence is an act committed by a minor for which an adult could be prosecuted in a criminal court. Juvenile delinquency is the legal term for criminal behaviour by young people below the age of adulthood (18 in India). Two kinds of juvenile misconduct are separated. A delinquency offence is an act that would be a crime at any age: theft, assault, robbery, vandalism, drug dealing. Its definition rests on the act itself, and the young person is dealt with by a juvenile system rather than an adult court. A status offence is an act that is illegal only because of the young person's age: truancy, running away from home, underage drinking, breaking curfew. An adult doing the same would not be prosecuted. In India, the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2015 calls a minor accused of an offence a child in conflict with law, and allows those aged 16 to 18 accused of heinous offences to be tried as adults after a preliminary assessment. Psychologically, delinquency overlaps with conduct disorder, and Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated adolescence-limited from life-course-persistent offenders. Example: Arjun, 15, steals a mobile phone from a shop; an adult would face criminal charges for theft, so this is a delinquency offence. His friend Rahul skips school for a month; that is a status offence. Option b is right because a delinquency offence is an act by a minor that would be prosecutable as a crime if an adult did it. Exam trap: options a and b differ by a single word, could versus could not; a, an act for which an adult could not be prosecuted, is the definition of a status offence.", "topic": "Delinquency versus status offences", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Consider the following statements in relation to psychosis. A: Psychosis refers to disordered thought and behaviour. B: Psychosis is a symptom that may result from several primary psychiatric disorders, general medical conditions and substance use. Which of the following options is right?", "options": {"a": "Only A", "b": "Only B", "c": "Both A and B", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: both statements are correct. Psychosis is a mental state in which a person's contact with reality is seriously impaired. Its main features are hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus, such as hearing voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs held against evidence), disorganised thinking and speech (derailment, incoherence) and grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, usually with poor insight. So Statement A, that psychosis refers to disordered thought and behaviour, captures its core description. Statement B is also true: psychosis is a syndrome, not one disease, and it can arise from many causes. Primary psychiatric causes include schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, brief psychotic disorder, and severe mania or depression with psychotic features. General medical causes include delirium, brain tumours, epilepsy (especially of the temporal lobe), encephalitis, thyroid disease and dementia. Substance causes include amphetamines, cocaine, cannabis, LSD, steroids and alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens). DSM-5 recognises this directly with the categories psychotic disorder due to another medical condition and substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder. Example: Kabir, 25, starts hearing voices after a week of heavy amphetamine use; his doctor first rules out drugs and medical illness before considering schizophrenia, because psychosis is a symptom with many possible sources. Option c is right because A gives the defining features of psychosis and B correctly says it can come from psychiatric, medical or substance causes. Exam trap: Statement A can look too loose, since the textbook core of psychosis is lost reality testing, so some candidates pick only B; but disordered thought and behaviour are central to psychosis, and A is accepted as true.", "topic": "Nature and causes of psychosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person is lying inside a scanner, taking part in an experiment after an injection of 2-deoxyglucose. This person is participating in:", "options": {"a": "An EEG study", "b": "An MEG study", "c": "An fMRI study", "d": "A PET study"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: a scanner plus an injection of 2-deoxyglucose means a positron emission tomography (PET) study. PET measures brain activity through metabolism. A glucose-like tracer labelled with a positron-emitting isotope, usually fluorine-18 in fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), is injected. Active neurons take up more glucose, so they take up more tracer, and because 2-deoxyglucose cannot be fully broken down, it stays trapped in the cells. Each emitted positron meets an electron and releases a pair of gamma rays, which detectors in the ring-shaped scanner record and turn into a map of activity. The method grew from Louis Sokoloff's (1977) deoxyglucose technique for measuring local glucose use in animal brains. PET is used in research on cognition and in clinical work to find tumours, epileptic foci and the reduced metabolism of Alzheimer's disease; it involves radiation and has poor time resolution. Option d is right because an injected radioactive deoxyglucose tracer is the signature of PET. Exam trap: fMRI (c) also uses a scanner and maps activity, so it tempts, but it needs no injection; it tracks the BOLD signal produced by changes in blood oxygen.", "topic": "PET and 2-deoxyglucose", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All of the following statements (except for one) can characterize a professional helping relationship. Which ONE statement does NOT represent a professional helping relationship?", "options": {"a": "A helping relationship is initially structured by the counselor or therapist but is open to cooperative restructuring based upon the needs of the client", "b": "A helping relationship is simply one in which one person does something for the other", "c": "A helping relationship is one in which all persons involved perceive the existence of trust, caring, concern, and commitment and act accordingly", "d": "A helping relationship is one that provides for the personal growth of all persons involved"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: a professional helping relationship is not simply one in which one person does something for the other. Carl Rogers (1958) defined a helping relationship as one in which at least one party intends to promote the growth, development, maturity, improved functioning and improved coping of the other. Counselling texts list its marks. It is structured at first by the counsellor or therapist (time, place, roles, confidentiality, goals), but the structure is open to cooperative revision as the client's needs become clear. All the people involved perceive trust, caring, concern and commitment, and act on them. And it allows personal growth for everyone in it, the helper included, who learns and develops through the work. What it is not is a one-way service in which the helper takes over and fixes things. Doing things for clients breeds dependence and tells them they cannot cope, which defeats the aim of helping. Gerard Egan, in The Skilled Helper (1975), framed the goal as helping clients manage their own problems and develop unused resources. Example: Meera, a counsellor, could phone her client Ananya's college to sort out an attendance problem; instead she helps Ananya plan and rehearse the conversation, and Ananya makes the call herself, gaining confidence for next time. Option b is right because a helping relationship is collaborative and growth-focused, not one person doing something for another. Exam trap: b matches the everyday meaning of helping, which is why it slips past candidates in a NOT item; in counselling, helping means enabling the client, not acting on the client's behalf.", "topic": "Professional helping relationship", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A teacher's praise can be an effective motivator if:", "options": {"a": "Praise is delivered contingently", "b": "Praise focuses students' attention on their neighbour's task-relevant behaviour", "c": "Praise is delivered just for showing up for class", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a teacher's praise motivates effectively when it is delivered contingently, that is, only after and because of the specific behaviour or achievement it is meant to strengthen. In operant terms (B. F. Skinner, 1953), praise is a social reinforcer, and a reinforcer strengthens behaviour only when it depends on that behaviour. Jere Brophy (1981), in a classic review of teacher praise, found that much classroom praise is ineffective because it is given randomly, globally or simply to manage the class. He listed the features of effective praise: it is delivered contingently, specifies what was done well, sounds sincere and spontaneous, rewards reaching a stated standard rather than mere participation, gives information about the student's competence, and orients students towards their own task-relevant behaviour and effort rather than comparing them with others. Later work by Claudia Mueller and Carol Dweck (1998) added that praising effort and strategy, rather than fixed ability, helps children persist after failure. Example: Ms Sharma tells Kabir, 'You checked every step of that long division, and that is why you got it right.' The praise comes right after the work, names the behaviour and links success to his effort, so Kabir starts checking his steps more often. Option a is right because contingency is the condition that makes praise work as a reinforcer. Exam trap: b borrows Brophy's phrase task-relevant behaviour but points it at the neighbour's work; effective praise directs students to their own behaviour, while comparison with peers is a mark of ineffective praise.", "topic": "Contingent teacher praise", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which ONE of the following statements is true about using person-centred theory in brief approaches?", "options": {"a": "Even brief-intervention counsellors have come to recognise the need for applying person-centred core conditions in their practices", "b": "Person-centred counselling cannot be used in a brief-therapy approach because of its focus on the personal characteristics of the counsellor and the client", "c": "Person-centred counselling cannot be used in a brief-therapy approach because it has no set of identified techniques", "d": "Person-centred counselling has a brief approach that allows it to be effective in as little as one session"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: even counsellors who use brief interventions have come to recognise that they need the person-centred core conditions in their practice. Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that therapeutic change needs a relationship in which the counsellor offers three core conditions: empathy (understanding the client's world from the inside), unconditional positive regard (warm, non-judgemental acceptance) and congruence (genuineness). Classic person-centred counselling is non-directive and open-ended, so it is not itself a brief therapy. But brief approaches, which aim to help within a few sessions, have borrowed its relational core. Solution-focused brief therapy (Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg, from the 1980s) depends on a respectful, collaborative stance; motivational interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, 1991) describes itself as a client-centred method; and research on common factors shows that the alliance predicts outcome whatever the length of therapy. Short-term work makes a strong relationship more important, not less, because there is no time to repair a bad start. Example: in a three-session college counselling service, Arjun is referred for exam stress. His counsellor uses a scaling question and sets one small goal, but she also listens empathically and accepts his fears without judgement; Arjun later says the acceptance was what made him try the plan. Option a is right because the core conditions have become a recognised ingredient of brief interventions. Exam trap: c leans on a true fact, that person-centred counselling avoids fixed techniques, to reach a false conclusion; the core conditions are attitudes that travel into any format, including brief work.", "topic": "Person-centred conditions in brief therapy", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Transactional Analysis (TA) is related to:", "options": {"a": "Learning", "b": "Attitudes", "c": "Perception", "d": "Personality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: transactional analysis (TA) is related to personality. Eric Berne (1961), a Canadian-born American psychiatrist, set out TA in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and popularised it in Games People Play (1964). TA is both a theory of personality and a method of therapy. Its personality structure has three ego states, each a coherent pattern of feeling, thinking and behaving: the Parent (attitudes and rules taken over from parents, nurturing or critical), the Adult (realistic, here-and-now processing of information) and the Child (feelings and impulses carried from childhood, free or adapted). A transaction is a unit of social exchange between ego states: complementary transactions flow smoothly, crossed ones break communication, and ulterior ones carry hidden messages. Berne also described strokes (units of recognition), games (repeated exchanges with a hidden payoff) and life scripts (unconscious life plans formed in childhood). Thomas Harris (1967), in I'm OK, You're OK, spread the four life positions. Example: Riya's manager snaps, 'Why is this report late again?' (Critical Parent to Child). If Riya replies, 'It's always my fault' (Child), the transaction is complementary; if she replies calmly, 'The data came on Friday; I can finish by noon' (Adult), she moves it towards an Adult-to-Adult exchange. Option d is right because TA describes the structure of personality in ego states and uses it to analyse behaviour. Exam trap: TA analyses communication between people, so candidates sometimes link it to social topics such as attitudes or perception; but its ego states are a personality model, and TA is classified under personality and psychotherapy.", "topic": "Transactional analysis", "fig": "Eric Berne"}, {"q": "Each of the following answers (except for one) describes a theoretical assumption of REBT. Which ONE does NOT describe a theoretical assumption of REBT?", "options": {"a": "REBT ascribes to the idea that cognitions or beliefs cause emotions and behavior", "b": "REBT theorists stress the interconnectedness of thinking, feeling and behaving", "c": "Other people do not make us feel bad or good", "d": "Other people can make us feel bad or good"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: the statement that other people can make us feel bad or good is not an assumption of rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT); it is the very idea REBT rejects. Albert Ellis (1955) founded the approach as rational therapy, later renamed it rational emotive therapy, and in 1993 rational emotive behaviour therapy. Its ABC model says that A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, and C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People usually think A causes C; Ellis held that B causes C. Healthy, rational beliefs are flexible preferences ('I would like her to approve of me'); unhealthy, irrational beliefs are rigid demands (musts and shoulds), awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of self or others. Therapy adds D, vigorous disputing of irrational beliefs, and E, an effective new philosophy. Ellis also insisted that thinking, feeling and behaving are interconnected, which is why behaviour joined the name and why he set homework such as shame-attacking exercises. He liked to quote the Stoic Epictetus: people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. Example: Kabir's friend ignores him at a party. If Kabir believes 'he must respect me, and it is awful that he did not', he feels furious; if he believes 'I'd prefer he greeted me, but perhaps he was distracted', he feels mildly disappointed. Same event, different beliefs, different feelings. Option d is right because REBT holds that we disturb ourselves through our beliefs, so others cannot directly make us feel bad or good. Exam trap: c and d are exact opposites; in a NOT item, the negative wording of c (others do not make us feel bad) is the true REBT assumption, and d is the false one.", "topic": "REBT core assumptions", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "Externalizing our unacceptable feelings and attributing them to others describes which Freudian defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Denial", "b": "Projection", "c": "Displacement", "d": "Reaction formation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: externalising our unacceptable feelings and attributing them to others is projection. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety caused by unacceptable impulses or painful realities. Sigmund Freud (1911) used projection to explain paranoia in his analysis of the Schreber case, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, placed it in the ego's catalogue of defences. In projection, a feeling or wish the person cannot accept in himself (hostility, envy, sexual desire, dishonesty) is pushed outward and seen in someone else. The person then feels threatened by, or critical of, the other person instead of facing the impulse within. Projection is common in everyday life and extreme in paranoid states; George Vaillant (1977) ranked it among the immature defences. The same idea underlies projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT, which assume that people read their own needs into ambiguous material. Example: Riya resents her new colleague and wishes her to fail, but she cannot admit such meanness to herself. Instead she becomes convinced that the colleague dislikes her and is plotting against her. Option b is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to another person is the definition of projection. Exam trap: displacement (c) also moves a feeling away from its source, so it is the near miss; but displacement redirects the feeling onto a safer target (shouting at a younger brother after a scolding from the boss) while still owning it, whereas projection disowns the feeling and places it in someone else.", "topic": "Projection as a defence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which method of calculating internal consistency reliability uses variance of the whole test for estimating coefficient?", "options": {"a": "Spearman-Brown prophecy formula", "b": "Rulon and Flanagan formulas", "c": "Kuder-Richardson formulas", "d": "Coefficient Alpha"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Rulon and Flanagan formulas estimate split-half reliability directly from the variance of the whole test, without stepping up a half-test correlation. In split-half reliability, a test is given once and divided into two comparable halves, often odd versus even items. Correlating the two half-scores describes a test only half as long, so it underestimates the full test's reliability. Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910) corrected this with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula: r full = 2r half / (1 + r half). Two later formulas work with variances instead. Phillip Rulon (1939) treated the difference between each person's two half-scores as error: reliability = 1 minus (variance of the difference scores / variance of total scores). John Flanagan (1937) used the half variances: reliability = 2 x [1 minus (sum of the two half variances / whole-test variance)]. The two give the same answer, and neither assumes the halves have equal variances. Example: Meera splits a 40-item anxiety scale into odd and even halves. The total-score variance is 20, the difference-score variance is 4, and each half has variance 6. Rulon gives 1 minus 4/20 = .80; Flanagan gives 2 x (1 minus 12/20) = .80. Option b is right because these split-half formulas are the ones built on whole-test variance. Exam trap: Spearman-Brown (a) is the best-known split-half formula, but it uses the half-test correlation, not the whole-test variance. Key note: KR-20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) and coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) also have total-test variance in their denominators, so c and d are near misses; but the textbook line about computing reliability from the variance of the whole test, without the half-test correlation, refers to Rulon and Flanagan, so b stands.", "topic": "Rulon and Flanagan formulas", "fig": "Phillip Rulon and John Flanagan"}, {"q": "MMPI stands for:", "options": {"a": "Multidimensional Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "b": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "c": "Multi Multiphasic Persona Inventory", "d": "Mini Multiphasic Personality Index"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: MMPI stands for Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built it at the University of Minnesota Hospitals to help diagnose psychiatric patients. Multiphasic means it measures many aspects of personality and psychopathology at once. It was made by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item's content. The original had 566 true-or-false statements (550 distinct, 16 repeated), ten clinical scales (such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia, Hypomania and Social Introversion) and the validity scales L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness). Revisions followed: the MMPI-2 (1989, 567 items), the MMPI-A for adolescents, the MMPI-2-RF (2008, 338 items) and the MMPI-3 (2020). In India, H. N. Murthy (1964) built a shorter Multiphasic Questionnaire on the same model. Option b is right because Minnesota, home of the university where it was made, is the first M in MMPI. Exam trap: d's Mini borrows from short screening tools such as the Mini-Mental State Examination, and a's Multidimensional sounds scientific, but the first M is Minnesota.", "topic": "MMPI full form", "fig": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "The assumption that individuals have unique patterns of ability that can be measured and matched with jobs is a key concept of:", "options": {"a": "Trait and factor theory", "b": "Developmental theory", "c": "Self-concept theory", "d": "Construct theory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the idea that each person has a unique, measurable pattern of abilities that can be matched with the demands of a job is the core of trait and factor theory. Frank Parsons (1909), the father of vocational guidance, set out the approach in Choosing a Vocation. His three steps were: know yourself (your aptitudes, abilities, interests, resources and limits), know the world of work (the requirements, pay and prospects of different jobs), and match the two by 'true reasoning'. Traits are the measurable characteristics of the person; factors are the requirements of the job. E. G. Williamson (1939) turned this into the directive 'Minnesota' style of counselling in How to Counsel Students, in which the counsellor tests, diagnoses and advises. John Holland's (1959) theory of vocational personalities, later known as RIASEC, is a modern descendant that matches person and work environment on six types. Example: a school counsellor gives Kabir aptitude and interest tests, finds high spatial and mechanical scores, and suggests engineering or architecture because those job demands fit his profile. Option a is right because only trait and factor theory starts from stable, measurable individual differences and treats career choice as an act of matching them to jobs. Exam trap: examiners use Donald Super's ideas as the distractors. Super's theory is developmental (career choice unfolds across life stages) and treats an occupation as a way of implementing the self-concept, so 'self-concept theory' sounds close, but it describes how people see themselves over time, not test-based matching of abilities to jobs.", "topic": "Trait and factor career theory", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Psychologists following the psychodynamic approach believe that in clinical work, objective tests are of little use because:", "options": {"a": "They are less reliable", "b": "It is difficult to be sure of them", "c": "Subjects are not usually aware of the unconscious determinants of their behaviour", "d": "It is difficult to interpret the results"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychodynamic clinicians think objective tests are of little use because people are not usually aware of the unconscious determinants of their behaviour. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) argued that the motives, wishes and conflicts that matter most are unconscious and are kept out of awareness by defences such as repression. An objective test, such as a self-report inventory like the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley (1943), asks direct questions ('I often feel tense') with fixed answers. It can only collect what the person consciously knows about himself and is willing to admit. If the real cause of a symptom is hidden, even honest answers miss it. This is why the psychodynamic tradition prefers projective techniques. Lawrence Frank (1939) named them 'projective methods': an ambiguous stimulus, such as a Rorschach inkblot or a TAT picture, lets the person project inner needs and conflicts without knowing what is being measured, so defences have less to guard against. Example: on a questionnaire Ananya reports a warm relationship with her father, yet her TAT stories keep showing daughters abandoned by cold fathers; a psychodynamic clinician would give more weight to the stories. Option c is right because the psychodynamic objection is about access: objective tests tap conscious self-report, while the theory places the causes of behaviour in the unconscious. Exam trap: option a sounds technical but is reversed. Objective tests are usually more reliable than projective tests; the psychodynamic complaint is about what they can reach, not about how consistent they are.", "topic": "Psychodynamic critique of objective tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Under certain conditions, Yates correction is applied in:", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Friedman's ANOVA", "c": "Kruskal-Wallis H test", "d": "Chi-square test"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: Yates correction is applied in the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) built the chi-square test to compare observed frequencies (O) with the frequencies expected under the null hypothesis (E): chi-square is the sum of (O minus E) squared divided by E across all cells. The chi-square distribution is continuous, but counts move in whole numbers, so in small tables the formula overstates chi-square and gives too many false positives (Type I errors). Frank Yates (1934) proposed a correction for continuity: subtract 0.5 from each absolute difference between O and E before squaring. The 'certain conditions' are a 2 x 2 table (1 degree of freedom) in which expected frequencies are small, usually when any expected cell is below 5. Example: Arjun compares 12 treated and 10 untreated patients on 'improved or not improved'; two expected cells come to about 4, so he applies Yates correction, which lowers chi-square and makes the test more cautious. For very small tables, Ronald Fisher's exact test (described in The Design of Experiments, 1935) computes the exact probability instead. Option d is right because only the chi-square test approximates whole-number frequency counts with a continuous distribution, and the 0.5 adjustment corrects exactly that mismatch. Exam trap: all three distractors are non-parametric tests, so a student who links Yates with 'non-parametric' or 'small samples' may pick one of them. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman are rank tests; Yates correction belongs to frequency tables, specifically the 2 x 2 chi-square.", "topic": "Yates continuity correction", "fig": "Frank Yates"}, {"q": "Which one of the following methods is used by researchers to make the most exacting study of naturally occurring behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Experimental research", "b": "Systematic observation of behaviour", "c": "Survey", "d": "Field experiment"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: GMCH's own key marks the field experiment, the method that brings an experiment's precision to behaviour in its everyday setting. A field experiment manipulates an independent variable in a real-world place (a street, a classroom, a hotel) instead of a laboratory, usually with people who do not know they are being studied. Because the researcher controls the independent variable and assigns conditions at random, it can show cause and effect, which is what makes it more exacting than watching alone. Because it happens in the field, the behaviour measured is the person's ordinary behaviour, so ecological validity stays high. Classic examples: Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969) staged a collapse on a New York subway train to study helping, and Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson (1968) told teachers that some pupils were about to bloom, then measured those pupils' gains. Example: to test whether a smile raises tips, a researcher has waiters in a real cafe smile broadly at randomly chosen tables and not at others, and compares the tips. Option d is right because it joins the control of an experiment to natural settings and natural behaviour. Exam trap: systematic observation (b) also studies naturally occurring behaviour with clear categories and trained observers, but it cannot show cause and effect, so it is precise description, not the most exacting test. Key note: this mock used to mark b, because observation leaves the behaviour untouched. GMCH's own key for this paper marks d. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH.", "topic": "Field experiment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The symbolic function substage is a part of the ______ in Piaget's theory of cognitive development.", "options": {"a": "Sensorimotor stage", "b": "Preoperational stage", "c": "Concrete operational stage", "d": "Formal operational stage"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the symbolic function substage is the first part of the preoperational stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980). Piaget described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). Around the second birthday the symbolic function appears: the ability to let one thing (a word, a mental image, a gesture, a toy) stand for something else. It shows in rapid language growth, pretend play, early drawings and deferred imitation. Most texts split the preoperational stage into the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4) and the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7), when children ask endless 'why' questions and reason by hunch rather than logic. Thought in the symbolic substage is still limited by egocentrism (seen in Piaget's three mountains task) and animism (believing objects are alive). Example: three-year-old Meera holds a banana to her ear and 'talks to Nani' on her pretend phone; the banana is a symbol for the phone. Option b is right because the symbolic function is the new achievement that opens the preoperational stage, a stage named for what the child still cannot do: perform mental operations such as conservation. Exam trap: the sensorimotor stage ends with the beginnings of mental representation (substage 6, about 18 to 24 months), so students slide symbolic thought back into it. The substage actually named 'symbolic function', however, is the first part of the preoperational stage.", "topic": "Piaget's preoperational substages", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "To assess the convergent validity of a new test of self-esteem, a researcher should establish that:", "options": {"a": "It correlates highly with a different test of self-esteem", "b": "It does not correlate too highly with a different test of self-esteem", "c": "It correlates highly with a test of narcissism", "d": "It does not correlate too highly with a test of narcissism"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: convergent validity is shown when a new self-esteem test correlates highly with a different test of self-esteem. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) defined construct validity as evidence that a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) split that evidence into two parts in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity: measures of the same construct, even by different methods, should agree, that is, correlate highly. Discriminant (divergent) validity: measures of different constructs should not correlate too highly, or the new test is really measuring something else. A test needs both. Example: Arjun writes a new self-esteem questionnaire for college students. He gives it together with the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (Morris Rosenberg, 1965) to 200 students, and a correlation of about .70 supports convergent validity. He then checks that it correlates only modestly with a narcissism inventory, which supports discriminant validity, since healthy self-esteem and narcissistic grandiosity are related but not the same thing. Option a is right because 'convergent' means two measures converge on the same construct, and a strong correlation with another self-esteem test is exactly that evidence. Exam trap: option d is a true and useful finding, but it is discriminant validity, not convergent. Examiners pair the two terms because they come together in Campbell and Fiske's matrix. Remember 'same construct, high correlation' for convergent and 'different construct, low correlation' for discriminant.", "topic": "Convergent versus discriminant validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a single-group pre-test and post-test design, which one of the following statistical methods can best be employed to evaluate the mean difference between the pre- and post-tests?", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Randomised ANOVA", "c": "Chi-Square", "d": "Repeated measures ANOVA"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: of the options given, repeated measures ANOVA is the best way to test the mean difference between pre-test and post-test in a single group. In a single-group pre-test post-test design (the 'one-group pretest-posttest design' of Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, 1963), the same people are measured twice, so the two sets of scores are dependent: each person's post-test is linked to their own pre-test. A within-subjects analysis uses that link. It removes stable differences between people from the error term and tests only the change within each person, which gives more statistical power. Repeated measures ANOVA, an extension of Ronald Fisher's (1925) analysis of variance, handles two or more measurements of the same participants on an interval scale. With only two time points it gives the same result as a paired t-test, since F equals t squared. Example: a clinic rates Rohan and 29 other patients on an anxiety scale before and after eight weeks of CBT, and asks whether the mean fall is larger than chance. Option d is right because it is the only option that treats the pre and post scores as related measurements of the same people. Exam trap: Mann-Whitney U is a rank test for two independent groups, so using it here ignores that the same people were measured twice. The correct non-parametric test for paired scores would be the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945). Key note: a paired (dependent) t-test is the usual first choice for two measurements of one group, and it would be the answer if it were offered; since it is not, repeated measures ANOVA, its general form, is the best option, and d stands.", "topic": "Repeated measures analysis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The unconscious feminine component of men is known as:", "options": {"a": "Animus", "b": "Shadow", "c": "Anima", "d": "Archetype"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the analytical psychology of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the anima is the unconscious feminine component of a man. Jung divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and a deeper collective unconscious shared by all humans. The collective unconscious holds archetypes: inherited, universal patterns that shape how we perceive and react, visible in myths, dreams and religions across cultures. The anima is the archetype of the feminine in a man, and the animus is the archetype of the masculine in a woman. Jung thought each person carries qualities of the other sex, shaped by the long human history of living with the opposite sex. The anima appears in dreams and in the image a man projects onto the women he is drawn to; a man who denies his anima may seem emotionally cut off. Example: Vikram keeps dreaming of a wise, gentle woman who guides him through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read her as his anima. Option c is right because anima is Jung's exact term for the feminine side of men. Exam trap: anima and animus are swapped on purpose. Remember that anima ends in 'a', like many feminine Latin words, and is the feminine within men; animus is the masculine within women.", "topic": "Jung's anima", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is best trained to treat people with schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Counseling", "b": "Clinical", "c": "Developmental", "d": "Psychometric"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: clinical psychologists are the psychologists best trained to assess and treat severe mental disorders such as schizophrenia. Lightner Witmer (1896) opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania and gave the field its name. A clinical psychologist is trained in psychopathology, diagnosis, psychological assessment and psychotherapy, with long supervised work in hospitals and clinics. In India, registration as a clinical psychologist has traditionally required an M.Phil in Clinical Psychology recognised by the Rehabilitation Council of India, and the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 counts clinical psychologists among mental health professionals. In schizophrenia the psychiatrist usually prescribes antipsychotic medicine, while the clinical psychologist provides the psychological side: diagnostic assessment, cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis, family psychoeducation to lower expressed emotion, social skills training and cognitive remediation. Example: after Kabir's first psychotic episode, the psychiatrist starts him on medication, and the clinical psychologist runs family sessions with his parents and uses CBT to help him test the beliefs he holds about his voices. Option b is right because clinical psychology is the specialty whose training centres on serious psychopathology and its treatment. Exam trap: counselling psychology sounds close because it also involves therapy, but counselling psychologists mainly help people in the normal range with adjustment, career, relationship and stress problems, not with psychotic disorders.", "topic": "Clinical versus other psychologists", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Freud's personality construct that contains the psychic energy to power all personality systems and is guided by the pleasure principle is the:", "options": {"a": "Id", "b": "Ego", "c": "Superego", "d": "Libido"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923, The Ego and the Id), the id contains the psychic energy that powers all the personality systems and is guided by the pleasure principle. The id is present at birth, wholly unconscious and made up of the instincts: Eros, the life instincts whose energy Freud called libido, and Thanatos, the death instinct added in 1920. It seeks immediate discharge of tension, which is the pleasure principle, and it thinks by primary process: wish-fulfilling images that ignore logic, time and reality, as in dreams. The ego grows out of the id to deal with the outside world. It follows the reality principle and secondary process (realistic thinking and planning), but it has no energy of its own and borrows it from the id. The superego, formed around age 5 to 6 as the Oedipus complex is resolved, holds the conscience and the ego ideal. Example: hungry baby Aditya screams at once, with no idea of waiting, because only his id is at work; years later his ego lets him wait for dinner and his superego stops him taking his sister's share. Option a is right because the id is both the reservoir of psychic energy and the system run by the pleasure principle. Exam trap: libido is the near miss. Libido is the energy itself (the energy of the life instincts), not a personality system; the stem asks for the structure that contains the energy, which is the id.", "topic": "Freud's id", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Shyness, seclusiveness and interpersonal difficulties are characteristic of which personality disorder?", "options": {"a": "Histrionic personality disorder", "b": "Avoidant personality disorder", "c": "Schizoid personality disorder", "d": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: shyness, seclusiveness and interpersonal difficulties are the classic description of schizoid personality disorder. The stem's wording comes from the early manuals: DSM-II (1968) described schizoid personality as 'shyness, oversensitivity, seclusiveness, avoidance of close or competitive relationships, and often eccentricity', and DSM-I (1952) drew a similar shy, seclusive, 'shut-in' picture, descended from the 'shut-in personality' of August Hoch (1910). In the modern DSM, schizoid personality disorder sits in Cluster A (odd or eccentric): a pervasive detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression. The person neither wants nor enjoys close relationships, almost always chooses solitary activities, takes pleasure in few things, has no close friends and seems indifferent to praise or criticism, coming across as cold and flat. Avoidant personality disorder, in Cluster C (anxious or fearful), looks similar from outside but has the opposite motive: the person longs for relationships but stays away because of feelings of inadequacy and fear of criticism or rejection. Example: Ishaan eats alone and has no friends. If he is content that way and unmoved by others, the pattern is schizoid; if he aches to join the group but fears being laughed at, it is avoidant. Option c is right because seclusiveness and withdrawal from relationships are the defining schizoid pattern in the wording the stem uses. Exam trap: modern readers see 'shyness' and jump to avoidant. Ask why the person is alone: lack of desire is schizoid, fear of rejection is avoidant. Key note: DSM-III (1980), influenced by Theodore Millon (1969), split avoidant personality off from the old schizoid category, so under DSM-5 a stem stressing fear of rejection would point to avoidant. With the old schizoid wording centred on seclusiveness, c stands.", "topic": "Schizoid versus avoidant personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which is a behavioural therapy that has proven successful in treating phobias?", "options": {"a": "Electroconvulsive shock treatment", "b": "Group therapy", "c": "Psychosurgery", "d": "Systematic desensitisation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: systematic desensitisation is the behavioural therapy with a proven record in treating phobias. Joseph Wolpe (1958, Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition) developed it from animal work on conditioned fear. The logic is counterconditioning: a phobia is a learned fear response, and a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment (reciprocal inhibition). Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy: about 10 to 15 situations ranked from mildly to intensely frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDs, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each scene, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when the current scene no longer causes anxiety. In vivo versions use real situations instead. Peter Lang and David Lazovik (1963) showed in a controlled study that it reduced snake phobia in students. Example: Priya, terrified of dogs, first pictures a photo of a puppy, then a dog across the street, then patting a calm dog, relaxing at each step until the fear fades. Option d is right because it is a learning-based technique designed specifically for phobic anxiety and supported by controlled research. Exam trap: all the distractors are real treatments, so students pick by familiarity. Ask which one is behavioural: ECT and psychosurgery are biological treatments, and group therapy is a format, not a specific behavioural technique.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation for phobias", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "The amplification method is used in:", "options": {"a": "Motivation", "b": "Emotion", "c": "Is a lie detector", "d": "Dream interpretation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: amplification is a method of dream interpretation from the analytical psychology of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961). Sigmund Freud (1900) used free association: the patient starts from a dream element and lets thoughts run away from it along a chain of associations until they reach the hidden wish. Jung thought this led away from the dream itself. He saw dreams as direct, creative messages from the unconscious, not disguises, so he stayed with the dream image and enlarged it. Amplification has two layers. Personal amplification gathers the dreamer's own associations to the image, circling back to it again and again. Collective (objective) amplification then adds parallels from myth, religion, fairy tales, folklore and alchemy, to show the archetype the image expresses. Example: Meera dreams of a snake shedding its skin. Her own associations bring up her recent move away from home; the analyst adds that the snake is a symbol of renewal in many myths, and reads the dream as a message about her own transformation. Option d is right because amplification is a technique for interpreting dream images, Jung's alternative to Freud's free association. Exam trap: the word 'amplification' sounds as if it belongs to physiology or emotion (amplifying arousal, amplifying signals in a lie detector), and the distractors exploit that. In psychology exams the term belongs to Jung's dream work, alongside active imagination.", "topic": "Jung's amplification", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Out of the following options, which one includes all of the others?", "options": {"a": "Autonomic nervous system", "b": "Peripheral nervous system", "c": "Somatic nervous system", "d": "Parasympathetic nervous system"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the peripheral nervous system includes all the others. The nervous system has two main parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is every nerve outside them: 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves. The PNS divides into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensation from skin, muscles and joints and sends motor commands to skeletal (voluntary) muscles, and the autonomic nervous system, which controls the heart, glands and smooth muscle without conscious effort. The autonomic system divides again into the sympathetic division (fight or flight: faster heart, dilated pupils, slowed digestion) and the parasympathetic division (rest and digest: slower heart, more saliva, active digestion). So the hierarchy runs PNS, then somatic and autonomic, then sympathetic and parasympathetic inside the autonomic. Example: when Neha picks up her pen, her somatic system moves her fingers; when she sees her exam result and her heart races, her sympathetic division is at work; when she relaxes afterwards, the parasympathetic division takes over. Option b is right because the somatic, autonomic and parasympathetic systems are all subdivisions of the peripheral nervous system. Exam trap: the autonomic system also contains one of the other options (the parasympathetic), so students stop one level too low; it does not contain the somatic system, so only the PNS includes all three.", "topic": "Divisions of the nervous system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these stimulates a muscle to contract?", "options": {"a": "Adrenal hormones", "b": "Receptors", "c": "Sensory neurons", "d": "Motor neurons"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: motor neurons stimulate muscles to contract. Neurons are classed by function. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry signals from receptors to the central nervous system; interneurons connect neurons inside the brain and spinal cord; motor (efferent) neurons carry commands out from the CNS to effectors, the muscles and glands. A motor neuron's cell body lies in the spinal cord or brainstem, and its axon runs out to a muscle. Where it meets a muscle fibre, at the neuromuscular junction, it releases acetylcholine, which depolarises the fibre and triggers contraction. Charles Sherrington (1906) called the motor neuron the 'final common path', because every reflex and every voluntary command must pass through it to move a muscle. When motor neurons die, as in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis or polio, muscles weaken and waste even though sensation stays intact. Example: Riya touches a hot pan; receptors and sensory neurons send the signal to her spinal cord, interneurons relay it, and motor neurons make her arm muscles pull her hand away, the classic reflex arc. Option d is right because only motor neurons carry the signal that directly makes skeletal muscle contract. Exam trap: 'sensory neurons' is the reversed option. Sensory neurons carry information in towards the CNS; motor neurons carry commands out to the muscles.", "topic": "Motor neurons and muscle contraction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The part of the brain most closely associated with maintaining balance and the coordination of complex sequences of movements is the:", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Cerebellum", "c": "Pons", "d": "Medulla"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the cerebellum is the part of the brain most closely tied to balance and the coordination of complex sequences of movement. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the hindbrain, behind the pons, and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It compares the movement the cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the vestibular (balance) organs of the inner ear, and corrects errors moment by moment, so movements come out smooth, accurate and well timed. It also stores motor skills learned by practice, such as cycling or typing. Damage causes ataxia: a wide, staggering gait, poor balance, slurred speech and an intention tremor that appears as the hand nears a target. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask drivers to walk a straight line or touch their nose. Example: Meera, a Bharatanatyam dancer, can link dozens of steps and hand gestures without thinking because her cerebellum has learned the whole sequence. Option b is right because balance and the coordination of skilled movement sequences are the cerebellum's defining job. Exam trap: the pons and medulla sit right next to the cerebellum in the hindbrain, so students mix them up. The medulla runs vital functions and the pons relays signals and helps regulate sleep; only the cerebellum coordinates movement and balance.", "topic": "Cerebellum and motor coordination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Loss of the ability of the brain to produce adequate levels of dopamine often leads to:", "options": {"a": "Aphasia", "b": "Alzheimer's disease", "c": "Parkinson's disease", "d": "Bipolar disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: loss of the brain's ability to produce enough dopamine leads to Parkinson's disease. James Parkinson (1817) first described the condition as the 'shaking palsy' in An Essay on the Shaking Palsy. The cause is the death of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra of the midbrain. These neurons project to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen) along the nigrostriatal pathway, part of the basal ganglia circuit that starts and smooths movement. Symptoms usually appear only after most of these neurons have been lost. The four motor signs are a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness and difficulty starting movement) and postural instability; patients also show a shuffling gait and a mask-like face, and many develop depression and, later, dementia. Treatment replaces the missing chemical: L-dopa, a precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier and is turned into dopamine, came into use in the 1960s. Example: Sana's grandfather has a hand that trembles at rest but steadies when he reaches for his tea, and he walks with small shuffling steps; his neurologist prescribes levodopa. Option c is right because Parkinson's disease is the classic disorder of dopamine depletion. Exam trap: Alzheimer's disease is the other famous neurodegenerative disorder, but it is linked to loss of acetylcholine (with plaques and tangles), not dopamine. Note also that too much dopamine activity, not too little, is linked to schizophrenia and mania.", "topic": "Dopamine and Parkinson's disease", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A relatively enduring behavioural change brought about by experience is called:", "options": {"a": "Learning", "b": "Habituation", "c": "Growth", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a relatively enduring change in behaviour brought about by experience is the definition of learning. The standard wording descends from Gregory Kimble (1961): learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour, or in the potential for behaviour, that results from experience or practice. Each part of the definition does work. 'Relatively permanent' rules out short-lived changes caused by fatigue, drugs, illness or a passing mood. 'Experience' rules out maturation, which is change on a biological timetable, and instinct. 'Potential' matters because learning is inferred from performance: a student may have learned something but not show it until she is tested. The main kinds are classical conditioning, operant conditioning, observational learning and insight learning. Example: after burning her hand once on a hot iron, Riya always checks the iron before touching it; one experience produced a lasting change. Option a is right because only learning combines both parts of the stem: the change lasts, and it comes from experience. Exam trap: habituation is a real but very simple form of learning (a fading response to a repeated, harmless stimulus, like no longer noticing the hum of a ceiling fan), so 'all of the above' tempts. But habituation is only one narrow type and often short-lived, and growth is a biological increase in size driven by maturation, not experience, so 'all of the above' fails. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks d, all of the above. That key is wrong: growth is a biological increase in size driven by maturation, not by experience, so it cannot be learning, and a stays.", "topic": "Definition of learning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The process of learning:", "options": {"a": "Improves adjustment", "b": "Improves efficiency", "c": "Is continuous", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: learning improves adjustment, improves efficiency and is continuous, so all three statements describe it. Educational psychology texts list the characteristics of learning in just these terms. Learning improves adjustment: it lets a person cope with new demands from the environment, from a child learning to share toys to an adult learning new software at work. Learning improves efficiency: practice cuts errors and time, so the task is done faster and with less effort. Edward Thorndike (1898) showed this with cats in puzzle boxes, whose escape times fell steadily over trials, giving some of the earliest animal learning curves. Learning is continuous: it goes on throughout life, from infancy to old age, is not confined to school, and each new piece builds on what came before. Other standard characteristics are that learning is a change in behaviour, is purposive or goal-directed, results from experience and can transfer to new situations. Example: when Arjun moves from Patna to Chennai for college, he learns Tamil phrases and the bus routes (adjustment), gets quicker at the commute each week (efficiency) and keeps picking up new skills all through his years there (continuity). Option d is right because a, b and c are each standard characteristics of learning, and none contradicts the others. Exam trap: each of a, b and c is true on its own, so a hasty student picks the first true statement and moves on. When every option reads as a true property of the concept, check whether 'all of the above' is offered before choosing.", "topic": "Characteristics of learning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) is published by the:", "options": {"a": "American Psychological Association", "b": "American Psychiatric Association", "c": "World Health Organization", "d": "Research Diagnostic Criteria"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association. The manual began as DSM-I in 1952 and has been revised as DSM-II (1968), DSM-III (1980), DSM-III-R (1987), DSM-IV (1994), DSM-IV-TR (2000), DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022). DSM-III was the turning point: led by Robert Spitzer (1980), it introduced explicit diagnostic criteria and a multiaxial system, which DSM-5 later dropped. The DSM is the main classification in the United States and in much research. Many countries, including India, also use the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10, now ICD-11) for official records. Example: a psychiatrist in Delhi may code Meera's diagnosis by ICD for hospital records but discuss the DSM-5-TR criteria at a case conference. Option b is right because the psychiatric, not the psychological, association writes and publishes the DSM. Exam trap: both bodies are abbreviated APA. The American Psychological Association publishes the psychologists' ethics code and the Publication Manual (APA referencing style), not the DSM. Remember 'DSM: psychiatric, for diagnosis'.", "topic": "DSM and its publisher", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Under certain conditions, Yates correction is applied in:", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Friedman's ANOVA", "c": "Kruskal-Wallis H test", "d": "Chi-square test"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: Yates correction is applied in the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) built the chi-square test to compare observed frequencies (O) with the frequencies expected under the null hypothesis (E): chi-square is the sum of (O minus E) squared divided by E across all cells. The chi-square distribution is continuous, but counts move in whole numbers, so in small tables the formula overstates chi-square and gives too many false positives (Type I errors). Frank Yates (1934) proposed a correction for continuity: subtract 0.5 from each absolute difference between O and E before squaring. The 'certain conditions' are a 2 x 2 table (1 degree of freedom) in which expected frequencies are small, usually when any expected cell is below 5. Example: Arjun compares 12 treated and 10 untreated patients on 'improved or not improved'; two expected cells come to about 4, so he applies Yates correction, which lowers chi-square and makes the test more cautious. For very small tables, Ronald Fisher's exact test (described in The Design of Experiments, 1935) computes the exact probability instead. Option d is right because only the chi-square test approximates whole-number frequency counts with a continuous distribution, and the 0.5 adjustment corrects exactly that mismatch. Exam trap: all three distractors are non-parametric tests, so a student who links Yates with 'non-parametric' or 'small samples' may pick one of them. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman are rank tests; Yates correction belongs to frequency tables, specifically the 2 x 2 chi-square.", "topic": "Yates continuity correction", "fig": "Frank Yates"}, {"q": "'Individual psychology' is a term used specifically to refer to the psychological method or science founded by:", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Hoffman", "d": "Freud"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: individual psychology is the psychological method and science founded by Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937). Adler was an early member of Sigmund Freud's circle in Vienna but broke away in 1911, rejecting Freud's view that sexual drives are the main force in personality, and named his new school individual psychology. 'Individual' comes from the Latin individuum, 'indivisible': Adler saw each person as an indivisible whole moving purposefully towards goals, not a battleground of id, ego and superego. Its key ideas are feelings of inferiority (growing out of his 1907 work on organ inferiority) and compensation for them, striving for superiority or perfection, fictional finalism (guiding goals that are useful fictions), the style of life, social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), birth order and early recollections. Therapy is an educational partnership that uncovers the client's mistaken goals and encourages social interest. Example: Kabir, a younger brother who always felt small beside his brilliant elder brother, becomes a fiercely competitive athlete; an Adlerian would see compensation for felt inferiority shaping his style of life. Option b is right because Adler himself coined 'individual psychology' as the name of his system. Exam trap: Carl Jung also left Freud, around 1913, and founded his own school, so students mix up the names. Jung's school is analytical psychology, Adler's is individual psychology and Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Adler's individual psychology", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "In Pavlov's original experiment, meat was the:", "options": {"a": "Unconditioned stimulus", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Unconditioned response", "d": "Conditioned response"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the original experiments of Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes), meat powder was the unconditioned stimulus. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, won the Nobel Prize in 1904 for his work on digestion. While collecting dogs' saliva through a tube in the cheek, he noticed that the dogs began to salivate before the food arrived, at the sight of the attendant or the sound of footsteps. He called this 'psychic secretion' and set out to study it systematically. The terms are as follows. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS), meat powder, naturally triggers the unconditioned response (UCR), salivation, without any learning. A neutral stimulus, such as a metronome or tone, is presented just before the meat. After repeated pairings the neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation on its own, now called the conditioned response (CR). Example: Arjun's dog drools when its food is served (UCS to UCR), and after weeks of hearing the steel bowl clink first, drools at the clink alone (CS to CR). Option a is right because meat produces salivation automatically, without prior training, which is the definition of an unconditioned stimulus. Exam trap: salivation appears on both sides, so students confuse stimulus with response. Meat and the metronome are stimuli; salivation is the response, unconditioned when caused by meat and conditioned when caused by the metronome.", "topic": "Pavlov's unconditioned stimulus", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "A mature defence mechanism is:", "options": {"a": "Denial", "b": "Isolation", "c": "Humor", "d": "Projection"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: humour is a mature defence mechanism. George Vaillant (1977, Adaptation to Life) ranked defences in a four-level hierarchy, using the Grant Study, which has followed a group of Harvard men since 1938. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial and distortion. Level II, immature: projection, fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation (including isolation of affect), repression, displacement, reaction formation and dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, humour, sublimation, suppression and anticipation. Mature defences handle painful feelings while keeping them in awareness and without harming oneself or others. Humour lets a person say openly what hurts in a way that eases tension for everyone; it differs from wit used to wound, which is closer to passive aggression. Vaillant found that men who relied on mature defences had better marriages, careers and physical and mental health, and that defences tended to mature with age. Example: after failing her driving test, Meera jokes to her family, 'At least the traffic cones will remember me,' and books another lesson; she faces the disappointment while keeping her spirits and her relationships intact. Option c is right because humour sits in Vaillant's mature level. Exam trap: isolation sounds calm and controlled, so students take it for mature. Isolation of affect (keeping an idea but stripping away its feeling) is a neurotic defence in Vaillant's scheme.", "topic": "Vaillant's mature defences", "fig": "George Vaillant"}, {"q": "In classical conditioning, the _______ becomes capable of eliciting a response previously triggered by the unconditioned stimulus.", "options": {"a": "Aversive stimulus", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Secondary reinforcer", "d": "Primary reinforcer"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in classical conditioning, the conditioned stimulus becomes capable of eliciting a response that was first triggered only by the unconditioned stimulus. Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed the process in dogs. An unconditioned stimulus (UCS), such as food, naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR), salivation. A neutral stimulus, such as a tone, at first produces nothing beyond an orienting 'what is that?' reaction. When the tone is repeatedly presented just before the food (acquisition), it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation by itself, the conditioned response (CR). Timing matters: conditioning is strongest when the CS comes shortly before the UCS (delay or forward conditioning) and weak when it comes after (backward conditioning). Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that the CS must also predict the UCS, not merely occur alongside it. Example: Riya once got food poisoning after eating prawns at a wedding, and now the mere smell of prawns makes her feel sick. The smell has become a conditioned stimulus that triggers the nausea first caused by the illness. Option b is right because the whole point of classical conditioning is that a once-neutral stimulus takes on the eliciting power of the UCS, and that stimulus is, by definition, the CS. Exam trap: options c and d are operant terms from the work of B. F. Skinner (1938). Reinforcers follow a behaviour and change how often it is made; in classical conditioning the stimulus comes before the response and elicits it.", "topic": "The conditioned stimulus", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The forces that govern the initiation and persistence of behavior are called:", "options": {"a": "Emotions", "b": "Motives", "c": "Intellectual capacities", "d": "Personality attributes"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the forces that start (initiate), direct and keep up (persist) behaviour are called motives. A motive is an inner state, a need or a desire, that energises behaviour and points it towards a goal. Textbooks describe motivation by three features: activation (starting the behaviour), direction (choosing one goal over others) and persistence (keeping at it despite obstacles), with intensity sometimes added as a fourth. Motives are inferred, not seen: we watch what a person does, how hard and for how long, and infer the motive behind it. They fall into two broad families. Primary (biological) motives such as hunger, thirst and sleep arise from bodily needs; secondary (learned or social) motives such as achievement, power and affiliation are acquired through experience. Theories differ on where motives come from: instinct theory (William McDougall, 1908), drive reduction theory (Clark Hull, 1943), arousal theory and incentive theory. Example: Arjun wakes at 5 a.m. to practise for the state cricket trials and keeps training through a hot summer even after a poor match. His wish to be selected is the motive: it started the behaviour, set its direction and kept it going. Option b is right because the initiation and persistence of goal-directed behaviour is the textbook definition of motivation, and motives are the forces that produce it. Exam trap: emotions (a) also energise behaviour, and the two words share the Latin root movere (to move), so examiners use emotion as the near miss; but emotions are felt reactions to events, while motives are the push towards goals.", "topic": "Definition of motives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "'Archetype' is a term associated with:", "options": {"a": "Jung", "b": "Freud", "c": "Adler", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'archetype' is a term of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the Swiss psychiatrist who founded analytical psychology after his break with Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) around 1913. Jung divided the psyche into three layers: the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed material, organised into emotionally charged clusters called complexes) and the collective unconscious, a deeper layer inherited by all humans from our ancestral past. The contents of the collective unconscious are the archetypes: inborn, universal patterns or predispositions to perceive and respond in certain ways. We never see an archetype directly; it shows itself in recurring images in myths, religions, dreams and folk tales across cultures. The main archetypes are the persona (the social mask we show others), the shadow (the dark, rejected side of the personality), the anima (the feminine side in a man) and animus (the masculine side in a woman), and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala). Others include the wise old man, the great mother and the hero. Example: Meera dreams of an old sage guiding her through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read the sage as the wise old man archetype, the same figure found in stories of rishis and wizards the world over. Option a is right because the archetypes and the collective unconscious are Jung's signature concepts. Exam trap: examiners pair Jung with Freud (b) because both were psychoanalysts who spoke of the unconscious; but Freud's unconscious is personal (repressed wishes), and only Jung added an inherited, collective layer filled with archetypes.", "topic": "Jung's archetypes", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "A disorder of neural development characterised by impaired social interaction and communication, and by restricted and repetitive behaviour, is:", "options": {"a": "Mental retardation", "b": "Down syndrome", "c": "Cat's cry syndrome", "d": "Pervasive developmental disorder"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a disorder of neural development marked by impaired social interaction, impaired communication and restricted, repetitive behaviour is a pervasive developmental disorder (PDD), the older name for what is now autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979) called these three features the triad of impairments. Leo Kanner (1943) first described infantile autism in eleven children with extreme aloneness and an anxious insistence on sameness, and Hans Asperger (1944) described more verbally able children. DSM-IV (1994) and ICD-10 (1992) grouped these conditions as pervasive developmental disorders: autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, Rett's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and PDD not otherwise specified. 'Pervasive' meant the problems touch almost every area of the child's life. DSM-5 (2013) merged most of them into a single autism spectrum disorder with two symptom domains: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, present from early childhood. Example: four-year-old Kabir avoids eye contact, does not point to share interest, lines up his toy cars in the same order every day and screams if the order is changed. This is autism, a pervasive developmental disorder. Option d is right because the stem lists exactly the defining triad of autism, and PDD is the diagnostic family autism belonged to. Exam trap: intellectual disability (a) and the genetic syndromes (b and c) often occur alongside autism and so tempt, but they are defined by low intelligence and physical features, not by the social, communication and repetitive-behaviour triad. Older papers still say PDD where DSM-5 says autism spectrum disorder.", "topic": "Autism and PDD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An individual who is fighting to climb the career ladder is striving to satisfy his:", "options": {"a": "Primary motives", "b": "Personal motives", "c": "Biological motives", "d": "Secondary motives"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: GMCH's own key marks personal motives. Some textbooks sort motives into three groups instead of two. Biological (physiological or primary) motives such as hunger, thirst, sleep and sex arise from bodily needs and keep the body in balance (homeostasis). Social motives such as affiliation, approval and power are learned from living with other people. Personal motives are a person's own learned goals, interests, habits and level of aspiration, the standard of future performance a person sets for himself or herself (a concept from Kurt Lewin's research group). Climbing the career ladder is the person's own aspiration, so in that scheme it is a personal motive. In the two-way scheme the same striving is a secondary (learned, acquired) motive, and David McClelland (The Achieving Society, 1961) would call it the need for achievement (nAch), measured by scoring stories told to Thematic Apperception Test pictures. Example: Riya already earns enough for food and a home, yet she takes a management course and applies for every promotion; no bodily need drives this, her own level of aspiration does. Option b is right because the paper uses the three-way scheme, where career ambition is a personal motive. Exam trap: primary (a) and biological (c) are the same family named twice, so they fail together. Key note: this mock used to mark d, secondary motives, the name in the two-way primary versus secondary scheme, which also describes this person. GMCH's own key for this paper marks b. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH; if a paper offers secondary motives but not personal motives, pick secondary.", "topic": "Classification of motives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, the S-O-R concept was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Woodworth", "b": "C. G. Jung", "c": "E. B. Titchener", "d": "Sigmund Freud"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Robert S. Woodworth (1869 to 1962) of Columbia University rejected the plain stimulus-response (S-R) formula and proposed S-O-R, stimulus-organism-response. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Woodworth argued that the same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism (O): its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, in which inner states stand between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth was a functionalist and is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot samosas (S) makes hungry Kabir reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), his state of hunger. Option a is right because the S-O-R concept is Woodworth's, introduced to correct the S-R connectionist view. Exam trap: Edward B. Titchener (1867 to 1927) (c) tempts because he is another early founder working in America, but he was a structuralist who analysed conscious experience into elements by introspection, not a critic of S-R bonds; and Thorndike is the connectionist the stem rejects, not the reformer.", "topic": "Woodworth's S-O-R model", "fig": "Robert S. Woodworth"}, {"q": "Which term was not part of Pavlov's theory of classical conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Excitation", "b": "Irradiation", "c": "Inhibition", "d": "Reinforcement"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: of the four terms, reinforcement is the best pick for the one not part of Ivan Pavlov's (1927) theory of classical conditioning, because textbooks teach it as the central term of operant conditioning. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, explained conditioning through processes in the cerebral cortex. Excitation is the active state of a cortical centre that produces a response; when a conditioned stimulus (a bell) is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (food), a link of excitation forms, so the bell alone brings salivation. Inhibition is the opposite active process that suppresses a response: external inhibition (a novel stimulus disrupts the response), internal inhibition (as in extinction, when the bell keeps coming without food) and conditioned inhibition. Irradiation is the spread of excitation or inhibition across the cortex from its starting point, Pavlov's explanation of stimulus generalisation; concentration is its later narrowing back to the point of origin, which underlies discrimination. Reinforcement in the sense of a consequence that strengthens the response that produced it comes from Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) operant conditioning. Example: Meera's dog salivates at the pressure cooker whistle because the whistle always comes before dinner; any similar whistle now sets it off too, which Pavlov would explain by irradiation. Option d is right because excitation, inhibition and irradiation are Pavlov's cortical constructs, while reinforcement is the signature term of operant learning. Exam trap: irradiation (b) sounds like an odd, non-psychological word, so students often pick it; it is in fact Pavlov's own term for the spread of excitation. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks b, irradiation. That key is wrong: irradiation is Pavlov's own term, and his Conditioned Reflexes (1927) devotes lectures to the irradiation and concentration of excitation and inhibition in the cortex. Strictly, Pavlov also used 'reinforcement' for pairing the conditioned stimulus with the unconditioned stimulus, and the word entered English through that 1927 translation, so no option is perfectly clean. d stays because reinforcement is the term textbooks file under operant conditioning (Thorndike, Skinner), while irradiation belongs to Pavlov alone.", "topic": "Pavlov's cortical concepts", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "The presence of two mutually opposing forces at the same time, in which the stronger inhibits the weaker, is referred to as the:", "options": {"a": "Principle of posterity", "b": "Reciprocal determinism", "c": "Reactive inhibition", "d": "Principle of reciprocal inhibition"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: when two mutually opposing responses are present at the same time and the stronger one inhibits the weaker, this is the principle of reciprocal inhibition. The idea began in physiology. Charles Sherrington (1906) showed that when one muscle of an opposing pair contracts (the biceps), the nervous system automatically inhibits the other (the triceps), so a limb can move smoothly. Joseph Wolpe (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958) carried the principle into behaviour therapy. He argued that if a response incompatible with anxiety can be made to occur in the presence of anxiety-provoking stimuli, and it is strong enough to suppress the anxiety wholly or partly, the bond between those stimuli and anxiety is weakened. Deep muscle relaxation, assertive responses and sexual responses are all incompatible with anxiety. This is the basis of systematic desensitisation: the deeply relaxed client imagines the steps of a fear hierarchy, from least to most frightening, and at each step relaxation outweighs the small dose of anxiety. Example: Arjun fears dogs. Relaxed in the therapist's chair, he first imagines a picture of a puppy, then a dog across the road, and so on; because relaxation is stronger at every step, the fear weakens. Option d is right because the stem gives the definition of reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible states together, the stronger blocking the weaker. Exam trap: reactive inhibition (c) also contains 'inhibition', but it is Clark Hull's (1943) fatigue-like tendency not to repeat a response just made; reciprocal determinism (b) shares the word 'reciprocal' but is Albert Bandura's (1978) model of person, behaviour and environment influencing one another.", "topic": "Reciprocal inhibition", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "A unique pattern of personal behaviours and characteristics by which each of us strives for perfection is known as:", "options": {"a": "Creative self", "b": "Striving for superiority", "c": "Fictional finalism", "d": "Style of life"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the individual psychology of Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), the unique pattern of behaviours, traits and habits through which each person strives for perfection (superiority) is the style of life. Adler, who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911, held that everyone begins life feeling small and inferior, and that the master motive is striving for superiority, later called striving for perfection or completion: the push from a felt minus towards a plus. Each child works out an individual way of pursuing this goal, formed largely in the first 4 to 5 years from family experiences, birth order and the way inferiority was handled. That consistent pattern is the style of life (lifestyle), and it shows in how the person meets the three tasks of life: work, friendship and love. Adler's related terms are easily confused. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal ('if I become a doctor, everyone will respect me') that guides behaviour, an idea he took from Hans Vaihinger's (1911) philosophy of 'as if'. The creative self is the power each person has to shape their own style of life, rather than being moulded by heredity and environment alone. Example: Kabir, a sickly child teased for being weak, strives to excel at study, chooses friends who admire his intelligence and avoids sport; this whole consistent pattern is his style of life. Option d is right because the stem describes an individual pattern of striving, which is exactly the style of life. Exam trap: striving for superiority (b) echoes the stem's wording ('strives for perfection'), but it is the universal motive itself; the style of life is each person's unique way of pursuing it.", "topic": "Adler's style of life", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Which psychological theory best explains the persistence of phobias in the absence of continuous exposure to the feared object?", "options": {"a": "Classical Conditioning", "b": "Operant Conditioning", "c": "Social Learning Theory", "d": "Cognitive Dissonance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the persistence of a phobia, even when the person is rarely exposed to the feared object, is best explained by operant conditioning, through negative reinforcement of avoidance. O. Hobart Mowrer (1947) set this out in his two-factor theory. Factor one is classical conditioning: a neutral object is paired with a frightening event and comes to trigger fear, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's (1920) Little Albert study. Classical conditioning alone, however, predicts that the fear should extinguish when the object keeps appearing without anything bad happening. Factor two explains why it does not. Each time the person avoids or escapes the object, their fear drops, and this relief negatively reinforces the avoidance (operant conditioning). Because the person always avoids, they never stay long enough to learn that the object is harmless, so extinction never takes place and the phobia can last for years. This is why exposure therapies, which block avoidance, work. Example: Meera was bitten by a dog at 7. Now, at 20, she crosses the road whenever she sees a dog; each crossing brings instant relief, so the habit grows stronger, and she never learns that most dogs are friendly. Option b is right because the stem asks about maintenance, and a phobia is maintained by avoidance being rewarded with fear reduction. Exam trap: classical conditioning (a) is the near miss because it explains how the phobia is first acquired; persistence is the operant half of Mowrer's theory.", "topic": "Two-factor theory of phobia", "fig": "O. Hobart Mowrer"}, {"q": "In a study on conformity, participants often agreed with a clearly incorrect majority. Which of the following best explains this behavior?", "options": {"a": "Informational Social Influence", "b": "Normative Social Influence", "c": "Cognitive Dissonance", "d": "Groupthink"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: when participants agree with a clearly incorrect majority, the best explanation is normative social influence, conforming in order to be liked and accepted and to avoid rejection. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) separated two reasons for conformity. Informational social influence is conforming because we think others know better, so we privately accept their view; it is strongest when the situation is ambiguous. Normative social influence is going along with the group to fit in, often while privately disagreeing (public compliance). Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) showed this with a line judgement task. A real participant sat with a group of confederates, and on critical trials every confederate gave the same obviously wrong answer about which of three lines matched a standard line. Alone, people made almost no errors, yet with the group about 75 per cent conformed at least once, and about a third of all critical answers were wrong. Afterwards many said they knew the answer was wrong but did not want to stand out. When a single ally gave the right answer, conformity fell sharply. Example: in a college quiz team, Riya knows the answer is Delhi, but when all four teammates confidently say Mumbai, she nods along to avoid looking foolish. Option b is right because the answer was clearly wrong, so the group offered no useful information; the pressure came from the wish to be accepted. Exam trap: informational influence (a) is the near miss; it belongs to Muzafer Sherif's (1935) autokinetic study, where a still point of light in a dark room seemed to move, so people had no clear answer and used the group as a source of information.", "topic": "Normative social influence", "fig": "Solomon Asch"}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is primarily associated with the reward system and addictive behaviors?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin", "b": "Dopamine", "c": "GABA", "d": "Glutamate"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: dopamine is the neurotransmitter most closely tied to the brain's reward system and to addictive behaviour. The core reward circuit is the mesolimbic dopamine pathway: dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain send their axons to the nucleus accumbens in the ventral striatum, with links to the prefrontal cortex. James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) found that rats would press a lever again and again to receive electrical stimulation of this system, the first evidence for 'pleasure centres'. Natural rewards such as food, sex and social contact release dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, and almost all addictive drugs raise it, directly or indirectly: cocaine blocks dopamine reuptake, amphetamine releases it, and nicotine, alcohol and opioids increase the firing of dopamine neurons. Wolfram Schultz (1997) showed that dopamine neurons signal reward prediction error, firing more when a reward is better than expected, which teaches the brain what to seek. Repeated drug use changes this system so that drug cues trigger intense craving ('wanting'), the incentive-sensitisation theory of Terry Robinson and Kent Berridge (1993). Example: Arjun's phone pings with a new like on his post; the unpredictable reward triggers a dopamine burst, and he checks the phone again and again. Option b is right because dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway is the common final path of natural reward and drug addiction. Exam trap: serotonin (a) is the usual near miss because it is famous for mood and depression; it modulates mood and impulse control, but reward and craving belong to dopamine.", "topic": "Dopamine and reward", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher finds that stress is negatively correlated with academic performance. What does this mean?", "options": {"a": "As stress increases, academic performance decreases", "b": "Stress causes poor academic performance", "c": "As stress increases, academic performance increases", "d": "No relationship exists between stress and academic performance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a negative correlation between stress and academic performance means that as stress increases, academic performance tends to decrease (and as stress falls, performance tends to rise). A correlation describes how two variables vary together. It is usually measured with Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment coefficient, r, which runs from minus 1 to plus 1. The sign shows the direction. Positive means the two variables rise and fall together (height and weight); negative means they move in opposite directions (hours of television and exam marks). The size, ignoring the sign, shows the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and values near 1 or minus 1 mean the points lie close to a straight line. So r = minus 0.70 is a stronger relationship than r = plus 0.30. Most important, correlation does not imply causation: stress might lower marks, poor marks might raise stress, or a third variable such as poor sleep or money worries might drive both. Only an experiment can show cause. Example: a survey of 200 college students finds r = minus 0.45 between stress scores and marks. Riya, with high stress, is likely (not certain) to have lower marks than Kabir, with low stress. Option a is right because 'negative' describes the direction of the relationship: the two variables move in opposite directions. Exam trap: option b turns a correlation into a cause, the classic error examiners test; option c describes a positive correlation, and option d confuses 'negative' with 'zero'.", "topic": "Negative correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of a secondary reinforcer in operant conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Food", "b": "Water", "c": "Money", "d": "Sleep"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: money is a secondary reinforcer. In operant conditioning, a reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely to happen again (B. F. Skinner, 1938). Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy biological needs and work without any learning: food, water, warmth, sleep and relief from pain. Secondary (conditioned) reinforcers are stimuli that were once neutral and became rewarding because they were paired with primary reinforcers. Praise, grades, tokens, medals and the clicker sound in dog training are all examples. Money is the classic case: a paper note means nothing to a baby, but it gains its power because it can be exchanged for food, shelter and many other rewards. A secondary reinforcer linked with many primary ones, as money is, is called a generalised reinforcer, and it keeps working whatever need the person has at the moment. The token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) uses tokens as secondary reinforcers that patients exchange for privileges. Example: Meera's parents give her a sticker each day she finishes her homework, and ten stickers buy a trip for ice cream. The stickers start out meaningless, but because they lead to the treat they come to reinforce homework on their own. Option c is right because money is valuable only through learning, by its link with primary rewards. Exam trap: food, water and sleep (a, b, d) are all primary reinforcers, so the examiner is testing which reward had to be learned; sleep may seem an odd reinforcer, but it meets a biological need.", "topic": "Primary and secondary reinforcers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "People attributing their own unwanted impulses and feelings to someone else is called:", "options": {"a": "Rejection", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Projection", "d": "Denial"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: attributing one's own unwanted impulses and feelings to someone else is projection, an ego defence mechanism. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) described defence mechanisms as unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety caused by unacceptable id impulses or harsh superego demands, and his daughter Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) set them out systematically. In projection, a thought or feeling that would cause guilt if owned, such as hostility, jealousy or sexual attraction, is pushed out and seen in another person instead. 'I hate him' becomes 'He hates me'. Because the impulse now seems to sit outside, the person can criticise it in others without facing it in themselves. Projection is common in paranoid thinking, and George Vaillant (1977) placed it among the immature defences. Projective tests such as the Rorschach and the Thematic Apperception Test rest on a related idea: people read their own needs into ambiguous material. Example: Arjun feels attracted to a colleague but believes this is wrong; instead he keeps telling friends that the colleague is always flirting with him. Option c is right because the stem defines projection exactly: one's own unacceptable feeling is attributed to another person. Exam trap: displacement (b) also moves a feeling, but it redirects a feeling the person still owns onto a safer target (shouting at a younger brother after being scolded by the boss), whereas projection changes who seems to have the feeling.", "topic": "Projection as a defence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which research method is best suited to determine cause-and-effect relationships?", "options": {"a": "Correlational Study", "b": "Experimental Study", "c": "Longitudinal Study", "d": "Case Study"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the experimental study is the research method best suited to determining cause and effect. A true experiment has three defining features. First, the researcher manipulates the independent variable (IV), deliberately creating different conditions rather than just observing what happens naturally. Second, participants are randomly assigned to conditions, which spreads other differences (age, ability, mood) evenly, so the groups are equal at the start. Third, the researcher controls extraneous variables, keeping everything except the IV the same. If the groups then differ on the measured dependent variable (DV), the difference can be put down to the IV; this is high internal validity. The other methods lack manipulation and random assignment. They can show that two things go together, describe change or explore a rare case, but they cannot rule out third variables or settle the direction of cause, hence the rule that correlation does not imply causation. Example: to test whether sleep affects memory, a researcher randomly assigns 60 students to sleep 8 hours or 4 hours, then gives everyone the same word list. If the 8-hour group, which includes students like Kabir, recalls more, sleep can be said to cause better recall, because random assignment balanced everything else. Option b is right because only an experiment combines manipulation, random assignment and control, the conditions needed to infer cause. Exam trap: the longitudinal study (c) sounds powerful because it follows the same people over years and shows which came first, but without manipulation a third variable could still explain the link; time order alone does not prove cause.", "topic": "Experiments and causation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which personality trait is most associated with openness to new experiences and intellectual curiosity?", "options": {"a": "Extraversion", "b": "Agreeableness", "c": "Openness to Experience", "d": "Conscientiousness"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: openness to experience is the Big Five trait most associated with curiosity, imagination and a willingness to try new things. The Big Five (five-factor model) grew out of the lexical approach: if a trait matters, languages will have words for it. Factor analyses of trait words by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961), Warren Norman (1963) and Lewis Goldberg (1981) kept finding five broad dimensions, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae built the NEO Personality Inventory (1985; NEO PI-R, 1992) to measure them. The acronym OCEAN lists them: Openness (imaginative, curious, artistic, open to ideas and values, versus conventional and practical), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable, self-disciplined), Extraversion (sociable, energetic, assertive), Agreeableness (warm, trusting, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious, moody, easily upset, versus emotionally stable). Costa and McCrae's six facets of openness are fantasy, aesthetics, feelings, actions, ideas and values. Openness is the Big Five trait most linked with creativity, and it correlates modestly with measured intelligence. Example: Riya reads about astronomy for fun, is learning Japanese, tries new cuisines and enjoys debating unusual ideas; she would score high on openness. Option c is right because intellectual curiosity and openness to new experiences are the defining features of this factor. Exam trap: extraversion (a) is the usual near miss because extraverts seek excitement and new social experiences, but extraversion is about sociability and positive energy; enjoying new ideas, art and intellectual exploration belongs to openness.", "topic": "Big Five: openness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which type of reinforcement schedule is most resistant to extinction?", "options": {"a": "Fixed Interval", "b": "Fixed Ratio", "c": "Variable Interval", "d": "Variable Ratio"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the variable ratio schedule is the most resistant to extinction. Extinction is the weakening of a learned response once reinforcement stops, and resistance to extinction is how long the response keeps going. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (Schedules of Reinforcement, 1957) mapped the effects of different schedules. Continuous reinforcement rewards every response and extinguishes fastest. Partial (intermittent) schedules reward only some responses and make behaviour far more durable, the partial reinforcement extinction effect (Lloyd Humphreys, 1939). The four basic partial schedules come from two choices: ratio (reward depends on the number of responses) or interval (reward depends on time passed), and fixed or variable. Fixed ratio (every 10th response) gives a high rate with a pause after each reward. Fixed interval (the first response after 5 minutes) gives a scallop: little responding just after a reward, faster responding as the time nears. Variable interval (after unpredictable times) gives a moderate, steady rate. Variable ratio (after an unpredictable number of responses around an average) gives the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance to extinction, because the next response might always be the winning one, so long runs without reward feel normal. Example: Kabir plays a mobile game that pays coins after an unpredictable number of spins; even after the coins stop entirely, he keeps spinning for days. Slot machines and lotteries work the same way. Option d is right because an unpredictable reward tied to the person's own responding makes the end of reinforcement hardest to notice. Exam trap: variable interval (c) is the near miss because it is also unpredictable and also resistant, but its rate is only moderate and it comes second; the fixed schedules (a, b) extinguish faster because the switch to no reward is easy to detect.", "topic": "Schedules and extinction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When someone attributes their successes to internal factors and their failures to external factors, they are exhibiting:", "options": {"a": "Self-serving bias", "b": "Fundamental attribution error", "c": "Actor-observer bias", "d": "Just-world hypothesis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: taking credit for successes (internal causes) and blaming failures on outside factors (external causes) is the self-serving bias. Attribution theory, begun by Fritz Heider (1958), studies how people explain the causes of behaviour, either internally (ability, effort, personality) or externally (luck, task difficulty, other people). The self-serving bias, reviewed by Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975), is a motivated error: it protects and enhances self-esteem. After a good result we say 'I worked hard' or 'I am talented'; after a bad one, 'the paper was unfair' or 'the umpire was biased'. It shows up in students, athletes, managers and couples. It tends to be weaker in collectivist cultures that value modesty, and people with depression often show the reverse pattern, blaming themselves for failure. Example: when Arjun scores 92 in mathematics he says he is good at the subject, but when he scores 48 in physics he blames the teacher's confusing questions. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly this split: internal causes for success, external causes for failure. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (b) and the actor-observer bias (c) are also attribution biases, but they concern explaining other people's behaviour by their dispositions, or one's own behaviour by the situation and others' by their traits, whatever the outcome; only the self-serving bias depends on whether the outcome was a success or a failure.", "topic": "Self-serving bias", "fig": "Dale Miller and Michael Ross"}, {"q": "A parent who reinforces a child's behaviour every time it occurs is using which type of reinforcement schedule?", "options": {"a": "Continuous", "b": "Fixed Ratio", "c": "Variable Interval", "d": "Intermittent"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: reinforcing a behaviour every time it occurs is continuous reinforcement (CRF), sometimes written as a fixed ratio 1 schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses are rewarded. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (Schedules of Reinforcement, 1957) divided schedules into continuous, where every correct response is reinforced, and intermittent (partial), where only some are. Intermittent schedules come in four basic forms: fixed ratio, variable ratio, fixed interval and variable interval. Continuous reinforcement is the fastest way to establish a new behaviour, because the link between the act and the reward is clear and immediate, so it is ideal for shaping and early learning. Its weakness is that behaviour learned this way extinguishes quickly once rewards stop, because the change is easy to notice. Intermittent reinforcement builds behaviour more slowly, but the behaviour lasts much longer (the partial reinforcement extinction effect). The practical rule is to start with continuous reinforcement, then thin it to an intermittent schedule. Example: each time three-year-old Meera puts her toys back in the box, her mother praises her and gives her a hug. Meera learns the habit quickly; later her mother praises her only now and then, which keeps the habit going for longer. Option a is right because 'every time it occurs' is the definition of continuous reinforcement. Exam trap: fixed ratio (b) tempts because CRF can technically be written as FR 1, but in exam usage fixed ratio means a reward after a set number of responses greater than one; intermittent (d) is the opposite of the stem, the umbrella term for reinforcing only some responses.", "topic": "Continuous reinforcement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which type of memory is primarily affected in individuals with Alzheimer's disease?", "options": {"a": "Procedural Memory", "b": "Semantic Memory", "c": "Episodic Memory", "d": "Working Memory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: episodic memory, memory for personal events tied to a time and place, is the system primarily affected in Alzheimer's disease, especially memory for recent events. Endel Tulving (1972) divided declarative (explicit) long-term memory into episodic memory (what happened, where and when: yesterday's lunch, last week's wedding) and semantic memory (general facts and word meanings: Delhi is the capital of India). Non-declarative (implicit) memory includes procedural memory, the skills and habits shown by doing, such as cycling. Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), is a degenerative dementia marked by amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles. The damage usually begins in the medial temporal lobe, in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus, the structures that form new episodic memories. So the earliest signs are forgetting recent events and conversations, repeating questions and misplacing things, while memories from long ago are relatively preserved at first. As the disease spreads into the temporal and parietal neocortex, semantic memory (word finding, naming, general knowledge) declines too, followed by language, visuospatial skills and judgement. Procedural memory, which depends more on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, is spared until late. Example: Kabir's grandmother cannot remember that her daughter visited this morning and asks the same question five times, yet she still knits and sings old bhajans perfectly. Option c is right because hippocampal damage first and most severely impairs the recording of new personal episodes. Exam trap: semantic memory (b) also declines in Alzheimer's disease and is the near miss, but later and less sharply; procedural memory (a) is spared until late, and its early loss points instead to Parkinson's or Huntington's disease.", "topic": "Memory loss in Alzheimer's", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An individual who constantly worries about health despite multiple reassurances from doctors may be exhibiting:", "options": {"a": "Panic disorder", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder", "c": "Illness anxiety disorder", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: a person who constantly worries about having or getting a serious illness, despite repeated medical reassurance, may have illness anxiety disorder. DSM-5 (2013) replaced the older diagnosis of hypochondriasis with two disorders. Somatic symptom disorder is for people with distressing bodily symptoms plus excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviour about them. Illness anxiety disorder is for people whose main problem is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, with no bodily symptoms or only mild ones. Its DSM-5 features are: preoccupation with having a serious illness; somatic symptoms absent or mild; high anxiety about health and easy alarm about personal health status; excessive health-related behaviour (checking the body, searching symptoms online) or maladaptive avoidance (avoiding doctors and hospitals); and a duration of at least 6 months. It has a care-seeking type and a care-avoidant type. Normal test results and doctors' reassurance bring only brief relief, and the fear soon returns or shifts to another illness. ICD-11 keeps the name hypochondriasis and places it with the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Example: Riya has had three normal ECGs and two normal blood reports, yet every small chest twinge convinces her she has heart disease, and she searches her symptoms online every night. Option c is right because health worry that persists despite reassurance is the defining picture of illness anxiety disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder (b) also involves constant worry, but GAD worry spreads across many everyday areas (work, family, money), while illness anxiety fixes on one theme, having a serious disease; panic disorder (a) can include a fear of dying, but that fear is tied to the attacks themselves.", "topic": "Illness anxiety disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The bystander effect can best be explained by:", "options": {"a": "Diffusion of responsibility", "b": "Increased empathy", "c": "Social loafing", "d": "Cognitive dissonance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the bystander effect is best explained by diffusion of responsibility, the sense that when many people are present, each one is less personally responsible for helping. The bystander effect is the finding that the more people witness an emergency, the less likely any one of them is to help, and the slower help comes. It was prompted by the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, where newspapers reported that 38 neighbours watched and none called the police (a figure later shown to be exaggerated). John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968) staged an emergency: students talking over an intercom heard another participant apparently having a seizure. When students believed they were the only listener, about 85 per cent went to help before the seizure ended; when they believed four others could also hear, only about 31 per cent did. Each person assumed someone else would act. Their later decision model (The Unresponsive Bystander, 1970) lists five steps: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, assume responsibility, know how to help, and decide to help. Diffusion of responsibility blocks the third step; pluralistic ignorance, where everyone looks calm so no one reads the event as an emergency, blocks the second. Example: on a crowded Mumbai platform a man collapses; Meera sees dozens of people nearby and assumes one of them, perhaps a doctor, will help, so she walks on. Option a is right because spreading responsibility across many witnesses is the core explanation Darley and Latané gave. Exam trap: social loafing (c) also involves responsibility spread across a group, but it is reduced effort on a shared task, not failure to help in an emergency.", "topic": "Bystander effect", "fig": "John Darley and Bibb Latané"}, {"q": "A psychologist who studies how people learn, remember and think would be classified as a:", "options": {"a": "Clinical psychologist", "b": "Social psychologist", "c": "Cognitive psychologist", "d": "Behavioural psychologist"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: a psychologist who studies how people learn, remember and think is a cognitive psychologist. Cognitive psychology is the study of internal mental processes: attention, perception, memory, language, thinking, problem solving, decision making and learning. It treats the mind as an information processor that takes in, stores, transforms and retrieves information, rather like a computer. The field grew out of the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, when work such as George Miller's (1956) 'magical number seven' paper on short-term memory and Noam Chomsky's (1959) critique of B. F. Skinner's account of language showed that behaviourism could not explain mental life. Ulric Neisser named and defined the field in his book Cognitive Psychology (1967) and is often called its father. Cognitive psychologists mostly use laboratory experiments, reaction times and memory tests, and today brain imaging as well (cognitive neuroscience). Example: Kabir runs an experiment in which students learn word lists with or without a night's sleep and are tested the next day; because he is studying how memories are stored, he is working as a cognitive psychologist. Option c is right because learning, remembering and thinking are the core mental processes that define cognitive psychology. Exam trap: behavioural psychologist (d) is the near miss because behaviourists also study learning, but they study observable responses and their conditioning and deliberately set aside inner processes such as thinking and remembering; clinical psychologists (a) assess and treat mental disorders, and social psychologists (b) study how people influence and relate to one another.", "topic": "Fields of psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which of the following needs must be satisfied first?", "options": {"a": "Esteem needs", "b": "Love and belongingness needs", "c": "Safety needs", "d": "Self-actualization needs"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Abraham Maslow's (1943) hierarchy of needs, safety needs are the lowest of the four levels offered, so they must be satisfied first. Maslow, a founder of humanistic psychology, arranged human needs in a pyramid of five levels, from bottom to top: physiological needs (food, water, sleep, air), safety needs (security, stability, protection, freedom from fear), love and belongingness needs (friendship, family, intimacy, acceptance), esteem needs (achievement, competence, respect from others and self-respect) and self-actualisation (realising one's full potential). The lower needs are prepotent: when they are unmet they dominate behaviour, and a higher need becomes the main motivator only once the lower ones are reasonably satisfied. The bottom four are deficiency needs (D-needs), felt when something is missing; self-actualisation is a growth or being need (B-need). In Motivation and Personality (1954) Maslow noted that the order is not rigid, and some people put ideals or creativity ahead of comfort. Example: Arjun has just moved to a new city for work. Once he has food and a roof, his first worry is a safe neighbourhood and a stable job; only after that does he look for friends, then for promotion, then for a creative hobby. Option c is right because physiological needs, the true first level, are not among the options, and safety needs come next. Exam trap: students who remember 'physiological first' may hunt for it and then guess; when it is missing, the lowest remaining level, safety, is the answer, while self-actualisation (d) sits at the top of the pyramid and is the last to be pursued.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core characteristic of borderline personality disorder?", "options": {"a": "Persistent mood swings", "b": "Disorganized speech", "c": "Chronic feelings of emptiness", "d": "Repetitive, ritualistic behaviours"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: chronic feelings of emptiness are a core characteristic of borderline personality disorder (BPD); the phrase appears word for word as criterion 7 in DSM-5 (2013). BPD is a Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, erratic) personality disorder: a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood. At least 5 of 9 criteria are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealising and devaluing; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substances, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour, threats or self-harm; affective instability from marked reactivity of mood; chronic feelings of emptiness; inappropriate, intense anger; and transient, stress-related paranoid ideas or severe dissociation. Marsha Linehan (1993) explained BPD through her biosocial theory and developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) to treat it. Example: Meera, 24, says she feels hollow inside; she cuts her arm after a friend cancels plans and swings from adoring to hating her boyfriend within a day. Option c is right because chronic emptiness is a BPD criterion exactly as written. Exam trap: each other option points to a different disorder: disorganised speech (b) is a psychotic symptom of schizophrenia, and repetitive, ritualistic behaviour (d) suggests OCD or autism. Key note: affective instability (criterion 6) is also a BPD feature, which is why persistent mood swings (a) tempt; but BPD mood shifts usually last a few hours and only rarely more than a few days, while 'persistent mood swings' points more to cyclothymia or bipolar disorder. Chronic emptiness is the criterion as written, and c stands.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gmch-2024": [{"q": "In the Simon-Binet test of intelligence, a person with a chronological age (CA) of 40 years has a mental age (MA) of 20 years. The person is intellectually:", "concept": "The answer is c: GMCH's own key marks above average, because an adult's chronological age is capped in the ratio IQ. The 'Simon-Binet' test in the stem is the scale that Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built in Paris to find children who needed special teaching. Their 1908 revision graded tasks by age, so a person's mental age (MA) is the age level of the tasks he or she can pass. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age (CA) to get a mental quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet: ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. That works for children, whose mental age grows with real age. It breaks for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so Terman capped the adult chronological age at 16 (the 1937 revision used 15). A 40-year-old is therefore scored as if CA were 16: 20 / 16 x 100 = 125, above average. The 1916 Stanford-Binet's own 'average adult' level was a mental age of 16, so a mental age of 20 sits above it. Example: Arjun, 40, passes the tasks of the 20-year level; with the adult cap his ratio IQ is 125, not 50. Option c is right because for an adult the divisor is the capped age, and a mental age above the adult norm means above-average ability. Exam trap: dividing by the full 40 gives 20 / 40 x 100 = 50, below average (option a); it looks like the obvious sum but ignores the adult cap, the very problem that led David Wechsler (1939) to the deviation IQ. Key note: this mock used to mark a, the plain 20 / 40 sum. GMCH's own key for this paper marks c. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH; if a question gives a child's ages, do the plain MA / CA sum.", "traps": {"a": "Below average comes from dividing by the full 40 (20 / 40 x 100 = 50). For an adult the ratio IQ divides by the capped age of 16, which gives 125, so GMCH's key rejects it.", "b": "Average would need a mental age close to the adult norm of about 16, giving an IQ near 100; a mental age of 20 is above that norm.", "d": "Genius was Lewis Terman's label for IQs of about 140 and above; 125 is above average but well short of it, and you reach 200 only by wrongly inverting the ratio (40 / 20 x 100)."}}, {"q": "MMPI is a test of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the MMPI is a test of personality, and in particular of psychopathology. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by the psychologist Starke Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. It is an objective, self-report inventory: the person marks hundreds of statements as true or false, and the answers are scored against fixed norms rather than interpreted freely. Its items were chosen by empirical criterion keying. An item stayed on a scale only if patients with a given diagnosis (for example, depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result is 10 clinical scales, such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia and Schizophrenia, plus validity scales (L for lying, F for rare or infrequent answers, K for defensiveness) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items and is still among the most used personality tests in clinics. For example, when Kabir is referred for low mood and unexplained bodily complaints, his MMPI-2 profile can show raised Depression and Hypochondriasis scales, and the validity scales tell the clinician whether he was faking good or faking bad. Option c is right because the MMPI describes personality traits and clinical patterns, not ability, opinions or vocational likes. Exam trap: the word 'multiphasic' makes some students think of a multi-ability battery; it only means many scales of personality. Also, the MMPI is an objective test, so do not group it with projective tests such as the Rorschach or the TAT.", "traps": {"a": "Intelligence is measured by ability tests such as David Wechsler's WAIS or the Stanford-Binet, which have right and wrong answers; the MMPI has no right answers.", "b": "Attitudes are measured by scales such as Louis Thurstone's (1928) equal-appearing intervals or Rensis Likert's (1932) agree-disagree scale, which ask for opinions on a topic, not personality traits.", "d": "Interest is measured by inventories such as Edward Strong's Strong Vocational Interest Blank (1927) or the Kuder Preference Record, which compare a person's likes with those of people in different jobs."}}, {"q": "The empty chair technique is one of the techniques of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the empty chair technique belongs to Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; the founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman. It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy. It holds that people carry unfinished business (feelings never expressed, such as grief or anger) and disown parts of themselves, and that healing comes from full awareness in the present moment. In the empty chair, the client imagines a person, or a part of the self, sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly. The client may then switch chairs and answer as that person. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'top dog' (the demanding inner critic) and the 'underdog' (the excuse-making side). For example, Meera's father died before she could tell him how hurt she felt. In the empty chair she speaks to him, says what she never said, and feels the grief finally move. Option c is right because Perls made the technique famous as a way of turning talk about feelings into direct, present experience. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology, founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka, studied perception and the idea that the whole is more than the sum of its parts; the therapy borrowed the word, not the research. Also, Jacob Moreno's psychodrama (1921) used similar role-play, but in exams the empty chair is Gestalt therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic psychotherapy, from Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), works through free association, dream analysis and the interpretation of transference and resistance; the analyst interprets rather than staging a dialogue with an empty chair.", "b": "Introspection is a research method, not a therapy: trained observers in Wilhelm Wundt's Leipzig laboratory (1879) and in Edward Titchener's structuralism reported their own conscious sensations.", "d": "Behaviour therapy, from Joseph Wolpe (1958) and the learning theorists, changes behaviour through systematic desensitisation, exposure and reinforcement, not through emotional dialogue with an imagined person."}}, {"q": "A behavioural psychologist is interested in the behaviour that:", "concept": "The answer is a: a behavioural psychologist studies behaviour that can be observed. Behaviourism began with John B. Watson (1913), whose paper 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' argued that psychology should be an objective natural science whose goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. Thoughts, images and feelings cannot be seen or measured by an outside observer, so Watson ruled them out and studied only what an organism does: responses that others can see, count and record, linked to the stimuli that produce them. B. F. Skinner (1938) carried this further in radical behaviourism, explaining behaviour by its consequences in the environment rather than by inner mental causes. For example, a behavioural psychologist working with Arjun, a child who disrupts class, would not ask what he is secretly thinking. She would count how often he leaves his seat in an hour, note what happens just before and just after (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) and then change the consequences. Option a is right because observability is the defining rule of behaviourism: if it cannot be observed and measured, it is not data. Exam trap: neobehaviourists such as Edward Tolman (1932) and Clark Hull (1943) did infer inner 'intervening variables' such as cognitive maps and drive, which makes option c tempting, but the classic textbook definition of the behavioural approach is the study of observable behaviour. Skinner also counted thoughts as private behaviour, yet he still studied people only through observable responses.", "traps": {"b": "Behaviour 'the organism chooses to perform' implies free will and choice, a humanistic or existential concern (Carl Rogers, 1951; Abraham Maslow, 1954); behaviourists explain behaviour by learning history, not choice.", "c": "Behaviour that 'can be inferred' points to inner processes such as memory and thinking, which cognitive psychology (Ulric Neisser, 1967) infers from performance; the behaviourist sticks to what is directly observable.", "d": "Conscious experience was the subject matter of Wilhelm Wundt's (1879) laboratory psychology, Edward Titchener's structuralism and William James's (1890) functionalism; Watson rejected consciousness as unscientific."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies helps patients to accept their illness experiences without avoidance?", "concept": "The answer is c: acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) teaches patients to accept painful thoughts, feelings and illness experiences without avoiding them. ACT was developed by Steven Hayes from the 1980s, with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (book 1999), and belongs to the 'third wave' of cognitive-behavioural therapy. Its core idea is that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance: the effort to escape or control unwanted inner experiences. Avoidance brings brief relief but narrows life and often makes the distress stronger. Instead of fighting symptoms, ACT builds psychological flexibility through six processes: acceptance (making room for feelings), cognitive defusion (seeing thoughts as just words, as in 'I am having the thought that I am useless'), contact with the present moment, self as context (the observing self), values (what matters to the person) and committed action (doing what matters while discomfort is present). For example, Kabir has chronic back pain and has stopped meeting friends to avoid flare-ups. ACT does not promise to remove the pain. It helps him notice the pain, stop struggling with it, and still go to his cousin's wedding because family matters to him. Option c is right because acceptance without avoidance is the named target of the therapy and the first word of its name. Exam trap: mindfulness meditation (option d) also teaches non-judgemental acceptance, and ACT uses mindfulness exercises, but mindfulness is a practice, not the therapy built around accepting illness experiences and acting on values.", "traps": {"a": "Relaxation, such as Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation (1929), lowers bodily arousal to reduce anxiety; it is a coping skill aimed at reducing symptoms, not at accepting them.", "b": "Stress inoculation training, by Donald Meichenbaum (1985), prepares people to cope with stress through conceptualisation, skills acquisition and rehearsal, and application; it teaches coping, not acceptance without avoidance.", "d": "Mindfulness meditation, brought into medicine by Jon Kabat-Zinn's mindfulness-based stress reduction (1979), trains present-moment, non-judgemental attention; it is one component of ACT, not the therapy defined by acceptance and commitment."}}, {"q": "Anhedonia is another word for:", "concept": "The answer is d: anhedonia is the inability to experience pleasure, or a marked loss of interest and enjoyment in activities that used to be pleasurable. The French psychologist Théodule Ribot (1896) coined the word from Greek roots meaning 'without pleasure'. It is a core symptom of a major depressive episode: DSM-5 requires either depressed mood or markedly diminished interest or pleasure for the diagnosis. It is also one of the negative symptoms of schizophrenia, alongside flat affect, alogia, avolition and asociality. Researchers now separate anticipatory anhedonia (not looking forward to things) from consummatory anhedonia (not enjoying them in the moment). For example, Riya used to love painting and meeting friends; now she does both without feeling anything, and soon she stops. Option d is right because it is the literal meaning of the term. Exam trap: anhedonia is often confused with avolition (lack of drive to start activities), apathy (lack of feeling or concern) and alexithymia (difficulty naming one's own feelings). Low energy and weight loss are separate depressive criteria, which is why they sit in the options as near misses.", "traps": {"a": "Weight loss (or gain) is a separate DSM-5 criterion for a major depressive episode, reflecting a change in appetite, not a loss of pleasure.", "b": "Low energy (fatigue, sometimes called anergia) is another separate depressive criterion; a person can be tired yet still enjoy things.", "c": "Low frustration tolerance is a term from Albert Ellis's (1955) rational emotive behaviour therapy for the belief that one cannot stand discomfort; it relates to irritability, not to pleasure."}}, {"q": "Punishment is effective if it is applied:", "concept": "The answer is a: punishment works best when it follows the behaviour immediately. Punishment is any consequence that lowers the future rate of the behaviour it follows. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding); negative punishment removes something pleasant (losing phone time). Edward Thorndike (1898) first showed with his puzzle-box cats that consequences shape behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1938) studied them in the operant chamber. Reviewing the animal research, Nathan Azrin and William Holz (1966) listed the conditions under which punishment suppresses behaviour well: it should be immediate, delivered every time the behaviour occurs, strong enough from the start rather than raised gradually, and combined with reinforcement of an acceptable alternative. Timing matters because learning depends on contiguity. The longer the gap, the more other behaviours happen in between, so the consequence becomes linked to whatever the person was doing when it arrived. For example, if Kabir's mother sees him hit his sister and scolds him at once, the link is clear. If she waits until his father comes home in the evening, Kabir may learn only that his father's arrival means trouble. Option a is right because immediate delivery is the best established rule for effective punishment. Exam trap: do not confuse punishment with negative reinforcement, which also involves something unpleasant but increases behaviour by removing it. The immediacy rule applies to reinforcement as well.", "traps": {"b": "Even a slight delay weakens punishment, because other responses slip in between the behaviour and the consequence; in animal studies suppression falls as the delay grows.", "c": "A marked delay is the least effective option; the punishment then attaches to the wrong behaviour or feels unconnected and unfair, as in 'wait till your father gets home'.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because immediacy is one of the clearest conditions for effective punishment, along with consistency and enough intensity."}}, {"q": "The biomedical model assumes that:", "concept": "The answer is a: the biomedical model assumes that all illness can be explained by aberrant somatic (bodily) processes. This model has dominated Western medicine since the nineteenth century. It treats disease as a deviation from normal biological measures, caused by things such as germs, genes, injuries or chemical imbalance. It is reductionist, because it reduces illness to processes in cells and organs, and dualist, because, following René Descartes (1641), it separates mind from body and treats the body as a machine to be repaired. In psychiatry it appears as the medical model: mental disorders are diseases of the brain, to be diagnosed and treated with drugs. The model has great strengths, such as vaccines and antibiotics, but it struggles to explain why two people with the same lesion fare so differently. George Engel (1977) argued in the journal Science for the biopsychosocial model, in which biological, psychological and social factors interact in every illness. For example, Meera has frequent headaches. A purely biomedical doctor orders scans and prescribes painkillers; a biopsychosocial clinician also asks about her exam stress, her sleep and conflict at home. Option a is right because the biomedical model places the cause of all illness in the body alone. Exam trap: the biopsychosocial model is Engel's challenge to the biomedical model, not a version of it, and the 'medical model' in psychiatry is the same disease-of-the-body approach under another name.", "traps": {"b": "Explaining illness by social factors such as poverty, stigma and social stress is the sociocultural (social) model; the biomedical model ignores these.", "c": "Explaining illness by psychological factors such as emotional conflict is the psychological or psychosomatic approach of Franz Alexander (1950), the opposite emphasis to the biomedical model.", "d": "Explaining illness by evolutionary factors is evolutionary (Darwinian) medicine, set out by Randolph Nesse and George Williams (1994); it asks why the body is vulnerable, not which bodily process has gone wrong."}}, {"q": "According to the two-factor theory of emotion, emotion equals:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) two-factor theory, emotion equals physiological arousal plus a cognitive label. The theory says arousal itself is general and much the same across emotions: a racing heart, sweating and trembling. What turns it into a particular emotion is how we interpret it, using cues from the situation. Both factors are needed. Arousal without a label is just a stirred-up feeling, and a label without arousal is cold thought. In their classic study, participants were injected with epinephrine (adrenaline) and told it was a vitamin. Some were correctly told it would make their hearts race; others were misinformed or told nothing. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. Those with no explanation for their arousal tended to take on the stooge's mood, while those who knew the drug's effects did so less, because they already had a label for what they felt. For example, Arjun's heart pounds before a cricket final. If he reads it as excitement he feels thrilled; if he reads it as danger he feels afraid. Option a is right because it names the two factors exactly. Exam trap: the theory is also called the cognitive arousal theory. Contrast it with the James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885), in which bodily change is the emotion, and the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927), in which arousal and emotion occur together. Other 'two-factor theories', such as Charles Spearman's (1904) on intelligence and O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) on avoidance, are different topics.", "traps": {"b": "Intelligence plays no part in the theory; emotional intelligence, from Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990), is a separate idea about understanding and managing emotions.", "c": "Attribution plus explanation is two words for the same cognitive step (explaining a cause, as in Fritz Heider's 1958 attribution theory); it leaves out the physiological arousal factor.", "d": "Attribution plus cognition again offers only thinking; the theory requires bodily arousal as one of its two factors."}}, {"q": "'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance.' Which type of hypothesis is this?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' is an alternate and directional hypothesis. A hypothesis is a testable prediction about the relation between variables, and researchers classify it in two ways. The first is null versus alternate. The null hypothesis (H0), named by Ronald Fisher (1935), states that there is no effect or no relation: 'reinforcement has no effect on performance'. The alternate (alternative) hypothesis (H1), formalised by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), states that an effect or relation exists. The second is directional versus non-directional. A directional hypothesis says which way the effect goes (higher, lower, improves, reduces) and is tested with a one-tailed test. A non-directional hypothesis predicts only that there will be a difference ('reinforcement will affect performance') and is tested with a two-tailed test. For example, Meera predicts that students praised after each correct sum will solve more sums than students given no praise. She expects an effect (alternate) in a stated direction (more sums), so she would use a one-tailed test. Option b is right because the statement claims that an effect exists and specifies that the effect is positive. Exam trap: students sometimes label any confident research prediction 'null'; the null is always the 'no difference' statement, and it is the one actually tested statistically. Also, 'positive effect' signals direction just as clearly as 'greater than' does.", "traps": {"a": "Null and directional is wrong on the first part: the statement predicts an effect, so it is not the null hypothesis of no effect.", "c": "Null and non-directional is wrong on both counts: the statement predicts an effect and also names its direction.", "d": "Alternate and non-directional is half right; the statement is alternate, but 'positive effect' states a direction, so it is not non-directional."}}, {"q": "The 'fruitful metaphor' concept was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the 'fruitful metaphor' concept was given by George Kelly (1955). In The Psychology of Personal Constructs, Kelly proposed that we can usefully look at every person as if he or she were a scientist. He called this picture of 'man the scientist' a fruitful metaphor: not a literal claim, but a way of seeing people that produces useful ideas. Like a scientist, each person builds theories about the world, uses them to predict events, tests the predictions in daily life and revises the theories when they fail. Kelly called these theories personal constructs: bipolar ways of construing events, such as friendly versus hostile or clever versus dull. His fundamental postulate states that a person's processes are psychologically channelled by the ways in which he or she anticipates events. Because the same events can always be construed in more than one way, he called his philosophy constructive alternativism. He measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep test) and treated clients with fixed-role therapy, in which the client acts out a new character for a few weeks. For example, Meera construes people as either 'with me or against me'. When a friend's honest criticism turns out to help her, the prediction fails, and she revises the construct. Option a is right because the scientist metaphor is the root of Kelly's whole theory. Exam trap: Kelly's theory is cognitive, yet it is often taught beside humanistic theories, so students wrongly credit the metaphor to phenomenologists such as Donald Snygg and Arthur Combs or to existential thinkers.", "traps": {"b": "Donald Snygg and Arthur Combs (1949) built a phenomenological theory around the phenomenal field, the whole world as the person experiences it at a given moment; it is not the scientist metaphor.", "c": "Medard Boss (1903 to 1990), a Swiss psychiatrist, developed Daseinsanalysis, an existential therapy based on Martin Heidegger's (1927) philosophy of being-in-the-world.", "d": "Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997) founded logotherapy, which treats the will to meaning as the main human motive; his ideas are existential, not Kelly's constructive alternativism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cognitive distortion?", "concept": "The answer is a: apprehension is not a cognitive distortion. It is an emotion, an anxious feeling of unease about what might happen. Cognitive distortions are habitual errors in thinking that make events seem worse than they are. Aaron Beck (1963) described them while studying the thinking of depressed patients, and David Burns (1980) popularised a list of ten in Feeling Good. They include all-or-none (dichotomous) thinking, overgeneralisation, mental filter, disqualifying the positive, jumping to conclusions (mind reading and fortune telling), magnification or catastrophising, emotional reasoning, should statements, labelling and personalisation. In Beck's cognitive model, a situation triggers automatic thoughts, the distortions bend those thoughts, and the bent thoughts then produce feelings such as sadness or anxiety. Therapy teaches clients to catch the thought, name the distortion and test it against the evidence. For example, Kabir scores 55 in one test and thinks, 'I am a complete failure and everyone will laugh at me.' That thought shows all-or-none thinking and catastrophising; the apprehension he feels the next morning is the emotional result, not the error itself. Option a is right because apprehension sits on the feeling side of the cognitive model, not on the thinking side. Exam trap: emotional reasoning ('I feel apprehensive, so something bad must be coming') is a distortion, which can make a feeling word look like a thinking error; but the distortion is the faulty conclusion drawn from the feeling, not the feeling itself.", "traps": {"b": "All-or-none (dichotomous) thinking is a core Beck and Burns distortion: seeing things in black-and-white categories, so anything short of perfect counts as total failure.", "c": "Catastrophising (magnification) is a distortion in which a person expects the worst possible outcome; it is close to Albert Ellis's (1962) 'awfulising'.", "d": "Personalisation is a distortion in which a person takes responsibility for events outside his or her control, such as blaming oneself for a friend's bad mood."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not an IQ test?", "concept": "The answer is c: IDEAS is not an IQ test. IDEAS is the Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale, published by the Indian Psychiatric Society (2002) to rate the disability caused by mental illness. It is used in India to certify psychiatric disability so that patients can receive government benefits. The clinician rates the person's functioning in four areas (self-care, interpersonal activities, communication and understanding, and work) and adds a score for the duration of illness; the total converts to a percentage of disability. It measures how much an illness limits daily life, not intelligence. The other three are intelligence tests. C. M. Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence (1955) has five subtests (Kohs Block Design, Alexander Pass-Along, Pattern Drawing, Immediate Memory and Picture Construction) and suits people with little schooling. The WAIS, by David Wechsler (1955), is the standard adult intelligence scale. MISIC is Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, Arthur J. Malin's (1969) Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children. For example, Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for six years, is assessed with IDEAS for a disability certificate, while his younger cousin with learning problems is tested with MISIC to obtain an IQ. Option c is right because IDEAS yields a disability percentage, not an IQ. Exam trap: the acronym looks as if it measures 'ideas' or thinking ability. Also do not confuse it with other Indian tools such as the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA), which is not an IQ test either.", "traps": {"a": "The Bhatia battery (C. M. Bhatia, 1955) is an Indian performance test of intelligence with five subtests, giving a performance IQ without needing literacy.", "b": "The WAIS (David Wechsler; fourth edition 2008) is the most widely used adult IQ test, giving a Full Scale IQ from four index scores.", "d": "MISIC (Arthur J. Malin, 1969) is an Indian adaptation of the WISC that yields verbal, performance and full-scale IQs for children."}}, {"q": "\"People control basic anxiety by moving toward, away from, and against others.\" Which group of personality psychologists theorised this statement?", "concept": "The answer is b: the statement comes from Karen Horney (1945), a neo-Freudian. Neo-Freudians kept Sigmund Freud's (1856 to 1939) ideas of unconscious conflict and the importance of childhood, but replaced his sexual and aggressive instincts with social and cultural forces. Horney argued that a child raised with indifference, inconsistency or domination develops basic anxiety: a feeling of being alone and helpless in a potentially hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that she first listed as ten neurotic needs (1942) and then grouped, in Our Inner Conflicts (1945), into three trends. Moving toward people is the compliant type, seeking love and approval. Moving against people is the aggressive type, seeking power and control. Moving away from people is the detached type, seeking independence and privacy. A healthy person uses all three flexibly as the situation needs; a neurotic person relies rigidly on one, which creates inner conflict. For example, Riya, whose parents were cold and unpredictable, always agrees with her friends and cannot say no (moving toward), while her brother Kabir picks fights to stay on top (moving against). Option b is right because Horney is a classic neo-Freudian, alongside Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), Erich Fromm (1900 to 1980) and Harry Stack Sullivan (1892 to 1949). Exam trap: option a tempts because Horney trained as a psychoanalyst, but 'psychoanalytic' in exam options means Freud's instinct-based theory, which Horney openly criticised, for example by rejecting penis envy.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic theory is Sigmund Freud's classical model of id, ego and superego driven by sexual and aggressive instincts; Horney left it to stress the social and cultural causes of anxiety.", "c": "Social learning theorists such as Albert Bandura (1977) and Julian Rotter (1954) explain personality through observation, reinforcement and expectancy, not through basic anxiety.", "d": "Cognitive theorists such as George Kelly (1955) explain personality through how people construe and interpret events, not through defensive interpersonal trends."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a method used in descriptive research?", "concept": "The answer is b: an experiment is not a descriptive method. Descriptive research aims to describe behaviour as it is: what happens, how often and in whom. It does not manipulate anything, so it cannot show cause and effect. Its main methods are the case study, the survey and naturalistic observation. A case study is an intensive study of one person or a small group, often over a long time. A survey asks a large sample questions through questionnaires or interviews, as in Alfred Kinsey's (1948) studies of sexual behaviour. Naturalistic observation records behaviour in its usual setting without interfering, as Jane Goodall (1960) did when she began watching chimpanzees at Gombe. An experiment is different in kind. The researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls other factors, ideally assigns participants to conditions at random, and measures the effect on a dependent variable. It is the only method that can establish causation. For example, Meera wants to study students' screen time. Watching them in the canteen or surveying 500 students describes their habits. Randomly assigning half of them to a no-phone week and comparing their sleep with the other half is an experiment. Option b is right because experiments test causes by manipulating variables, while the other three only describe. Exam trap: correlational studies are sometimes grouped with descriptive research because they also manipulate nothing, and a 'field experiment' takes place in a natural setting but is still an experiment, not naturalistic observation.", "traps": {"a": "A case study is an in-depth description of one person or group, such as Sigmund Freud's case histories; it describes but does not manipulate.", "c": "A survey describes the attitudes or behaviour of a sample through questionnaires or interviews, as in Alfred Kinsey's work; it is a classic descriptive method.", "d": "Naturalistic observation describes behaviour in its natural setting without interference, as in Jane Goodall's chimpanzee studies; nothing is manipulated."}}, {"q": "The therapeutic method of systematic desensitization is believed to work by means of:", "concept": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is believed to work by counterconditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the technique on the idea that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time. Deep relaxation is incompatible with anxiety, so if relaxation is paired again and again with a feared stimulus, the old conditioned response (fear) is replaced by a new one (calm). Replacing a conditioned response with an opposite one is counterconditioning, and Wolpe called the underlying process reciprocal inhibition. An early example came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), who freed a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate food he liked. The therapy has three steps: training in progressive muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929), building an anxiety hierarchy of situations ranked from least to most frightening, and then imagining each step while relaxed, moving up only when the current step causes no anxiety. For example, Kabir fears dogs. He starts by picturing a photo of a puppy while relaxed, and weeks later he can imagine stroking a large dog and stay calm. Option c is right because the method replaces fear with relaxation through repeated pairing. Exam trap: reciprocal inhibition (Wolpe) is not reactive inhibition (Clark Hull). Later research suggested that exposure itself, through extinction, may do much of the work, but the classic textbook mechanism is counterconditioning.", "traps": {"a": "Implosion (implosive therapy) is Thomas Stampfl's (1967) technique of imagining the most frightening scenes at full intensity, without relaxation, so that anxiety extinguishes; it is the opposite of graded pairing.", "b": "Paradoxical intention is a logotherapy technique of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), in which the client deliberately wishes for or exaggerates the feared symptom to break anticipatory anxiety.", "d": "Reactive inhibition is Clark Hull's (1943) fatigue-like inhibition that builds up with repeated responding and fades with rest; it has nothing to do with replacing fear by relaxation."}}, {"q": "According to the Indian Lunacy Act, who can be detained in an asylum without a medical certificate?", "concept": "The answer is b: under the Indian Lunacy Act 1912, a criminal lunatic could be detained in an asylum without the usual medical certificate. The Act brought together the earlier colonial lunacy laws and governed mental health care in India for most of the twentieth century. It was custodial: its main purpose was to detain 'lunatics' in asylums and protect society, not to treat patients or protect their rights. The usual civil route was a reception order made by a magistrate on a petition, and a petition needed medical certificates confirming that the person was of unsound mind. A criminal lunatic was different. This was a person found to be insane in a criminal proceeding, for example an accused person found unfit to stand trial because of unsoundness of mind. Such a person was sent to the asylum on the order of the court or the government, and that order took the place of the medical certificate. For example, if Ramesh is charged with assault and the court finds him insane, it is the court's order, not a doctor's certificate, that sends him to the asylum. Option b is right because a legal order from the criminal court or the government was itself the authority for detention. Exam trap: students expect the 'criminal' case to need more paperwork, not less. Remember too that the 1912 Act was replaced by the Mental Health Act 1987 (in force 1993), which dropped the word 'lunatic' for 'mentally ill person', and then by the rights-based Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (in force 2018).", "traps": {"a": "A minor had no special exemption; the Act tied detention without a certificate to an order of a criminal court or the government, not to the person's age.", "c": "The Act applied to persons of unsound mind of either sex; there was no rule letting only male lunatics be detained without certification.", "d": "Old age gave no exemption either; an elderly person admitted through the civil route still needed a petition backed by medical certificates."}}, {"q": "Which subtest is not a subtest of the WAIS-IV?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Pass-Along test is not a WAIS-IV subtest. The WAIS-IV (2008) is the fourth edition of the adult intelligence scale of David Wechsler (1939, Wechsler-Bellevue), for ages 16 to 90. It has 15 subtests, 10 core and 5 supplemental, grouped into four index scores. Verbal Comprehension: Similarities, Vocabulary and Information (core), with Comprehension. Perceptual Reasoning: Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles (core), with Picture Completion and Figure Weights. Working Memory: Digit Span and Arithmetic (core), with Letter-Number Sequencing. Processing Speed: Symbol Search and Coding (core), with Cancellation. The Pass-Along test belongs to a different tradition. W. P. Alexander (1932) designed it for his performance battery: the person slides coloured blocks along a tray, without lifting them, until they match a pattern on a card. C. M. Bhatia (1955) later included it in his Indian Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence. For example, Meera, a farm worker who cannot read, might be given the Bhatia battery with Pass-Along and Kohs blocks, while a college student would be given the WAIS-IV with Digit Span and Visual Puzzles. Option c is right because no WAIS-IV subtest uses the Pass-Along task or anything like it. Exam trap: Kohs blocks and Pass-Along sit side by side in the Bhatia battery, so students assume both are Wechsler tasks. Key note: the Kohs Block Design test (Samuel Kohs, 1920) is not literally a WAIS-IV subtest either, but Wechsler's Block Design is built directly on Kohs blocks, so the paper treats it as present. The Pass-Along test has no WAIS counterpart, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Kohs Block Design (Samuel Kohs, 1920) asks the person to copy designs with coloured cubes; Wechsler adopted it as the WAIS Block Design subtest, so it counts as present.", "b": "Digit Span is a core Working Memory subtest of the WAIS-IV, with forward, backward and sequencing trials.", "d": "Visual Puzzles is a core Perceptual Reasoning subtest added in the WAIS-IV (2008): the person chooses the three pieces that together make a target puzzle."}}, {"q": "'Poverty of content' in schizophrenia is when:", "concept": "The answer is a: poverty of content of speech is speech that is adequate in amount, often with plenty of words, but that conveys little information. Nancy Andreasen (1979) defined it in her Scale for the Assessment of Thought, Language and Communication (TLC). The speech is vague, over-abstract, over-concrete, repetitive or stereotyped, so a long answer can be summed up in a few words. The listener comes away thinking, 'he said a lot but told me nothing.' Andreasen contrasted it with poverty of speech, in which the amount of speech itself is reduced: brief, one-word replies and little spontaneous talk. Both are forms of alogia, a negative symptom of schizophrenia. For example, asked what he did yesterday, Kabir talks for two minutes about 'the forces of life and the way things go on in the general sense of the universe', yet never says what he did. That is poverty of content. If he had simply said 'nothing' and fallen silent, that would be poverty of speech. Option a is right because it is the only option that describes plenty of words carrying little meaning. Exam trap: poverty of content (empty meaning) and poverty of speech (few words) sound alike but are opposite on quantity. Key note: option a gets the core idea right, but its 'grammatically incorrect' tail is off, since in poverty of content the grammar is usually intact. It is still the only option that describes adequate quantity with empty content, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Jumping from one topic to another, even within a sentence, describes derailment or loosening of associations (Eugen Bleuler, 1911), a disorder of the links between ideas rather than of empty content.", "c": "Poor vocabulary reflects limited education or a language problem; it is not one of Nancy Andreasen's thought disorder terms.", "d": "Poor grammar suggests agrammatism, as in Broca's aphasia, or incoherence (word salad); in poverty of content the sentences are usually grammatical."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following does not measure aptitude?", "concept": "The answer is b: the TAT does not measure aptitude; it is a projective test of personality. Aptitude is the potential to learn a skill or succeed in an area given training, such as mechanical, clerical or numerical ability, and aptitude tests predict future learning. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was created by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. The person is shown ambiguous pictures and asked to tell a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The examiner looks for themes of needs (such as achievement or affiliation) and press (forces in the environment), following Murray's (1938) need-press theory. There are no right answers, so the test reveals motives and conflicts rather than abilities. The other three are aptitude batteries. The GATB (General Aptitude Test Battery) was developed by the United States Employment Service for job counselling. The DAT (Differential Aptitude Tests) was published by George Bennett and colleagues (1947) for school guidance. TAMANNA (Try And Measure Aptitude And Natural Abilities) was developed by NCERT with CBSE to help Indian students in Classes 9 and 10 choose their subjects. For example, Riya takes TAMANNA in Class 10 to see her numerical and verbal strengths; if a psychologist wants to understand her fear of failure, she might tell stories to TAT cards. Option b is right because the TAT measures personality dynamics, not the potential to learn. Exam trap: the abbreviations look alike, and examiners rely on students not knowing what each one stands for.", "traps": {"a": "The GATB (General Aptitude Test Battery), from the United States Employment Service, measures several job-related aptitudes such as verbal, numerical, spatial and manual skills.", "c": "TAMANNA (Try And Measure Aptitude And Natural Abilities) is the NCERT and CBSE aptitude test for Indian students in Classes 9 and 10.", "d": "The DAT (Differential Aptitude Tests, George Bennett and colleagues, 1947) measures aptitudes such as verbal reasoning, numerical ability and mechanical reasoning for educational and vocational guidance."}}, {"q": "Re-educative and reconstructive psychotherapies usually focus on the ______ of defences an individual has:", "concept": "The answer is a: re-educative and reconstructive psychotherapies usually aim at the alteration of a person's defences. Lewis Wolberg (1954), in The Technique of Psychotherapy, grouped psychotherapies into three classes by their depth and goals. Supportive therapy (reassurance, guidance, persuasion, changing the environment) aims to strengthen existing defences and restore the person to an earlier balance, without trying to change the personality. Re-educative therapy (for example behaviour therapy, directive counselling and relationship therapy) aims at a deliberate readjustment of attitudes and behaviour, modifying how the person copes while largely leaving unconscious conflicts alone. Reconstructive therapy (psychoanalysis and psychoanalytically oriented therapy) aims at insight into unconscious conflicts, so that the character structure itself changes and new growth becomes possible. Both deeper groups therefore set out to change defences rather than shore them up: re-educative therapy modifies them, and reconstructive therapy rebuilds them from the roots. For example, Arjun breaks down after losing his job. Supportive therapy would calm him and help him return to his old way of coping. Re-educative therapy would teach him new ways of handling criticism. Reconstructive therapy would explore why failure feels like annihilation to him, trace it to his childhood and change that pattern at its source. Option a is right because alteration of defences is exactly what separates these two deeper approaches from supportive work. Exam trap: restoration (option c) is the aim of supportive therapy, the near miss that examiners place beside the deeper approaches.", "traps": {"b": "Facilitation is not one of Lewis Wolberg's aims; the word suggests Carl Rogers's (1951) picture of the therapist as a facilitator of growth, not a change in defences.", "c": "Restoration of defences is the goal of supportive therapy, which strengthens existing defences to bring back balance rather than changing them.", "d": "Adoption of defences is not a category in Wolberg's scheme; therapies may teach new coping, but the defined aim of the deeper therapies is to alter existing defences."}}, {"q": "A fixed interval schedule is one that is based on a:", "concept": "The answer is d: a fixed interval schedule is based on a set period of time. In a fixed interval (FI) schedule, the first response made after a fixed amount of time has passed is reinforced; responses during the interval earn nothing. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957) described the four basic partial schedules in Schedules of Reinforcement. Ratio schedules depend on the number of responses and interval schedules on time; 'fixed' means the requirement stays the same, while 'variable' means it changes around an average. FI produces a typical scallop on the cumulative record: a pause just after each reinforcer, then faster and faster responding as the next one becomes due. For example, Meera has a class test every Friday. She barely studies on Saturday and Sunday, but her studying speeds up sharply by Thursday night, a classic FI pattern. Option d is right because the defining requirement of FI is a constant amount of time, not a number of responses. Exam trap: on FI a response is still required, since time alone earns nothing; giving the reinforcer after a fixed time regardless of behaviour is a different schedule, called fixed time. Remember the rule: 'ratio' means number, 'interval' means time. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks c, a set number of responses. That key is wrong: a set number of responses defines a fixed ratio schedule, while a fixed interval is defined by time, so d stays.", "traps": {"a": "A varying number of responses defines a variable ratio (VR) schedule, as in a slot machine; it produces high, steady responding and strong resistance to extinction.", "b": "A varying period of time defines a variable interval (VI) schedule, such as checking for new messages; it produces slow, steady responding without scallops.", "c": "A set number of responses defines a fixed ratio (FR) schedule, such as being paid for every ten pieces made; it produces fast responding with a pause after each reinforcer."}}, {"q": "Individuals prone to depression often make ______ and ______ attributions for their problems:", "concept": "The answer is a: people prone to depression tend to explain their bad events with internal and stable attributions. Learned helplessness was first described by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967): dogs given shocks they could not escape later stopped trying to escape even when they could. To explain why only some people become helpless after setbacks, Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated the theory in terms of attributions, the causes people give for events. They proposed three dimensions. Internal versus external: is the cause me or the situation? Stable versus unstable: will it last or pass? Global versus specific: does it affect everything or just this one area? A depressive attributional style explains bad events as internal ('it is my fault'), stable ('it will always be like this') and global ('I ruin everything'). Internal causes lower self-esteem, stable causes make helplessness last, and global causes spread it to every part of life. For example, when Riya fails her driving test, she thinks, 'I am hopeless and I will never learn anything.' Her friend Meera, who also fails, thinks, 'The examiner was strict today and I was nervous', an external, unstable and specific explanation that protects her mood. Option a is right because internal and stable are the two offered dimensions that match the depressive style. Exam trap: Bernard Weiner's (1985) third attribution dimension is controllability, while the third dimension of Abramson and colleagues is globality; and the self-serving bias is the opposite, non-depressive pattern of blaming outside causes for failure.", "traps": {"b": "Internal and unstable attributions ('I did not practise enough this time') blame the self but only for a passing cause such as effort; this tends to motivate more effort rather than hopelessness.", "c": "External and unstable attributions ('it was bad luck today') are the optimistic style that protects mood, the opposite of the depressive pattern.", "d": "External and stable attributions ('the system is always against me') blame a lasting outside cause; they protect self-esteem and may bring anger or resentment, not the self-blame of depression."}}, {"q": "Which is NOT a principle of nonverbal communication?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'it is possible not to communicate nonverbally' is not a principle; the real principle is the reverse. Paul Watzlawick, Janet Beavin Bavelas and Don Jackson (1967), in Pragmatics of Human Communication, stated the first axiom of communication: one cannot not communicate. Every behaviour in the presence of another person carries a message, and there is no such thing as non-behaviour. Silence, looking away, sitting with folded arms or leaving the room all say something, whether or not the person intends it. Nonverbal communication is the exchange of messages through facial expression, eye contact, gesture, posture, touch, use of space and tone of voice. Textbooks list several principles: it occurs even without words, it is continuous, it is often ambiguous, it is culture-bound, it carries much of the emotional and relational meaning, and when it contradicts the words, people usually believe the nonverbal message. For example, Kabir sits silently through a family meeting, staring at his phone. He says nothing, yet everyone reads his boredom or anger. Option b is right because it denies the most basic principle of nonverbal communication. Exam trap: the item asks which is NOT a principle, so the three true principles are the distractors. Also, Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) showed that some basic facial expressions are universal, but display rules and gestures vary by culture, so 'culture-bound' remains a true principle.", "traps": {"a": "That communication occurs without language is a true principle: facial expression, gesture, posture and tone send messages without a single word.", "c": "The word is missing in the original paper (probably 'ambiguous' or 'continuous'), but whatever it was, option c was meant as a true principle of nonverbal communication, so it is not the answer.", "d": "That nonverbal communication is culture-bound is a true principle: gestures, eye contact and personal space mean different things in different cultures, such as the Indian head wobble."}}, {"q": "Helping someone with a 'severe disorder' of functionality, personality, and aberrations of thoughts, feelings and behaviour is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: helping someone with a severe disorder of functioning and personality, with marked disturbance of thoughts, feelings and behaviour, is psychotherapy. Psychotherapy is a planned, professional treatment in which a trained therapist uses psychological methods to relieve symptoms, change disturbed patterns of behaviour and promote personality growth, much as Lewis Wolberg (1954) defined it. It is used for clinical conditions such as major depression, personality disorders and obsessive-compulsive disorder. It often goes deeper and lasts longer, and it may aim to change long-standing personality patterns. Counselling, in the classic textbook contrast, serves essentially normal people facing developmental, situational or adjustment problems: choosing a career, exam stress, a break-up. It is usually shorter, focused on the present and on conscious problems, and draws on the person's existing strengths. For example, Meera, who is anxious about her board exams, is well served by a few counselling sessions. Arjun, who has had severe mood swings, self-harm and unstable relationships since his teens, needs psychotherapy. Option b is right because the stem stresses severity, personality-level problems and disturbed thoughts, feelings and behaviour, which are the province of psychotherapy. Exam trap: the line between the two is blurred in practice, and Carl Rogers (1942) used the words almost interchangeably in Counseling and Psychotherapy; but in exams, normal problems map to counselling and severe disorders to psychotherapy.", "traps": {"a": "Counselling helps essentially normal people with adjustment, developmental and situational problems; it is not designed for severe personality-level disorders.", "c": "Advising means giving information or recommendations about what to do; it involves no treatment of disturbed thoughts or feelings.", "d": "Guiding (guidance), rooted in Frank Parsons's (1909) vocational guidance, helps people make educational and career choices; it is not treatment of severe disorders."}}, {"q": "One important issue in therapy for specific phobias is to address:", "concept": "The answer is b: therapy for a specific phobia must address the phobic beliefs the sufferer holds about the feared object or situation. A specific phobia is an intense, irrational fear of one object or situation (spiders, heights, injections) that the person knows is excessive yet still avoids. Cognitive models say the fear is kept alive by beliefs about danger: a spider phobic may believe a spider will run straight at her, crawl inside her clothes, or that she will lose control or faint with fear. These beliefs overestimate both the likelihood and the cost of harm, and they drive avoidance. Modern treatment therefore combines exposure with cognitive work. Joseph Wolpe (1958) used systematic desensitisation, and Lars-Göran Öst (1989) showed that a single long session of guided exposure can cure many specific phobias. In both, exposure works partly as a behavioural experiment: the client tests the belief and finds it false. Example: Meera is terrified of lizards and believes one will leap onto her face. Her therapist asks her to rate how strongly she holds this belief (90 per cent), then stays with her near a lizard for twenty minutes; it never leaps, and her belief drops to 20 per cent, along with her fear. Option b is right because changing these danger beliefs, through questioning and through exposure that disconfirms them, is the core of effective phobia therapy. Exam trap: the option about never meeting the phobic stimulus sounds kind, but avoidance is exactly what maintains a phobia (O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory, 1947): each escape relieves fear, so avoidance is negatively reinforced and the false belief is never tested.", "traps": {"a": "Keeping the person away from the phobic stimulus is avoidance, which O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory shows maintains fear through negative reinforcement; therapy aims at contact through exposure, not permanent escape.", "c": "Simply talking about the feared situation is ventilation, a supportive or cathartic step; talk alone does not change the danger beliefs or give the disconfirming exposure that treatment needs.", "d": "Analysing dreams is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic method (his 1909 Little Hans case read a horse phobia as displaced Oedipal fear); it is not an evidence-based focus of phobia therapy."}}, {"q": "How many chromatic cards does the Rorschach Inkblot Test have?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test has five chromatic (coloured) cards out of its ten. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in Psychodiagnostik. It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order from Card I to Card X. Five are achromatic, printed only in black and grey: Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Two are black with bright red patches: Cards II and III. Three are fully multicoloured in pastel shades: Cards VIII, IX and X. Chromatic simply means carrying colour, so the chromatic cards number 2 plus 3, which makes 5. This is why textbooks describe the set as five black-and-white and five coloured. Colour matters in scoring: responses driven by colour (FC, CF, C) are read as signs of emotional responsiveness and control, and a sudden slowing, rejection or disorganisation on the first red card (Card II) or the first all-colour card (Card VIII) was classically called colour shock. Example: Arjun answers Card I fluently, then falls silent for forty seconds at Card II; the examiner notes the delay as a possible reaction to the red. Option a is right because Cards II, III, VIII, IX and X all contain colour, giving five chromatic cards. Exam trap: 3 counts only the fully coloured cards and forgets the red in II and III; 2 counts only the black-and-red pair; 10 is the total number of cards. Key note: some books call only the three pastel cards chromatic and treat II and III as a separate black-and-red group, which is why 3 tempts; but Cards II and III carry bright red, and the usual count of chromatic cards is five, so a stands. GMCH's own key for this paper marks d, 10. That key is wrong: 10 is the total number of Rorschach cards, and five of them (I, IV, V, VI and VII) carry no colour at all, so a stays.", "traps": {"b": "Two is the number of black-and-red cards (II and III); they are chromatic, but so are the three fully coloured cards, so 2 undercounts.", "c": "Three is the number of fully multicoloured cards (VIII, IX and X); it leaves out the red of Cards II and III, which also makes them chromatic.", "d": "Ten is the total number of Rorschach cards (five achromatic plus five chromatic), not the number of coloured ones."}}, {"q": "The therapeutic method of systematic desensitization is believed to work by means of:", "concept": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is believed to work by counterconditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the technique on the idea that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time. Deep relaxation is incompatible with anxiety, so if relaxation is paired again and again with a feared stimulus, the old conditioned response (fear) is replaced by a new one (calm). Replacing a conditioned response with an opposite one is counterconditioning, and Wolpe called the underlying process reciprocal inhibition. An early example came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), who freed a boy called Peter of his fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate food he liked. The therapy has three steps: training in progressive muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929), building an anxiety hierarchy of situations ranked from least to most frightening, and then imagining each step while relaxed, moving up only when the current step causes no anxiety. For example, Kabir fears dogs. He starts by picturing a photo of a puppy while relaxed, and weeks later he can imagine stroking a large dog and stay calm. Option c is right because the method replaces fear with relaxation through repeated pairing. Exam trap: reciprocal inhibition (Wolpe) is not reactive inhibition (Clark Hull). Later research suggested that exposure itself, through extinction, may do much of the work, but the classic textbook mechanism is counterconditioning.", "traps": {"a": "Implosion (implosive therapy) is Thomas Stampfl's (1967) technique of imagining the most frightening scenes at full intensity, without relaxation, so that anxiety extinguishes; it is the opposite of graded pairing.", "b": "Paradoxical intention is a logotherapy technique of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), in which the client deliberately wishes for or exaggerates the feared symptom to break anticipatory anxiety.", "d": "Reactive inhibition is Clark Hull's (1943) fatigue-like inhibition that builds up with repeated responding and fades with rest; it has nothing to do with replacing fear by relaxation."}}, {"q": "Kohlberg is known for his theory of:", "concept": "The answer is d: Lawrence Kohlberg is known for his theory of moral development. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), in his doctoral work at the University of Chicago, extended Jean Piaget's (1932) ideas on children's moral judgement into a stage theory of moral reasoning. He posed moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma (should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife?), and scored the reasons people gave, not their yes or no answers. He described three levels with two stages each. At the preconventional level, right is what avoids punishment (Stage 1) or serves one's own interest (Stage 2). At the conventional level, right is what wins approval (Stage 3, good boy-nice girl) or upholds law and order (Stage 4). At the postconventional level, right rests on social contract and individual rights (Stage 5) and on universal ethical principles (Stage 6). The stages are said to follow an invariant order and to depend on cognitive growth. Example: asked about Heinz, Arjun (8) says he should not steal or he will go to jail (Stage 1), while his sister Riya (16) says he should steal because a good husband looks after his wife (Stage 3). Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory as biased towards a male ethic of justice and proposed an ethic of care. Option d is right because Kohlberg's stages describe how moral reasoning develops. Exam trap: because Kohlberg was a cognitive-developmental theorist who built on Piaget, cognitive development (a) is the near miss; but the cognitive stages (sensorimotor to formal operations) are Piaget's, and Kohlberg's own theory is about morality.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive development is Jean Piaget's stage theory (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete and formal operations); Kohlberg borrowed its logic but applied it to moral reasoning.", "b": "Social or psychosocial development is linked with Erik Erikson's eight stages (1950) and Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory, not with Kohlberg.", "c": "Physical and motor development is the field of maturation theorists such as Arnold Gesell (age norms of growth), not of Kohlberg's moral dilemmas."}}, {"q": "Orientation, attention span, recent memory and remote memory are included in which part of the mental status examination?", "concept": "The answer is c: orientation, attention span, recent memory and remote memory are tested in the cognition section of the mental status examination (MSE). The MSE is a structured, here-and-now description of a patient's mental state, recorded at interview much as a physical examination records the body. It usually runs: appearance, behaviour (and speech), mood and affect, thought (form, stream and content), perception, cognition, insight and judgement. The cognitive part checks the higher functions in a set order: level of consciousness; orientation to time, place and person (time is usually lost first, for example in delirium); attention and concentration (digit span, serial sevens from 100, months backwards); memory, immediate (repeat three words), recent (recall them after five minutes, or describe yesterday's meals) and remote (childhood events, past personal history); general information and intelligence; and abstract thinking (proverbs, similarities). Bedside tools such as the Mini-Mental State Examination (Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975) package many of these tests. Example: Kabir, aged 72, cannot say the date or the name of the hospital and recalls none of three words after five minutes, yet describes his school days in detail; his recent memory is impaired and his remote memory preserved, a pattern typical of early dementia. Option c is right because orientation, attention and memory are cognitive functions, grouped under cognition (often called the sensorium) in the MSE. Exam trap: thought processes (d) sounds mental enough to attract candidates, but it describes the form and flow of thinking (flight of ideas, loosening of associations, thought block), not orientation or memory.", "traps": {"a": "Appearance records what the examiner sees: build, dress, grooming, hygiene and apparent age; it does not test memory or orientation.", "b": "Behaviour covers psychomotor activity, eye contact, gestures, rapport and attitude to the interviewer; it is observed, whereas orientation and memory have to be tested.", "d": "Thought processes describe the form and stream of thinking (goal-directedness, flight of ideas, derailment, thought block); they do not cover orientation or memory."}}, {"q": "______ is a strategy or course of action enabling a person to live an acceptable life with a problem that may be intractable and incapable of solution.", "concept": "The answer is d: a resolution is a strategy or course of action that lets a person live an acceptable life with a problem that may be intractable and incapable of solution. In counselling, problems are of two broad kinds. Some can be solved: a solution removes the problem altogether, as when a student who failed an examination through poor study habits learns a study plan and passes. Others cannot be removed: a chronic illness, a permanent disability, a bereavement, a family member who will not change. For these, insisting on a solution only breeds frustration and a sense of failure. The counsellor instead helps the client find a resolution: a way of understanding and managing the problem so that life is still satisfying, through new coping skills, changed expectations, support and acceptance. The problem remains, but its hold over the person's life shrinks. Example: Riya, aged 30, has type 1 diabetes. No counselling will cure it, so the goal is not a solution. With her counsellor she builds a routine for insulin and meals, joins a support group and plans her work around clinic days; she now describes her life as full, with diabetes in the background. That is a resolution. Option d is right because the stem's key words, living an acceptable life with a problem that is incapable of solution, define resolution, the counterpart of solution. Exam trap: solution (b) shares the stem's subject, problems, and is the word most people reach for, but the stem itself says the problem is incapable of solution, so the strategy cannot be a solution.", "traps": {"a": "Investment means what a person puts into something, such as the time, effort and emotion a client commits to therapy or a relationship; it names commitment, not a way of living with an unsolvable problem.", "b": "A solution removes or ends the problem; the stem describes a problem that may be intractable and incapable of solution, so a solution is exactly what is unavailable.", "c": "Congruence is Carl Rogers's (1957) term for the counsellor's genuineness, the match between inner experience and outward expression; it is a core condition of the relationship, not a coping strategy."}}, {"q": "An IQ ranging between 70 and 80 is:", "concept": "The answer is c: an IQ of about 70 to 79 falls in the borderline range. A modern IQ is a deviation IQ: David Wechsler (1939) compared each person with others of the same age, on a scale with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Score bands are named by their distance from the mean. In the Wechsler classification, 130 and above is very superior, 120 to 129 superior, 110 to 119 high average, 90 to 109 average, 80 to 89 low average, 70 to 79 borderline, and 69 and below extremely low, the range where intellectual disability is considered. A score of 70 lies 2 standard deviations below the mean, at about the 2nd percentile, so the borderline band sits just above the cut-off for intellectual disability. ICD-10 grades intellectual disability (formerly mental retardation) as mild (50 to 69), moderate (35 to 49), severe (20 to 34) and profound (below 20), and both ICD and DSM-5 also require deficits in adaptive behaviour. Example: Kabir, aged 12, scores 75 on the WISC. He reads two years behind his class and needs extra help, but he dresses, travels and shops on his own; he has borderline intellectual functioning, not intellectual disability. Option c is right because 70 to 79 is the borderline band, just below low average and just above the range for mild mental retardation. Exam trap: the stem's upper end, 80, really belongs to the low average band (80 to 89), which tempts candidates towards b; read the range as 70 to 79, which is borderline.", "traps": {"a": "Mild mental retardation (now mild intellectual disability) is IQ 50 to 69 in ICD-10, below the borderline band, and it also needs adaptive deficits.", "b": "Low average is IQ 80 to 89 on the Wechsler scales, one band above borderline.", "d": "Average is IQ 90 to 109, the middle half of the population, well above 70 to 79."}}, {"q": "The apparent lack of empathy and the tendency to exploit others for self-benefit have led psychologists to compare narcissistic personality disorder with which one of the following?", "concept": "The answer is b: narcissistic personality disorder is often compared with antisocial personality disorder (APD), because both show a lack of empathy and a readiness to exploit others for personal gain. Both are Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, erratic) disorders in DSM-5. Narcissistic personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy; two of its nine criteria are being interpersonally exploitative (taking advantage of others to achieve one's own ends) and lacking empathy. APD is a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others since age 15, with deceitfulness, impulsivity, aggression, irresponsibility and lack of remorse. The overlap is strong enough that Otto Kernberg (1984) described malignant narcissism, a severe form in which narcissism mixes with antisocial behaviour, aggression and paranoid traits, and Robert Hare's psychopathy checklist (PCL-R, 1991) includes a grandiose sense of self-worth. The difference lies in motive and style: the narcissist exploits mainly to protect an inflated but fragile self-image and win admiration, while the antisocial person exploits for material gain or thrills, often breaking the law. Example: Vikram takes credit for a junior's report because he believes he deserves the spotlight (narcissistic); his cousin forges his employer's signature to take money, then shrugs it off (antisocial). Option b is right because lack of empathy and exploitation are core features shared by narcissistic and antisocial personality disorders. Exam trap: histrionic personality disorder (a) is also Cluster B and also craves attention, but its hallmark is emotional, seductive display, not cold exploitation, so it is only the near miss.", "traps": {"a": "Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) is excessive emotionality and attention-seeking, often seductive and theatrical; it shares a need for attention with narcissism but not the cold exploitation seen in APD.", "c": "Paranoid personality disorder (Cluster A) is pervasive distrust and suspicion, reading others' motives as malevolent; its core is suspicion, not exploitation.", "d": "Schizotypal personality disorder (Cluster A) involves odd beliefs, magical thinking, eccentric behaviour and social anxiety; it belongs to the schizophrenia spectrum, not to exploitation."}}, {"q": "If we talk about the nature of psychotherapies, which of the following is not true?", "concept": "The answer is c: psychotherapy does not follow a fixed procedure for all clients; it is tailored to each person. Psychotherapy is a voluntary, structured relationship in which a trained therapist uses psychological methods to help a client change troubling thoughts, feelings and behaviour. Standard descriptions of its nature (for example in the NCERT Class 12 psychology text used across India) list several features: it involves the systematic application of principles drawn from a theory of therapy; only persons who have received practical training under expert supervision should practise it; the therapeutic situation involves a therapist and a client who seeks help; and the heart of the work is the therapeutic relationship, which changes over the course of treatment, making therapy a dynamic process. What stays fixed is the framework (confidentiality, goals, boundaries); the techniques, pace and focus are chosen to fit the client's problem, history, culture and readiness. Gordon Paul (1967) put the guiding question well: what treatment, by whom, is most effective for this individual with that specific problem, under which set of circumstances? Example: Riya, who has panic attacks, and Arjun, who is grieving his father, see the same counsellor; Riya gets interoceptive exposure and breathing work, while Arjun gets space to mourn and help rebuilding his routines. Same therapist, different procedures. Option c is right because a single fixed procedure for every client contradicts the individualised, dynamic nature of psychotherapy. Exam trap: in a NOT item, the systematic application in a can be misread as a rigid, fixed procedure; systematic means guided by principles, not identical for everyone.", "traps": {"a": "Systematic application is a true feature: therapy applies the principles of a theory (psychodynamic, behavioural, cognitive, humanistic) in an organised, planned way.", "b": "Calling psychotherapy a dynamic process is true: the client-therapist relationship and the goals evolve over the sessions.", "d": "This is also a true feature: psychotherapy should be practised only by people trained under expert supervision, because an untrained helper can do harm."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the term IQ (intelligence quotient) was given by William Stern. Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision grouped items by age, so a child's level could be expressed as a mental age (MA). A mental age alone is awkward: being two years behind means much more at age 4 than at 14. William Stern (1912), a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), calling the result the Intelligenzquotient, the intelligence or mental quotient. Lewis Terman (1916), revising the scale at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove decimals and made the abbreviation IQ famous: ratio IQ = MA / CA x 100. A child of 10 with a mental age of 12 therefore scores 120. David Wechsler (1939) later replaced the ratio with the deviation IQ, comparing a person with others of the same age (mean 100, standard deviation 15), because mental age stops growing in adulthood. Example: Meera, aged 8, passes the items typical of 10-year-olds; Stern's quotient is 10/8 = 1.25, and Terman's ratio IQ is 125. Option a is right because Stern coined both the quotient and the term in 1912. Exam trap: Binet is the classic distractor because he created the test and the mental-age idea, but he never used an intelligence quotient. Key note: Terman is the near miss, since he added the x100 and popularised IQ in English, which is why some books credit him; but the concept and the word were Stern's, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Alfred Binet, with Théodore Simon (1905), built the first intelligence test and the mental-age idea, but he did not form a quotient or use the term IQ.", "c": "David Wechsler (1939) introduced the deviation IQ, which replaced the ratio IQ; he did not coin the term.", "d": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied Stern's quotient by 100 in the Stanford-Binet and popularised the abbreviation IQ, but the quotient and the term came from Stern."}}, {"q": "Gillian works as a cognitive therapist. She is always very conscientious about attending to the client's experience and responding to the client's needs in the moment. Most of her clients would describe her as warm and supportive. Gillian most likely attends to which ONE of the following constructs associated with cognitive therapies?", "concept": "The answer is c: Gillian is attending to the importance of therapeutic empathy. Cognitive therapies are often pictured as cool and technical, but Aaron Beck and his colleagues, in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979), stressed that the therapist's warmth, accurate empathy and genuineness are necessary, though not sufficient, for change. These qualities come from Carl Rogers (1957), who named empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence as the core conditions of therapy. Therapeutic empathy means sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own and communicating that understanding, moment by moment. In cognitive therapy it has a purpose: a client who feels understood trusts the therapist, discloses painful automatic thoughts and joins the collaborative work of testing them. Research on the working alliance shows that a warm, attuned relationship predicts better outcomes across therapies. Gillian's behaviour (attending to the client's experience, responding to needs in the moment, being seen as warm and supportive) describes empathy, not any technique. Example: when Riya starts crying while filling in a thought record, her therapist pauses the exercise, reflects that this brings up a lot of sadness, and returns to the record only when Riya is ready. Option c is right because warmth, attunement and responsiveness to the client's immediate experience are the marks of therapeutic empathy. Exam trap: the importance of cognitions (b) is the defining construct of cognitive therapy, so it looks like the safe pick, but nothing in the stem mentions thoughts or beliefs; the stem is about the relationship.", "traps": {"a": "The importance of learning refers to the behavioural roots of cognitive-behavioural therapy (classical and operant conditioning, Ivan Pavlov and B. F. Skinner), not to warmth or responsiveness.", "b": "The importance of cognitions is the central assumption that thoughts and beliefs shape feelings (Aaron Beck, Albert Ellis); it is real, but the stem describes relational behaviour, not work on thoughts.", "d": "Operational definitions and functional analysis are behavioural assessment tools: defining a problem in measurable terms and mapping its antecedents and consequences; they describe method, not warmth."}}, {"q": "Which function of communication is illustrated when a person rolls his or her eyes while making a negative comment about another?", "concept": "The answer is b: rolling the eyes while making a negative comment illustrates accenting, a nonverbal cue that emphasises part of a verbal message. Nonverbal behaviour (face, eyes, gesture, posture, voice) works alongside words. Mark Knapp (1972), building on Paul Ekman's work in the 1960s, listed six functions it can serve in relation to speech. Repeating restates the words (saying 'go straight' and pointing ahead). Complementing adds to or fits the words (a sad face while apologising). Substituting replaces words (a nod for yes, a shrug with no speech). Accenting stresses or underlines the words (banging the table on 'never', a raised tone on a key word). Regulating manages the flow of conversation (nodding to keep someone talking, raising a hand to take a turn, looking away to signal the end). Contradicting sends a message opposite to the words ('I'm fine' said through tears). Eye-rolling during a negative remark points the same way as the words and intensifies their scorn, so it accents them. Example: Kabir says 'Rohan's presentation was a disaster' and rolls his eyes on 'disaster'; the roll does not replace, regulate or contradict his words, it underlines his contempt. Option b is right because the eye-roll emphasises the negative message the person is already giving. Exam trap: contradicting (d) is chosen because eye-rolling is famous as a sign of sarcasm; it would contradict only if the words were positive, such as 'great idea' with an eye-roll, but with a negative comment the two channels agree.", "traps": {"a": "Substituting is nonverbal behaviour used instead of words (a nod for yes, a wave for goodbye); here the person is speaking, so nothing is being replaced.", "c": "Regulating controls the back-and-forth of conversation (nods, eye contact and hand raises that manage turn-taking); an eye-roll at a remark does not manage turns.", "d": "Contradicting is a nonverbal signal that conflicts with the words, such as rolling the eyes while praising someone; with a negative comment the eye-roll agrees with the words."}}, {"q": "Jackie was passed over for a promotion in favour of a colleague with more seniority and greater experience. She is upset because she thinks her boss does not consider her qualified. What would a CBT therapist do to help Jackie?", "concept": "The answer is b: a CBT therapist would help Jackie generate realistic alternative explanations for being passed over. Cognitive behavioural therapy, rooted in Aaron Beck's (1976) cognitive model, holds that our interpretation of events, more than the events themselves, shapes how we feel. Jackie's event is the lost promotion; her automatic thought is 'my boss thinks I am not qualified'; her emotion is hurt and upset. The thought is an example of mind reading, a form of jumping to conclusions in David Burns's (1980) list of distortions: she is sure what her boss thinks without evidence. The therapist neither argues with her nor simply reassures her. Through Socratic questioning and collaborative empiricism, the therapist helps her examine the evidence: the colleague had more seniority and greater experience, the company may promote by length of service, and her boss may rate her highly but have followed those rules. Jackie then weighs these alternatives, settles on the most realistic one, and notices how her mood changes. She might even test the thought by asking her boss for feedback, a behavioural experiment. Example: in a similar case, Priya rates her belief 'I'm not good enough' at 85 per cent; after listing other possible reasons for the decision, she rates it at 30 per cent and feels calmer. Option b is right because generating and testing realistic alternative explanations is the core cognitive restructuring step in CBT. Exam trap: empathising (a) sounds caring, and empathy does matter in CBT, but option a also declares that she was treated unfairly, which the facts in the stem do not support; CBT empathises with the feeling while questioning the interpretation.", "traps": {"a": "Empathy is part of the relationship, but declaring that she was unfairly treated takes a one-sided view as fact; the stem gives a fair reason (seniority and experience), so this colludes with the distortion.", "c": "Agreeing that the boss deemed her unqualified confirms her mind-reading distortion without evidence, the opposite of what cognitive restructuring does.", "d": "Advising her to change jobs is directive advice-giving that skips the thought behind the upset; CBT works collaboratively on the interpretation before any practical decision."}}, {"q": "Shell shock was later termed:", "concept": "The answer is d: shell shock was later termed posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). During the First World War, soldiers exposed to heavy shelling developed tremors, mutism, paralysis, nightmares, panic and an exaggerated startle. Charles Myers (1915), a British psychologist, brought the term shell shock into the medical literature, at first thinking the blast itself damaged the nerves; but many cases arose in men never near an explosion, showing the cause was psychological trauma. The condition was later called war neurosis, then combat or battle fatigue in the Second World War, and Abram Kardiner (1941) described The Traumatic Neuroses of War. DSM-I (1952) listed gross stress reaction, and after studies of Vietnam veterans, DSM-III (1980) introduced PTSD. DSM-5 (2013) moved it into a new chapter of trauma- and stressor-related disorders, with four symptom clusters (intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in mood and cognition, and arousal) lasting more than a month. Option d is right because PTSD is the modern diagnosis for the trauma reaction once called shell shock. Exam trap: DPD can stand for depersonalisation disorder, which involves feeling detached and can follow trauma, so it tempts; but it is a dissociative disorder, not the successor of shell shock.", "traps": {"a": "OCD (obsessive-compulsive disorder) is recurrent intrusive obsessions and compulsions, not a reaction to combat trauma.", "b": "ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) is a neurodevelopmental disorder of inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity that begins in childhood; it has nothing to do with war trauma.", "c": "DPD usually stands for dependent personality disorder (a clinging need to be cared for), and sometimes for depersonalisation disorder; neither is the modern name for combat trauma."}}, {"q": "A delinquency offense can be defined as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a delinquency offence is an act committed by a minor for which an adult could be prosecuted in a criminal court. Juvenile delinquency is the legal term for criminal behaviour by young people below the age of adulthood (18 in India). Two kinds of juvenile misconduct are separated. A delinquency offence is an act that would be a crime at any age: theft, assault, robbery, vandalism, drug dealing. Its definition rests on the act itself, and the young person is dealt with by a juvenile system rather than an adult court. A status offence is an act that is illegal only because of the young person's age: truancy, running away from home, underage drinking, breaking curfew. An adult doing the same would not be prosecuted. In India, the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2015 calls a minor accused of an offence a child in conflict with law, and allows those aged 16 to 18 accused of heinous offences to be tried as adults after a preliminary assessment. Psychologically, delinquency overlaps with conduct disorder, and Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated adolescence-limited from life-course-persistent offenders. Example: Arjun, 15, steals a mobile phone from a shop; an adult would face criminal charges for theft, so this is a delinquency offence. His friend Rahul skips school for a month; that is a status offence. Option b is right because a delinquency offence is an act by a minor that would be prosecutable as a crime if an adult did it. Exam trap: options a and b differ by a single word, could versus could not; a, an act for which an adult could not be prosecuted, is the definition of a status offence.", "traps": {"a": "An act by a minor for which an adult could not be prosecuted is a status offence (truancy, running away, underage drinking), illegal only because of age.", "c": "Disequilibrium of homeostasis borrows the language of physiology (Walter Cannon's homeostasis, 1932); it is not a legal or psychological definition of delinquency.", "d": "Lacking a rational motive is no part of the definition, and most delinquency has motives (gain, peer status, thrills); motiveless acts point instead to impulse-control problems such as kleptomania."}}, {"q": "Consider the following statements in relation to psychosis. A: Psychosis refers to disordered thought and behaviour. B: Psychosis is a symptom that may result from several primary psychiatric disorders, general medical conditions and substance use. Which of the following options is right?", "concept": "The answer is c: both statements are correct. Psychosis is a mental state in which a person's contact with reality is seriously impaired. Its main features are hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus, such as hearing voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs held against evidence), disorganised thinking and speech (derailment, incoherence) and grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, usually with poor insight. So Statement A, that psychosis refers to disordered thought and behaviour, captures its core description. Statement B is also true: psychosis is a syndrome, not one disease, and it can arise from many causes. Primary psychiatric causes include schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, brief psychotic disorder, and severe mania or depression with psychotic features. General medical causes include delirium, brain tumours, epilepsy (especially of the temporal lobe), encephalitis, thyroid disease and dementia. Substance causes include amphetamines, cocaine, cannabis, LSD, steroids and alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens). DSM-5 recognises this directly with the categories psychotic disorder due to another medical condition and substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder. Example: Kabir, 25, starts hearing voices after a week of heavy amphetamine use; his doctor first rules out drugs and medical illness before considering schizophrenia, because psychosis is a symptom with many possible sources. Option c is right because A gives the defining features of psychosis and B correctly says it can come from psychiatric, medical or substance causes. Exam trap: Statement A can look too loose, since the textbook core of psychosis is lost reality testing, so some candidates pick only B; but disordered thought and behaviour are central to psychosis, and A is accepted as true.", "traps": {"a": "Only A ignores Statement B, which is also true: psychosis can arise from psychiatric disorders, medical conditions and substances.", "b": "Only B wrongly rejects Statement A; disordered thought (delusions, disorganised speech) and disordered behaviour are core features of psychosis.", "d": "None of the above would mean both statements are false, but each correctly describes psychosis."}}, {"q": "A person is lying inside a scanner, taking part in an experiment after an injection of 2-deoxyglucose. This person is participating in:", "concept": "The answer is d: a scanner plus an injection of 2-deoxyglucose means a positron emission tomography (PET) study. PET measures brain activity through metabolism. A glucose-like tracer labelled with a positron-emitting isotope, usually fluorine-18 in fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), is injected. Active neurons take up more glucose, so they take up more tracer, and because 2-deoxyglucose cannot be fully broken down, it stays trapped in the cells. Each emitted positron meets an electron and releases a pair of gamma rays, which detectors in the ring-shaped scanner record and turn into a map of activity. The method grew from Louis Sokoloff's (1977) deoxyglucose technique for measuring local glucose use in animal brains. PET is used in research on cognition and in clinical work to find tumours, epileptic foci and the reduced metabolism of Alzheimer's disease; it involves radiation and has poor time resolution. Option d is right because an injected radioactive deoxyglucose tracer is the signature of PET. Exam trap: fMRI (c) also uses a scanner and maps activity, so it tempts, but it needs no injection; it tracks the BOLD signal produced by changes in blood oxygen.", "traps": {"a": "An EEG study records the brain's electrical activity through scalp electrodes (Hans Berger, 1929); it needs no scanner and no injection.", "b": "An MEG study measures the tiny magnetic fields produced by neural currents with sensitive SQUID sensors; it is non-invasive and uses no tracer.", "c": "An fMRI study measures the BOLD signal (Seiji Ogawa, 1990), changes in blood oxygen that follow neural activity, without any radioactive injection."}}, {"q": "All of the following statements (except for one) can characterize a professional helping relationship. Which ONE statement does NOT represent a professional helping relationship?", "concept": "The answer is b: a professional helping relationship is not simply one in which one person does something for the other. Carl Rogers (1958) defined a helping relationship as one in which at least one party intends to promote the growth, development, maturity, improved functioning and improved coping of the other. Counselling texts list its marks. It is structured at first by the counsellor or therapist (time, place, roles, confidentiality, goals), but the structure is open to cooperative revision as the client's needs become clear. All the people involved perceive trust, caring, concern and commitment, and act on them. And it allows personal growth for everyone in it, the helper included, who learns and develops through the work. What it is not is a one-way service in which the helper takes over and fixes things. Doing things for clients breeds dependence and tells them they cannot cope, which defeats the aim of helping. Gerard Egan, in The Skilled Helper (1975), framed the goal as helping clients manage their own problems and develop unused resources. Example: Meera, a counsellor, could phone her client Ananya's college to sort out an attendance problem; instead she helps Ananya plan and rehearse the conversation, and Ananya makes the call herself, gaining confidence for next time. Option b is right because a helping relationship is collaborative and growth-focused, not one person doing something for another. Exam trap: b matches the everyday meaning of helping, which is why it slips past candidates in a NOT item; in counselling, helping means enabling the client, not acting on the client's behalf.", "traps": {"a": "This is true of a helping relationship: the counsellor sets the initial structure (roles, time, confidentiality) but reshapes it cooperatively as the client's needs emerge.", "c": "This is true: mutual trust, caring, concern and commitment, felt and acted on by everyone involved, are defining qualities of the relationship.", "d": "This is true: a helping relationship fosters growth for all involved, including the helper, in line with Carl Rogers's growth-centred view."}}, {"q": "A teacher's praise can be an effective motivator if:", "concept": "The answer is a: a teacher's praise motivates effectively when it is delivered contingently, that is, only after and because of the specific behaviour or achievement it is meant to strengthen. In operant terms (B. F. Skinner, 1953), praise is a social reinforcer, and a reinforcer strengthens behaviour only when it depends on that behaviour. Jere Brophy (1981), in a classic review of teacher praise, found that much classroom praise is ineffective because it is given randomly, globally or simply to manage the class. He listed the features of effective praise: it is delivered contingently, specifies what was done well, sounds sincere and spontaneous, rewards reaching a stated standard rather than mere participation, gives information about the student's competence, and orients students towards their own task-relevant behaviour and effort rather than comparing them with others. Later work by Claudia Mueller and Carol Dweck (1998) added that praising effort and strategy, rather than fixed ability, helps children persist after failure. Example: Ms Sharma tells Kabir, 'You checked every step of that long division, and that is why you got it right.' The praise comes right after the work, names the behaviour and links success to his effort, so Kabir starts checking his steps more often. Option a is right because contingency is the condition that makes praise work as a reinforcer. Exam trap: b borrows Brophy's phrase task-relevant behaviour but points it at the neighbour's work; effective praise directs students to their own behaviour, while comparison with peers is a mark of ineffective praise.", "traps": {"b": "Brophy's effective praise focuses students on their own task-relevant behaviour; directing attention to a neighbour invites social comparison, a feature of ineffective praise.", "c": "Praise just for showing up is noncontingent: it rewards mere attendance, not performance, so it loses both its reinforcing value and its credibility.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a states the established condition for effective praise."}}, {"q": "Which ONE of the following statements is true about using person-centred theory in brief approaches?", "concept": "The answer is a: even counsellors who use brief interventions have come to recognise that they need the person-centred core conditions in their practice. Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that therapeutic change needs a relationship in which the counsellor offers three core conditions: empathy (understanding the client's world from the inside), unconditional positive regard (warm, non-judgemental acceptance) and congruence (genuineness). Classic person-centred counselling is non-directive and open-ended, so it is not itself a brief therapy. But brief approaches, which aim to help within a few sessions, have borrowed its relational core. Solution-focused brief therapy (Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg, from the 1980s) depends on a respectful, collaborative stance; motivational interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, 1991) describes itself as a client-centred method; and research on common factors shows that the alliance predicts outcome whatever the length of therapy. Short-term work makes a strong relationship more important, not less, because there is no time to repair a bad start. Example: in a three-session college counselling service, Arjun is referred for exam stress. His counsellor uses a scaling question and sets one small goal, but she also listens empathically and accepts his fears without judgement; Arjun later says the acceptance was what made him try the plan. Option a is right because the core conditions have become a recognised ingredient of brief interventions. Exam trap: c leans on a true fact, that person-centred counselling avoids fixed techniques, to reach a false conclusion; the core conditions are attitudes that travel into any format, including brief work.", "traps": {"b": "The personal characteristics of counsellor and client do not rule out brief use; the core conditions are attitudes any counsellor can bring to short-term work.", "c": "Having no fixed set of techniques is a feature of person-centred counselling, but it does not stop its core conditions being used in brief therapy.", "d": "Person-centred counselling has no recognised single-session model of its own; single-session therapy (Moshe Talmon, 1990) is a separate approach, so d overstates the case."}}, {"q": "Transactional Analysis (TA) is related to:", "concept": "The answer is d: transactional analysis (TA) is related to personality. Eric Berne (1961), a Canadian-born American psychiatrist, set out TA in Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy and popularised it in Games People Play (1964). TA is both a theory of personality and a method of therapy. Its personality structure has three ego states, each a coherent pattern of feeling, thinking and behaving: the Parent (attitudes and rules taken over from parents, nurturing or critical), the Adult (realistic, here-and-now processing of information) and the Child (feelings and impulses carried from childhood, free or adapted). A transaction is a unit of social exchange between ego states: complementary transactions flow smoothly, crossed ones break communication, and ulterior ones carry hidden messages. Berne also described strokes (units of recognition), games (repeated exchanges with a hidden payoff) and life scripts (unconscious life plans formed in childhood). Thomas Harris (1967), in I'm OK, You're OK, spread the four life positions. Example: Riya's manager snaps, 'Why is this report late again?' (Critical Parent to Child). If Riya replies, 'It's always my fault' (Child), the transaction is complementary; if she replies calmly, 'The data came on Friday; I can finish by noon' (Adult), she moves it towards an Adult-to-Adult exchange. Option d is right because TA describes the structure of personality in ego states and uses it to analyse behaviour. Exam trap: TA analyses communication between people, so candidates sometimes link it to social topics such as attitudes or perception; but its ego states are a personality model, and TA is classified under personality and psychotherapy.", "traps": {"a": "Learning refers to conditioning and observational theories (Ivan Pavlov, B. F. Skinner, Albert Bandura); TA does not rest on a learning paradigm.", "b": "Attitudes are a social psychology topic (evaluations of people, objects and ideas, measured by Louis Thurstone's or Rensis Likert's scales); TA is not an attitude theory.", "c": "Perception is the organising and interpreting of sensory input (Gestalt laws, depth cues); TA's ego states describe personality, not perceptual processes."}}, {"q": "Each of the following answers (except for one) describes a theoretical assumption of REBT. Which ONE does NOT describe a theoretical assumption of REBT?", "concept": "The answer is d: the statement that other people can make us feel bad or good is not an assumption of rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT); it is the very idea REBT rejects. Albert Ellis (1955) founded the approach as rational therapy, later renamed it rational emotive therapy, and in 1993 rational emotive behaviour therapy. Its ABC model says that A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, and C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People usually think A causes C; Ellis held that B causes C. Healthy, rational beliefs are flexible preferences ('I would like her to approve of me'); unhealthy, irrational beliefs are rigid demands (musts and shoulds), awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of self or others. Therapy adds D, vigorous disputing of irrational beliefs, and E, an effective new philosophy. Ellis also insisted that thinking, feeling and behaving are interconnected, which is why behaviour joined the name and why he set homework such as shame-attacking exercises. He liked to quote the Stoic Epictetus: people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. Example: Kabir's friend ignores him at a party. If Kabir believes 'he must respect me, and it is awful that he did not', he feels furious; if he believes 'I'd prefer he greeted me, but perhaps he was distracted', he feels mildly disappointed. Same event, different beliefs, different feelings. Option d is right because REBT holds that we disturb ourselves through our beliefs, so others cannot directly make us feel bad or good. Exam trap: c and d are exact opposites; in a NOT item, the negative wording of c (others do not make us feel bad) is the true REBT assumption, and d is the false one.", "traps": {"a": "This is a true REBT assumption: beliefs (B) about events, rather than the events (A) themselves, cause emotional and behavioural consequences (C).", "b": "This is true of REBT: Albert Ellis stressed that thinking, feeling and behaving interact, which is why he added behaviour to the therapy's name in 1993.", "c": "This is true of REBT: other people do not make us feel bad or good; our beliefs about what they do create our feelings."}}, {"q": "Externalizing our unacceptable feelings and attributing them to others describes which Freudian defense mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is b: externalising our unacceptable feelings and attributing them to others is projection. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety caused by unacceptable impulses or painful realities. Sigmund Freud (1911) used projection to explain paranoia in his analysis of the Schreber case, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, placed it in the ego's catalogue of defences. In projection, a feeling or wish the person cannot accept in himself (hostility, envy, sexual desire, dishonesty) is pushed outward and seen in someone else. The person then feels threatened by, or critical of, the other person instead of facing the impulse within. Projection is common in everyday life and extreme in paranoid states; George Vaillant (1977) ranked it among the immature defences. The same idea underlies projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT, which assume that people read their own needs into ambiguous material. Example: Riya resents her new colleague and wishes her to fail, but she cannot admit such meanness to herself. Instead she becomes convinced that the colleague dislikes her and is plotting against her. Option b is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to another person is the definition of projection. Exam trap: displacement (c) also moves a feeling away from its source, so it is the near miss; but displacement redirects the feeling onto a safer target (shouting at a younger brother after a scolding from the boss) while still owning it, whereas projection disowns the feeling and places it in someone else.", "traps": {"a": "Denial is refusing to accept an external reality (a diagnosis, a loss); it blocks awareness of a fact rather than attributing one's own feelings to someone else.", "c": "Displacement redirects an impulse from its real target to a safer one (shouting at a sibling after a scolding from the boss); the feeling is still owned, only aimed elsewhere.", "d": "Reaction formation turns an unacceptable impulse into its exaggerated opposite (excessive sweetness towards someone one hates); the feeling is reversed, not attributed to others."}}, {"q": "Which method of calculating internal consistency reliability uses variance of the whole test for estimating coefficient?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Rulon and Flanagan formulas estimate split-half reliability directly from the variance of the whole test, without stepping up a half-test correlation. In split-half reliability, a test is given once and divided into two comparable halves, often odd versus even items. Correlating the two half-scores describes a test only half as long, so it underestimates the full test's reliability. Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910) corrected this with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula: r full = 2r half / (1 + r half). Two later formulas work with variances instead. Phillip Rulon (1939) treated the difference between each person's two half-scores as error: reliability = 1 minus (variance of the difference scores / variance of total scores). John Flanagan (1937) used the half variances: reliability = 2 x [1 minus (sum of the two half variances / whole-test variance)]. The two give the same answer, and neither assumes the halves have equal variances. Example: Meera splits a 40-item anxiety scale into odd and even halves. The total-score variance is 20, the difference-score variance is 4, and each half has variance 6. Rulon gives 1 minus 4/20 = .80; Flanagan gives 2 x (1 minus 12/20) = .80. Option b is right because these split-half formulas are the ones built on whole-test variance. Exam trap: Spearman-Brown (a) is the best-known split-half formula, but it uses the half-test correlation, not the whole-test variance. Key note: KR-20 (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) and coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) also have total-test variance in their denominators, so c and d are near misses; but the textbook line about computing reliability from the variance of the whole test, without the half-test correlation, refers to Rulon and Flanagan, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) steps up the correlation between two halves to full length; it works from that correlation, not from whole-test variance.", "c": "The Kuder-Richardson formulas (1937), KR-20 and KR-21, estimate consistency from item statistics (pq) for right-or-wrong items; they are not split-half formulas.", "d": "Coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) generalises KR-20 to items with any scoring, using the sum of item variances; it reflects all possible splits rather than the variances of one split."}}, {"q": "MMPI stands for:", "concept": "The answer is b: MMPI stands for Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built it at the University of Minnesota Hospitals to help diagnose psychiatric patients. Multiphasic means it measures many aspects of personality and psychopathology at once. It was made by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item's content. The original had 566 true-or-false statements (550 distinct, 16 repeated), ten clinical scales (such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia, Hypomania and Social Introversion) and the validity scales L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness). Revisions followed: the MMPI-2 (1989, 567 items), the MMPI-A for adolescents, the MMPI-2-RF (2008, 338 items) and the MMPI-3 (2020). In India, H. N. Murthy (1964) built a shorter Multiphasic Questionnaire on the same model. Option b is right because Minnesota, home of the university where it was made, is the first M in MMPI. Exam trap: d's Mini borrows from short screening tools such as the Mini-Mental State Examination, and a's Multidimensional sounds scientific, but the first M is Minnesota.", "traps": {"a": "Multidimensional is a plausible-sounding but wrong expansion; the first M is for Minnesota, where Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley built the test.", "c": "Multi Multiphasic Persona Inventory is a garbled distractor that repeats multi and swaps personality for persona.", "d": "Mini and Index echo short tools such as the Mini-Mental State Examination and the Mini-Mult short form; the MMPI is a long inventory, not an index."}}, {"q": "The assumption that individuals have unique patterns of ability that can be measured and matched with jobs is a key concept of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the idea that each person has a unique, measurable pattern of abilities that can be matched with the demands of a job is the core of trait and factor theory. Frank Parsons (1909), the father of vocational guidance, set out the approach in Choosing a Vocation. His three steps were: know yourself (your aptitudes, abilities, interests, resources and limits), know the world of work (the requirements, pay and prospects of different jobs), and match the two by 'true reasoning'. Traits are the measurable characteristics of the person; factors are the requirements of the job. E. G. Williamson (1939) turned this into the directive 'Minnesota' style of counselling in How to Counsel Students, in which the counsellor tests, diagnoses and advises. John Holland's (1959) theory of vocational personalities, later known as RIASEC, is a modern descendant that matches person and work environment on six types. Example: a school counsellor gives Kabir aptitude and interest tests, finds high spatial and mechanical scores, and suggests engineering or architecture because those job demands fit his profile. Option a is right because only trait and factor theory starts from stable, measurable individual differences and treats career choice as an act of matching them to jobs. Exam trap: examiners use Donald Super's ideas as the distractors. Super's theory is developmental (career choice unfolds across life stages) and treats an occupation as a way of implementing the self-concept, so 'self-concept theory' sounds close, but it describes how people see themselves over time, not test-based matching of abilities to jobs.", "traps": {"b": "Developmental theory is Donald Super's (1953) life-span approach, in which career choice unfolds through stages (growth, exploration, establishment, maintenance, decline). It stresses change over time, not a one-time match of measured abilities to jobs.", "c": "Self-concept theory is also Super's: people choose occupations that let them express and implement their picture of themselves. It centres on self-perception, not on measured ability patterns matched to job requirements.", "d": "Construct theory points to Mark Savickas's career construction theory (2005), in which people build careers by giving personal meaning to their experiences and telling their life story. It is narrative and subjective, the opposite of objective trait matching."}}, {"q": "Psychologists following the psychodynamic approach believe that in clinical work, objective tests are of little use because:", "concept": "The answer is c: psychodynamic clinicians think objective tests are of little use because people are not usually aware of the unconscious determinants of their behaviour. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) argued that the motives, wishes and conflicts that matter most are unconscious and are kept out of awareness by defences such as repression. An objective test, such as a self-report inventory like the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley (1943), asks direct questions ('I often feel tense') with fixed answers. It can only collect what the person consciously knows about himself and is willing to admit. If the real cause of a symptom is hidden, even honest answers miss it. This is why the psychodynamic tradition prefers projective techniques. Lawrence Frank (1939) named them 'projective methods': an ambiguous stimulus, such as a Rorschach inkblot or a TAT picture, lets the person project inner needs and conflicts without knowing what is being measured, so defences have less to guard against. Example: on a questionnaire Ananya reports a warm relationship with her father, yet her TAT stories keep showing daughters abandoned by cold fathers; a psychodynamic clinician would give more weight to the stories. Option c is right because the psychodynamic objection is about access: objective tests tap conscious self-report, while the theory places the causes of behaviour in the unconscious. Exam trap: option a sounds technical but is reversed. Objective tests are usually more reliable than projective tests; the psychodynamic complaint is about what they can reach, not about how consistent they are.", "traps": {"a": "Low reliability describes projective tests better than objective ones: inventories with fixed answers and standard scoring usually have high test-retest and internal consistency. Reliability is not the psychodynamic objection.", "b": "'It is difficult to be sure of them' is a vague phrase that names no real psychometric or theoretical problem. The psychodynamic argument is specific: the test cannot reach unconscious material.", "d": "Difficult interpretation is the usual criticism of projective tests, whose free responses need clinical judgement. Objective tests are scored by a key and compared with norms, so they are easy to interpret."}}, {"q": "Under certain conditions, Yates correction is applied in:", "concept": "The answer is d: Yates correction is applied in the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) built the chi-square test to compare observed frequencies (O) with the frequencies expected under the null hypothesis (E): chi-square is the sum of (O minus E) squared divided by E across all cells. The chi-square distribution is continuous, but counts move in whole numbers, so in small tables the formula overstates chi-square and gives too many false positives (Type I errors). Frank Yates (1934) proposed a correction for continuity: subtract 0.5 from each absolute difference between O and E before squaring. The 'certain conditions' are a 2 x 2 table (1 degree of freedom) in which expected frequencies are small, usually when any expected cell is below 5. Example: Arjun compares 12 treated and 10 untreated patients on 'improved or not improved'; two expected cells come to about 4, so he applies Yates correction, which lowers chi-square and makes the test more cautious. For very small tables, Ronald Fisher's exact test (described in The Design of Experiments, 1935) computes the exact probability instead. Option d is right because only the chi-square test approximates whole-number frequency counts with a continuous distribution, and the 0.5 adjustment corrects exactly that mismatch. Exam trap: all three distractors are non-parametric tests, so a student who links Yates with 'non-parametric' or 'small samples' may pick one of them. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman are rank tests; Yates correction belongs to frequency tables, specifically the 2 x 2 chi-square.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares the ranks of two independent groups on an ordinal or non-normal measure. It works with ranks, not cell frequencies, so it is not where Yates correction belongs.", "b": "Friedman's ANOVA (Milton Friedman, 1937) is a rank test for three or more related conditions, the non-parametric version of repeated measures ANOVA. It ranks scores within each person and has nothing to do with 2 x 2 frequency tables.", "c": "The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) is a rank test for three or more independent groups, the non-parametric one-way ANOVA. Yates correction is not part of it."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following methods is used by researchers to make the most exacting study of naturally occurring behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is d: GMCH's own key marks the field experiment, the method that brings an experiment's precision to behaviour in its everyday setting. A field experiment manipulates an independent variable in a real-world place (a street, a classroom, a hotel) instead of a laboratory, usually with people who do not know they are being studied. Because the researcher controls the independent variable and assigns conditions at random, it can show cause and effect, which is what makes it more exacting than watching alone. Because it happens in the field, the behaviour measured is the person's ordinary behaviour, so ecological validity stays high. Classic examples: Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969) staged a collapse on a New York subway train to study helping, and Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson (1968) told teachers that some pupils were about to bloom, then measured those pupils' gains. Example: to test whether a smile raises tips, a researcher has waiters in a real cafe smile broadly at randomly chosen tables and not at others, and compares the tips. Option d is right because it joins the control of an experiment to natural settings and natural behaviour. Exam trap: systematic observation (b) also studies naturally occurring behaviour with clear categories and trained observers, but it cannot show cause and effect, so it is precise description, not the most exacting test. Key note: this mock used to mark b, because observation leaves the behaviour untouched. GMCH's own key for this paper marks d. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH.", "traps": {"a": "Experimental research manipulates an independent variable under controlled, usually laboratory, conditions to establish cause and effect. Its control makes behaviour artificial, so it is not the study of naturally occurring behaviour.", "b": "Systematic observation records naturally occurring behaviour with operational definitions, coding schemes and inter-rater checks, as in Jane Goodall's chimpanzee studies at Gombe. But it manipulates nothing, so it can describe behaviour and show associations, not cause and effect; GMCH's key rates the field experiment as more exacting.", "c": "A survey collects self-reports through questionnaires or interviews from a large sample. It records what people say they do, which can differ from what they actually do, so it is not direct study of behaviour."}}, {"q": "The symbolic function substage is a part of the ______ in Piaget's theory of cognitive development.", "concept": "The answer is b: the symbolic function substage is the first part of the preoperational stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980). Piaget described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). Around the second birthday the symbolic function appears: the ability to let one thing (a word, a mental image, a gesture, a toy) stand for something else. It shows in rapid language growth, pretend play, early drawings and deferred imitation. Most texts split the preoperational stage into the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4) and the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7), when children ask endless 'why' questions and reason by hunch rather than logic. Thought in the symbolic substage is still limited by egocentrism (seen in Piaget's three mountains task) and animism (believing objects are alive). Example: three-year-old Meera holds a banana to her ear and 'talks to Nani' on her pretend phone; the banana is a symbol for the phone. Option b is right because the symbolic function is the new achievement that opens the preoperational stage, a stage named for what the child still cannot do: perform mental operations such as conservation. Exam trap: the sensorimotor stage ends with the beginnings of mental representation (substage 6, about 18 to 24 months), so students slide symbolic thought back into it. The substage actually named 'symbolic function', however, is the first part of the preoperational stage.", "traps": {"a": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to 2) has six substages, from reflexes to the beginnings of representation, and its key achievement is object permanence. It has no substage called symbolic function.", "c": "The concrete operational stage (7 to 11) brings logical operations on concrete objects: conservation, classification, seriation and reversibility. The symbolic function is already in place long before it.", "d": "The formal operational stage (11 onwards) brings abstract, hypothetical-deductive reasoning, seen in Piaget's pendulum problem. It comes years after the symbolic function substage."}}, {"q": "To assess the convergent validity of a new test of self-esteem, a researcher should establish that:", "concept": "The answer is a: convergent validity is shown when a new self-esteem test correlates highly with a different test of self-esteem. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) defined construct validity as evidence that a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) split that evidence into two parts in their multitrait-multimethod matrix. Convergent validity: measures of the same construct, even by different methods, should agree, that is, correlate highly. Discriminant (divergent) validity: measures of different constructs should not correlate too highly, or the new test is really measuring something else. A test needs both. Example: Arjun writes a new self-esteem questionnaire for college students. He gives it together with the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (Morris Rosenberg, 1965) to 200 students, and a correlation of about .70 supports convergent validity. He then checks that it correlates only modestly with a narcissism inventory, which supports discriminant validity, since healthy self-esteem and narcissistic grandiosity are related but not the same thing. Option a is right because 'convergent' means two measures converge on the same construct, and a strong correlation with another self-esteem test is exactly that evidence. Exam trap: option d is a true and useful finding, but it is discriminant validity, not convergent. Examiners pair the two terms because they come together in Campbell and Fiske's matrix. Remember 'same construct, high correlation' for convergent and 'different construct, low correlation' for discriminant.", "traps": {"b": "A low correlation with another self-esteem test would suggest the new test fails to measure self-esteem at all. Weak agreement with a same-construct measure is evidence against convergent validity, not for it.", "c": "A high correlation with narcissism, a different construct, would show poor discriminant validity: it suggests the new test is partly measuring narcissism instead of self-esteem.", "d": "This describes discriminant (divergent) validity, the companion of convergent validity in Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske's matrix. It supports construct validity, but it is not what the stem asks for."}}, {"q": "In a single-group pre-test and post-test design, which one of the following statistical methods can best be employed to evaluate the mean difference between the pre- and post-tests?", "concept": "The answer is d: of the options given, repeated measures ANOVA is the best way to test the mean difference between pre-test and post-test in a single group. In a single-group pre-test post-test design (the 'one-group pretest-posttest design' of Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, 1963), the same people are measured twice, so the two sets of scores are dependent: each person's post-test is linked to their own pre-test. A within-subjects analysis uses that link. It removes stable differences between people from the error term and tests only the change within each person, which gives more statistical power. Repeated measures ANOVA, an extension of Ronald Fisher's (1925) analysis of variance, handles two or more measurements of the same participants on an interval scale. With only two time points it gives the same result as a paired t-test, since F equals t squared. Example: a clinic rates Rohan and 29 other patients on an anxiety scale before and after eight weeks of CBT, and asks whether the mean fall is larger than chance. Option d is right because it is the only option that treats the pre and post scores as related measurements of the same people. Exam trap: Mann-Whitney U is a rank test for two independent groups, so using it here ignores that the same people were measured twice. The correct non-parametric test for paired scores would be the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945). Key note: a paired (dependent) t-test is the usual first choice for two measurements of one group, and it would be the answer if it were offered; since it is not, repeated measures ANOVA, its general form, is the best option, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares the ranks of two independent groups. Pre and post scores from the same people are not independent, so it suits a different design.", "b": "Randomised (completely randomised, between-groups) ANOVA compares separate groups of different people assigned at random. It treats every score as coming from a different person and so ignores the pairing in a one-group pre-post design.", "c": "Chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900) compares frequencies in categories, not mean scores. It cannot test the difference between two means from the same people."}}, {"q": "The unconscious feminine component of men is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the analytical psychology of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the anima is the unconscious feminine component of a man. Jung divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and a deeper collective unconscious shared by all humans. The collective unconscious holds archetypes: inherited, universal patterns that shape how we perceive and react, visible in myths, dreams and religions across cultures. The anima is the archetype of the feminine in a man, and the animus is the archetype of the masculine in a woman. Jung thought each person carries qualities of the other sex, shaped by the long human history of living with the opposite sex. The anima appears in dreams and in the image a man projects onto the women he is drawn to; a man who denies his anima may seem emotionally cut off. Example: Vikram keeps dreaming of a wise, gentle woman who guides him through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read her as his anima. Option c is right because anima is Jung's exact term for the feminine side of men. Exam trap: anima and animus are swapped on purpose. Remember that anima ends in 'a', like many feminine Latin words, and is the feminine within men; animus is the masculine within women.", "traps": {"a": "The animus is Jung's masculine archetype in women, the mirror image of the anima. The stem asks about men, so it is the reversed option.", "b": "The shadow is Jung's archetype of the dark, rejected, animal side of the personality that we deny in ourselves and project onto others. It is not specifically feminine.", "d": "Archetype is the general class of inherited images in the collective unconscious (persona, shadow, anima, animus, self). The anima is one archetype, so the general term is too broad."}}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is best trained to treat people with schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is b: clinical psychologists are the psychologists best trained to assess and treat severe mental disorders such as schizophrenia. Lightner Witmer (1896) opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania and gave the field its name. A clinical psychologist is trained in psychopathology, diagnosis, psychological assessment and psychotherapy, with long supervised work in hospitals and clinics. In India, registration as a clinical psychologist has traditionally required an M.Phil in Clinical Psychology recognised by the Rehabilitation Council of India, and the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 counts clinical psychologists among mental health professionals. In schizophrenia the psychiatrist usually prescribes antipsychotic medicine, while the clinical psychologist provides the psychological side: diagnostic assessment, cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis, family psychoeducation to lower expressed emotion, social skills training and cognitive remediation. Example: after Kabir's first psychotic episode, the psychiatrist starts him on medication, and the clinical psychologist runs family sessions with his parents and uses CBT to help him test the beliefs he holds about his voices. Option b is right because clinical psychology is the specialty whose training centres on serious psychopathology and its treatment. Exam trap: counselling psychology sounds close because it also involves therapy, but counselling psychologists mainly help people in the normal range with adjustment, career, relationship and stress problems, not with psychotic disorders.", "traps": {"a": "Counselling psychologists work mainly with people in the normal range facing adjustment, career, educational and relationship problems. Their training is less focused on psychosis and severe psychopathology.", "c": "Developmental psychologists study how people change across the lifespan (cognition, language, social growth). It is mainly a research field, not a treatment profession.", "d": "Psychometric psychologists (psychometricians) build, standardise and evaluate tests and scales. They measure traits and abilities but are not trained to treat patients."}}, {"q": "Freud's personality construct that contains the psychic energy to power all personality systems and is guided by the pleasure principle is the:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923, The Ego and the Id), the id contains the psychic energy that powers all the personality systems and is guided by the pleasure principle. The id is present at birth, wholly unconscious and made up of the instincts: Eros, the life instincts whose energy Freud called libido, and Thanatos, the death instinct added in 1920. It seeks immediate discharge of tension, which is the pleasure principle, and it thinks by primary process: wish-fulfilling images that ignore logic, time and reality, as in dreams. The ego grows out of the id to deal with the outside world. It follows the reality principle and secondary process (realistic thinking and planning), but it has no energy of its own and borrows it from the id. The superego, formed around age 5 to 6 as the Oedipus complex is resolved, holds the conscience and the ego ideal. Example: hungry baby Aditya screams at once, with no idea of waiting, because only his id is at work; years later his ego lets him wait for dinner and his superego stops him taking his sister's share. Option a is right because the id is both the reservoir of psychic energy and the system run by the pleasure principle. Exam trap: libido is the near miss. Libido is the energy itself (the energy of the life instincts), not a personality system; the stem asks for the structure that contains the energy, which is the id.", "traps": {"b": "The ego is the executive that mediates between id, superego and reality. It follows the reality principle, not the pleasure principle, and draws its energy from the id.", "c": "The superego is the internalised moral voice of parents and society (conscience and ego ideal). It works by the morality principle and often opposes the id's pleasure-seeking.", "d": "Libido is Freud's name for the energy of the life instincts, not one of the three personality systems. It is what the id contains, not the container."}}, {"q": "Shyness, seclusiveness and interpersonal difficulties are characteristic of which personality disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: shyness, seclusiveness and interpersonal difficulties are the classic description of schizoid personality disorder. The stem's wording comes from the early manuals: DSM-II (1968) described schizoid personality as 'shyness, oversensitivity, seclusiveness, avoidance of close or competitive relationships, and often eccentricity', and DSM-I (1952) drew a similar shy, seclusive, 'shut-in' picture, descended from the 'shut-in personality' of August Hoch (1910). In the modern DSM, schizoid personality disorder sits in Cluster A (odd or eccentric): a pervasive detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression. The person neither wants nor enjoys close relationships, almost always chooses solitary activities, takes pleasure in few things, has no close friends and seems indifferent to praise or criticism, coming across as cold and flat. Avoidant personality disorder, in Cluster C (anxious or fearful), looks similar from outside but has the opposite motive: the person longs for relationships but stays away because of feelings of inadequacy and fear of criticism or rejection. Example: Ishaan eats alone and has no friends. If he is content that way and unmoved by others, the pattern is schizoid; if he aches to join the group but fears being laughed at, it is avoidant. Option c is right because seclusiveness and withdrawal from relationships are the defining schizoid pattern in the wording the stem uses. Exam trap: modern readers see 'shyness' and jump to avoidant. Ask why the person is alone: lack of desire is schizoid, fear of rejection is avoidant. Key note: DSM-III (1980), influenced by Theodore Millon (1969), split avoidant personality off from the old schizoid category, so under DSM-5 a stem stressing fear of rejection would point to avoidant. With the old schizoid wording centred on seclusiveness, c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) is excessive emotionality and attention-seeking, often with dramatic or seductive behaviour. Such people seek the spotlight, the opposite of seclusiveness.", "b": "Avoidant personality disorder (Cluster C) is social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation in someone who wants relationships. It was carved out of the schizoid category only in DSM-III (1980), and the stem's older seclusive wording describes schizoid.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder (Cluster B) is instability of relationships, self-image and mood, with impulsivity, fear of abandonment and self-harm. Its relationships are intense and stormy, not withdrawn."}}, {"q": "Which is a behavioural therapy that has proven successful in treating phobias?", "concept": "The answer is d: systematic desensitisation is the behavioural therapy with a proven record in treating phobias. Joseph Wolpe (1958, Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition) developed it from animal work on conditioned fear. The logic is counterconditioning: a phobia is a learned fear response, and a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment (reciprocal inhibition). Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy: about 10 to 15 situations ranked from mildly to intensely frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDs, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each scene, starting at the bottom, and moves up only when the current scene no longer causes anxiety. In vivo versions use real situations instead. Peter Lang and David Lazovik (1963) showed in a controlled study that it reduced snake phobia in students. Example: Priya, terrified of dogs, first pictures a photo of a puppy, then a dog across the street, then patting a calm dog, relaxing at each step until the fear fades. Option d is right because it is a learning-based technique designed specifically for phobic anxiety and supported by controlled research. Exam trap: all the distractors are real treatments, so students pick by familiarity. Ask which one is behavioural: ECT and psychosurgery are biological treatments, and group therapy is a format, not a specific behavioural technique.", "traps": {"a": "Electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938) passes a brief electric current through the brain to induce a seizure. It is a biological treatment used mainly for severe depression, not a behavioural therapy for phobias.", "b": "Group therapy is a format in which a therapist treats several clients together; Irvin Yalom (1970) described its therapeutic factors. Many approaches can be run in groups, so it is not itself a behavioural technique for phobias.", "c": "Psychosurgery, such as the prefrontal leucotomy of António Egas Moniz (1935), destroys or disconnects brain tissue to change behaviour. It is a biological, now rarely used treatment, not a behavioural one."}}, {"q": "The amplification method is used in:", "concept": "The answer is d: amplification is a method of dream interpretation from the analytical psychology of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961). Sigmund Freud (1900) used free association: the patient starts from a dream element and lets thoughts run away from it along a chain of associations until they reach the hidden wish. Jung thought this led away from the dream itself. He saw dreams as direct, creative messages from the unconscious, not disguises, so he stayed with the dream image and enlarged it. Amplification has two layers. Personal amplification gathers the dreamer's own associations to the image, circling back to it again and again. Collective (objective) amplification then adds parallels from myth, religion, fairy tales, folklore and alchemy, to show the archetype the image expresses. Example: Meera dreams of a snake shedding its skin. Her own associations bring up her recent move away from home; the analyst adds that the snake is a symbol of renewal in many myths, and reads the dream as a message about her own transformation. Option d is right because amplification is a technique for interpreting dream images, Jung's alternative to Freud's free association. Exam trap: the word 'amplification' sounds as if it belongs to physiology or emotion (amplifying arousal, amplifying signals in a lie detector), and the distractors exploit that. In psychology exams the term belongs to Jung's dream work, alongside active imagination.", "traps": {"a": "Motivation is the study of what drives and directs behaviour (needs, drives, incentives, goals). Amplification is not a motivational concept or method.", "b": "Silvan Tomkins (1962) did describe affects as amplifiers of drives, so the word appears in emotion theory, but that is a theory of what emotions do, not a method. The method called amplification is Jung's way of interpreting dreams.", "c": "A lie detector (polygraph) records heart rate, breathing and skin conductance during questioning. Its machine amplifies body signals, but the psychological method called amplification has nothing to do with lie detection."}}, {"q": "Out of the following options, which one includes all of the others?", "concept": "The answer is b: the peripheral nervous system includes all the others. The nervous system has two main parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is every nerve outside them: 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves. The PNS divides into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensation from skin, muscles and joints and sends motor commands to skeletal (voluntary) muscles, and the autonomic nervous system, which controls the heart, glands and smooth muscle without conscious effort. The autonomic system divides again into the sympathetic division (fight or flight: faster heart, dilated pupils, slowed digestion) and the parasympathetic division (rest and digest: slower heart, more saliva, active digestion). So the hierarchy runs PNS, then somatic and autonomic, then sympathetic and parasympathetic inside the autonomic. Example: when Neha picks up her pen, her somatic system moves her fingers; when she sees her exam result and her heart races, her sympathetic division is at work; when she relaxes afterwards, the parasympathetic division takes over. Option b is right because the somatic, autonomic and parasympathetic systems are all subdivisions of the peripheral nervous system. Exam trap: the autonomic system also contains one of the other options (the parasympathetic), so students stop one level too low; it does not contain the somatic system, so only the PNS includes all three.", "traps": {"a": "The autonomic nervous system is one branch of the PNS. It contains the parasympathetic division but not the somatic system, so it does not include all the others.", "c": "The somatic nervous system is the other branch of the PNS, serving voluntary movement and body sensation. It includes neither the autonomic nor the parasympathetic system.", "d": "The parasympathetic nervous system is the lowest level here, a division of the autonomic system that calms the body after arousal. It contains none of the other options."}}, {"q": "Which of these stimulates a muscle to contract?", "concept": "The answer is d: motor neurons stimulate muscles to contract. Neurons are classed by function. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry signals from receptors to the central nervous system; interneurons connect neurons inside the brain and spinal cord; motor (efferent) neurons carry commands out from the CNS to effectors, the muscles and glands. A motor neuron's cell body lies in the spinal cord or brainstem, and its axon runs out to a muscle. Where it meets a muscle fibre, at the neuromuscular junction, it releases acetylcholine, which depolarises the fibre and triggers contraction. Charles Sherrington (1906) called the motor neuron the 'final common path', because every reflex and every voluntary command must pass through it to move a muscle. When motor neurons die, as in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis or polio, muscles weaken and waste even though sensation stays intact. Example: Riya touches a hot pan; receptors and sensory neurons send the signal to her spinal cord, interneurons relay it, and motor neurons make her arm muscles pull her hand away, the classic reflex arc. Option d is right because only motor neurons carry the signal that directly makes skeletal muscle contract. Exam trap: 'sensory neurons' is the reversed option. Sensory neurons carry information in towards the CNS; motor neurons carry commands out to the muscles.", "traps": {"a": "Adrenal hormones (adrenaline and cortisol) travel in the blood and prepare the body for stress by raising heart rate and blood sugar. They change the body's readiness but do not directly trigger skeletal muscle contraction; motor neurons do.", "b": "Receptors are the detector cells of the sense organs (or the binding proteins on neurons) that pick up stimuli. They begin the reflex arc; they do not send commands to muscles.", "c": "Sensory neurons are afferent: they carry signals from receptors towards the brain and spinal cord. They bring information in, not commands out to muscles."}}, {"q": "The part of the brain most closely associated with maintaining balance and the coordination of complex sequences of movements is the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the cerebellum is the part of the brain most closely tied to balance and the coordination of complex sequences of movement. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the hindbrain, behind the pons, and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It compares the movement the cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the vestibular (balance) organs of the inner ear, and corrects errors moment by moment, so movements come out smooth, accurate and well timed. It also stores motor skills learned by practice, such as cycling or typing. Damage causes ataxia: a wide, staggering gait, poor balance, slurred speech and an intention tremor that appears as the hand nears a target. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask drivers to walk a straight line or touch their nose. Example: Meera, a Bharatanatyam dancer, can link dozens of steps and hand gestures without thinking because her cerebellum has learned the whole sequence. Option b is right because balance and the coordination of skilled movement sequences are the cerebellum's defining job. Exam trap: the pons and medulla sit right next to the cerebellum in the hindbrain, so students mix them up. The medulla runs vital functions and the pons relays signals and helps regulate sleep; only the cerebellum coordinates movement and balance.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus, below the thalamus, regulates homeostasis: hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary gland's hormones. It does not coordinate movement.", "c": "The pons ('bridge') links the cortex and cerebellum with the lower brainstem and helps regulate sleep, arousal and breathing. It relays motor signals but does not do the coordinating.", "d": "The medulla oblongata controls vital reflexes such as breathing, heart rate, blood pressure, swallowing and vomiting. Damage there threatens life, but it is not the balance centre."}}, {"q": "Loss of the ability of the brain to produce adequate levels of dopamine often leads to:", "concept": "The answer is c: loss of the brain's ability to produce enough dopamine leads to Parkinson's disease. James Parkinson (1817) first described the condition as the 'shaking palsy' in An Essay on the Shaking Palsy. The cause is the death of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra of the midbrain. These neurons project to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen) along the nigrostriatal pathway, part of the basal ganglia circuit that starts and smooths movement. Symptoms usually appear only after most of these neurons have been lost. The four motor signs are a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness and difficulty starting movement) and postural instability; patients also show a shuffling gait and a mask-like face, and many develop depression and, later, dementia. Treatment replaces the missing chemical: L-dopa, a precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier and is turned into dopamine, came into use in the 1960s. Example: Sana's grandfather has a hand that trembles at rest but steadies when he reaches for his tea, and he walks with small shuffling steps; his neurologist prescribes levodopa. Option c is right because Parkinson's disease is the classic disorder of dopamine depletion. Exam trap: Alzheimer's disease is the other famous neurodegenerative disorder, but it is linked to loss of acetylcholine (with plaques and tangles), not dopamine. Note also that too much dopamine activity, not too little, is linked to schizophrenia and mania.", "traps": {"a": "Aphasia is loss of language after damage to language areas such as Broca's area (Paul Broca, 1861) or Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874). It follows stroke or injury, not dopamine depletion.", "b": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is a dementia marked by amyloid plaques, neurofibrillary tangles and loss of acetylcholine neurons. Memory, not movement, fails first.", "d": "Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder of manic and depressive episodes. Mania is linked to excess rather than loss of dopamine activity, and lithium (John Cade, 1949) is its classic treatment."}}, {"q": "A relatively enduring behavioural change brought about by experience is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: a relatively enduring change in behaviour brought about by experience is the definition of learning. The standard wording descends from Gregory Kimble (1961): learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour, or in the potential for behaviour, that results from experience or practice. Each part of the definition does work. 'Relatively permanent' rules out short-lived changes caused by fatigue, drugs, illness or a passing mood. 'Experience' rules out maturation, which is change on a biological timetable, and instinct. 'Potential' matters because learning is inferred from performance: a student may have learned something but not show it until she is tested. The main kinds are classical conditioning, operant conditioning, observational learning and insight learning. Example: after burning her hand once on a hot iron, Riya always checks the iron before touching it; one experience produced a lasting change. Option a is right because only learning combines both parts of the stem: the change lasts, and it comes from experience. Exam trap: habituation is a real but very simple form of learning (a fading response to a repeated, harmless stimulus, like no longer noticing the hum of a ceiling fan), so 'all of the above' tempts. But habituation is only one narrow type and often short-lived, and growth is a biological increase in size driven by maturation, not experience, so 'all of the above' fails. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks d, all of the above. That key is wrong: growth is a biological increase in size driven by maturation, not by experience, so it cannot be learning, and a stays.", "traps": {"b": "Habituation is a decline in response to a repeated, harmless stimulus. It is the simplest kind of learning, not the general term, and it is often short-lived, so it does not define a relatively enduring change.", "c": "Growth is a physical increase in size and capacity driven by maturation and nutrition. It changes behaviour without depending on experience, which is exactly what the definition of learning excludes.", "d": "All of the above fails because growth is not caused by experience and habituation is only one narrow type of learning, not the name for all enduring change from experience."}}, {"q": "The process of learning:", "concept": "The answer is d: learning improves adjustment, improves efficiency and is continuous, so all three statements describe it. Educational psychology texts list the characteristics of learning in just these terms. Learning improves adjustment: it lets a person cope with new demands from the environment, from a child learning to share toys to an adult learning new software at work. Learning improves efficiency: practice cuts errors and time, so the task is done faster and with less effort. Edward Thorndike (1898) showed this with cats in puzzle boxes, whose escape times fell steadily over trials, giving some of the earliest animal learning curves. Learning is continuous: it goes on throughout life, from infancy to old age, is not confined to school, and each new piece builds on what came before. Other standard characteristics are that learning is a change in behaviour, is purposive or goal-directed, results from experience and can transfer to new situations. Example: when Arjun moves from Patna to Chennai for college, he learns Tamil phrases and the bus routes (adjustment), gets quicker at the commute each week (efficiency) and keeps picking up new skills all through his years there (continuity). Option d is right because a, b and c are each standard characteristics of learning, and none contradicts the others. Exam trap: each of a, b and c is true on its own, so a hasty student picks the first true statement and moves on. When every option reads as a true property of the concept, check whether 'all of the above' is offered before choosing.", "traps": {"a": "Improving adjustment is a true characteristic of learning, since learned skills help people cope with their surroundings. It is incomplete on its own because b and c are also true.", "b": "Improving efficiency is also true: practice reduces errors and time, as Edward Thorndike's (1898) falling learning curves showed. It is only one of three correct statements.", "c": "Being continuous (lifelong) is also a standard property of learning. Choosing it alone ignores that a and b are equally true."}}, {"q": "The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) is published by the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association. The manual began as DSM-I in 1952 and has been revised as DSM-II (1968), DSM-III (1980), DSM-III-R (1987), DSM-IV (1994), DSM-IV-TR (2000), DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022). DSM-III was the turning point: led by Robert Spitzer (1980), it introduced explicit diagnostic criteria and a multiaxial system, which DSM-5 later dropped. The DSM is the main classification in the United States and in much research. Many countries, including India, also use the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10, now ICD-11) for official records. Example: a psychiatrist in Delhi may code Meera's diagnosis by ICD for hospital records but discuss the DSM-5-TR criteria at a case conference. Option b is right because the psychiatric, not the psychological, association writes and publishes the DSM. Exam trap: both bodies are abbreviated APA. The American Psychological Association publishes the psychologists' ethics code and the Publication Manual (APA referencing style), not the DSM. Remember 'DSM: psychiatric, for diagnosis'.", "traps": {"a": "The American Psychological Association is the professional body of psychologists; it publishes the Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct and the APA Publication Manual. It shares the APA abbreviation but does not publish the DSM.", "c": "The World Health Organization publishes the International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10 mental disorders chapter, 1992; ICD-11, in force from 2022). It is the DSM's sister system, not its publisher.", "d": "The Research Diagnostic Criteria (Robert Spitzer, Jean Endicott and Eli Robins, 1978) were a set of research criteria that fed into DSM-III. They are a document, not an organisation, and should not be confused with the later Research Domain Criteria (RDoC) of the US National Institute of Mental Health."}}, {"q": "Under certain conditions, Yates correction is applied in:", "concept": "The answer is d: Yates correction is applied in the chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) built the chi-square test to compare observed frequencies (O) with the frequencies expected under the null hypothesis (E): chi-square is the sum of (O minus E) squared divided by E across all cells. The chi-square distribution is continuous, but counts move in whole numbers, so in small tables the formula overstates chi-square and gives too many false positives (Type I errors). Frank Yates (1934) proposed a correction for continuity: subtract 0.5 from each absolute difference between O and E before squaring. The 'certain conditions' are a 2 x 2 table (1 degree of freedom) in which expected frequencies are small, usually when any expected cell is below 5. Example: Arjun compares 12 treated and 10 untreated patients on 'improved or not improved'; two expected cells come to about 4, so he applies Yates correction, which lowers chi-square and makes the test more cautious. For very small tables, Ronald Fisher's exact test (described in The Design of Experiments, 1935) computes the exact probability instead. Option d is right because only the chi-square test approximates whole-number frequency counts with a continuous distribution, and the 0.5 adjustment corrects exactly that mismatch. Exam trap: all three distractors are non-parametric tests, so a student who links Yates with 'non-parametric' or 'small samples' may pick one of them. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis and Friedman are rank tests; Yates correction belongs to frequency tables, specifically the 2 x 2 chi-square.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares the ranks of two independent groups on an ordinal or non-normal measure. It works with ranks, not cell frequencies, so it is not where Yates correction belongs.", "b": "Friedman's ANOVA (Milton Friedman, 1937) is a rank test for three or more related conditions, the non-parametric version of repeated measures ANOVA. It ranks scores within each person and has nothing to do with 2 x 2 frequency tables.", "c": "The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) is a rank test for three or more independent groups, the non-parametric one-way ANOVA. Yates correction is not part of it."}}, {"q": "'Individual psychology' is a term used specifically to refer to the psychological method or science founded by:", "concept": "The answer is b: individual psychology is the psychological method and science founded by Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937). Adler was an early member of Sigmund Freud's circle in Vienna but broke away in 1911, rejecting Freud's view that sexual drives are the main force in personality, and named his new school individual psychology. 'Individual' comes from the Latin individuum, 'indivisible': Adler saw each person as an indivisible whole moving purposefully towards goals, not a battleground of id, ego and superego. Its key ideas are feelings of inferiority (growing out of his 1907 work on organ inferiority) and compensation for them, striving for superiority or perfection, fictional finalism (guiding goals that are useful fictions), the style of life, social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), birth order and early recollections. Therapy is an educational partnership that uncovers the client's mistaken goals and encourages social interest. Example: Kabir, a younger brother who always felt small beside his brilliant elder brother, becomes a fiercely competitive athlete; an Adlerian would see compensation for felt inferiority shaping his style of life. Option b is right because Adler himself coined 'individual psychology' as the name of his system. Exam trap: Carl Jung also left Freud, around 1913, and founded his own school, so students mix up the names. Jung's school is analytical psychology, Adler's is individual psychology and Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious and archetypes. He also broke with Freud, which is why his name is the near miss.", "c": "Hoffman most likely refers to Edward Hoffman, who wrote a biography of Adler (The Drive for Self, 1994). He wrote about individual psychology but did not found it.", "d": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, built on unconscious sexual and aggressive drives and the id, ego and superego. Adler left Freud's circle precisely to set up individual psychology."}}, {"q": "In Pavlov's original experiment, meat was the:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the original experiments of Ivan Pavlov (1927, Conditioned Reflexes), meat powder was the unconditioned stimulus. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, won the Nobel Prize in 1904 for his work on digestion. While collecting dogs' saliva through a tube in the cheek, he noticed that the dogs began to salivate before the food arrived, at the sight of the attendant or the sound of footsteps. He called this 'psychic secretion' and set out to study it systematically. The terms are as follows. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS), meat powder, naturally triggers the unconditioned response (UCR), salivation, without any learning. A neutral stimulus, such as a metronome or tone, is presented just before the meat. After repeated pairings the neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation on its own, now called the conditioned response (CR). Example: Arjun's dog drools when its food is served (UCS to UCR), and after weeks of hearing the steel bowl clink first, drools at the clink alone (CS to CR). Option a is right because meat produces salivation automatically, without prior training, which is the definition of an unconditioned stimulus. Exam trap: salivation appears on both sides, so students confuse stimulus with response. Meat and the metronome are stimuli; salivation is the response, unconditioned when caused by meat and conditioned when caused by the metronome.", "traps": {"b": "The conditioned stimulus is the originally neutral signal, such as a metronome or tone, that gains the power to trigger salivation after pairing with meat. Meat needed no pairing to do this.", "c": "The unconditioned response is the dog's natural salivation to the meat. It is something the dog does, not the stimulus the experimenter presents.", "d": "The conditioned response is the learned salivation to the metronome or tone alone. Meat is a stimulus, not a learned response."}}, {"q": "A mature defence mechanism is:", "concept": "The answer is c: humour is a mature defence mechanism. George Vaillant (1977, Adaptation to Life) ranked defences in a four-level hierarchy, using the Grant Study, which has followed a group of Harvard men since 1938. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial and distortion. Level II, immature: projection, fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation (including isolation of affect), repression, displacement, reaction formation and dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, humour, sublimation, suppression and anticipation. Mature defences handle painful feelings while keeping them in awareness and without harming oneself or others. Humour lets a person say openly what hurts in a way that eases tension for everyone; it differs from wit used to wound, which is closer to passive aggression. Vaillant found that men who relied on mature defences had better marriages, careers and physical and mental health, and that defences tended to mature with age. Example: after failing her driving test, Meera jokes to her family, 'At least the traffic cones will remember me,' and books another lesson; she faces the disappointment while keeping her spirits and her relationships intact. Option c is right because humour sits in Vaillant's mature level. Exam trap: isolation sounds calm and controlled, so students take it for mature. Isolation of affect (keeping an idea but stripping away its feeling) is a neurotic defence in Vaillant's scheme.", "traps": {"a": "Denial, refusing to accept a painful outer reality, is psychotic in Vaillant's scheme when it denies reality outright, and is otherwise treated as immature. It distorts reality instead of coping with it.", "b": "Isolation (isolation of affect) keeps a painful idea in mind while cutting off its feeling. Vaillant places it with intellectualisation among the neurotic defences, a level below mature.", "d": "Projection, attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to others, is an immature defence in Vaillant's hierarchy, and delusional projection is psychotic. It is common in paranoid thinking and prejudice."}}, {"q": "In classical conditioning, the _______ becomes capable of eliciting a response previously triggered by the unconditioned stimulus.", "concept": "The answer is b: in classical conditioning, the conditioned stimulus becomes capable of eliciting a response that was first triggered only by the unconditioned stimulus. Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed the process in dogs. An unconditioned stimulus (UCS), such as food, naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR), salivation. A neutral stimulus, such as a tone, at first produces nothing beyond an orienting 'what is that?' reaction. When the tone is repeatedly presented just before the food (acquisition), it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation by itself, the conditioned response (CR). Timing matters: conditioning is strongest when the CS comes shortly before the UCS (delay or forward conditioning) and weak when it comes after (backward conditioning). Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that the CS must also predict the UCS, not merely occur alongside it. Example: Riya once got food poisoning after eating prawns at a wedding, and now the mere smell of prawns makes her feel sick. The smell has become a conditioned stimulus that triggers the nausea first caused by the illness. Option b is right because the whole point of classical conditioning is that a once-neutral stimulus takes on the eliciting power of the UCS, and that stimulus is, by definition, the CS. Exam trap: options c and d are operant terms from the work of B. F. Skinner (1938). Reinforcers follow a behaviour and change how often it is made; in classical conditioning the stimulus comes before the response and elicits it.", "traps": {"a": "An aversive stimulus is any unpleasant event (shock, loud noise) used in punishment, avoidance learning or aversion therapy. It can serve as a UCS, but it is not the name for the stimulus that acquires eliciting power.", "c": "A secondary (conditioned) reinforcer, such as money or praise, gains its value through association with primary reinforcers. It is an operant term for a consequence that strengthens a response, not the classical CS.", "d": "A primary reinforcer, such as food or water, satisfies a biological need and strengthens operant behaviour without prior learning. In classical conditioning food would be the UCS, not the stimulus that becomes capable of eliciting the response."}}, {"q": "The forces that govern the initiation and persistence of behavior are called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the forces that start (initiate), direct and keep up (persist) behaviour are called motives. A motive is an inner state, a need or a desire, that energises behaviour and points it towards a goal. Textbooks describe motivation by three features: activation (starting the behaviour), direction (choosing one goal over others) and persistence (keeping at it despite obstacles), with intensity sometimes added as a fourth. Motives are inferred, not seen: we watch what a person does, how hard and for how long, and infer the motive behind it. They fall into two broad families. Primary (biological) motives such as hunger, thirst and sleep arise from bodily needs; secondary (learned or social) motives such as achievement, power and affiliation are acquired through experience. Theories differ on where motives come from: instinct theory (William McDougall, 1908), drive reduction theory (Clark Hull, 1943), arousal theory and incentive theory. Example: Arjun wakes at 5 a.m. to practise for the state cricket trials and keeps training through a hot summer even after a poor match. His wish to be selected is the motive: it started the behaviour, set its direction and kept it going. Option b is right because the initiation and persistence of goal-directed behaviour is the textbook definition of motivation, and motives are the forces that produce it. Exam trap: emotions (a) also energise behaviour, and the two words share the Latin root movere (to move), so examiners use emotion as the near miss; but emotions are felt reactions to events, while motives are the push towards goals.", "traps": {"a": "Emotions are short-lived felt states with bodily arousal and expressive behaviour (fear, joy, anger); they can trigger action, but the term for the forces that start and sustain goal-directed behaviour is motives.", "c": "Intellectual capacities are abilities such as intelligence and reasoning; they set what a person can do, not what makes them start or keep doing it, since ability and motivation are separate factors in performance.", "d": "Personality attributes are stable traits (such as conscientiousness) that describe how a person typically behaves across situations; they colour behaviour but are not the energising, goal-directed forces the stem defines."}}, {"q": "'Archetype' is a term associated with:", "concept": "The answer is a: 'archetype' is a term of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the Swiss psychiatrist who founded analytical psychology after his break with Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) around 1913. Jung divided the psyche into three layers: the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed material, organised into emotionally charged clusters called complexes) and the collective unconscious, a deeper layer inherited by all humans from our ancestral past. The contents of the collective unconscious are the archetypes: inborn, universal patterns or predispositions to perceive and respond in certain ways. We never see an archetype directly; it shows itself in recurring images in myths, religions, dreams and folk tales across cultures. The main archetypes are the persona (the social mask we show others), the shadow (the dark, rejected side of the personality), the anima (the feminine side in a man) and animus (the masculine side in a woman), and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala). Others include the wise old man, the great mother and the hero. Example: Meera dreams of an old sage guiding her through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read the sage as the wise old man archetype, the same figure found in stories of rishis and wizards the world over. Option a is right because the archetypes and the collective unconscious are Jung's signature concepts. Exam trap: examiners pair Jung with Freud (b) because both were psychoanalysts who spoke of the unconscious; but Freud's unconscious is personal (repressed wishes), and only Jung added an inherited, collective layer filled with archetypes.", "traps": {"b": "Sigmund Freud's key terms are the id, ego and superego, the psychosexual stages and the repressed personal unconscious; he did not propose inherited archetypes.", "c": "Alfred Adler's individual psychology uses inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, style of life and social interest, not archetypes.", "d": "B. F. Skinner was a radical behaviourist (operant conditioning, reinforcement, the Skinner box) who set aside inner mental structures altogether, so archetypes have no place in his work."}}, {"q": "A disorder of neural development characterised by impaired social interaction and communication, and by restricted and repetitive behaviour, is:", "concept": "The answer is d: a disorder of neural development marked by impaired social interaction, impaired communication and restricted, repetitive behaviour is a pervasive developmental disorder (PDD), the older name for what is now autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Lorna Wing and Judith Gould (1979) called these three features the triad of impairments. Leo Kanner (1943) first described infantile autism in eleven children with extreme aloneness and an anxious insistence on sameness, and Hans Asperger (1944) described more verbally able children. DSM-IV (1994) and ICD-10 (1992) grouped these conditions as pervasive developmental disorders: autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, Rett's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and PDD not otherwise specified. 'Pervasive' meant the problems touch almost every area of the child's life. DSM-5 (2013) merged most of them into a single autism spectrum disorder with two symptom domains: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, present from early childhood. Example: four-year-old Kabir avoids eye contact, does not point to share interest, lines up his toy cars in the same order every day and screams if the order is changed. This is autism, a pervasive developmental disorder. Option d is right because the stem lists exactly the defining triad of autism, and PDD is the diagnostic family autism belonged to. Exam trap: intellectual disability (a) and the genetic syndromes (b and c) often occur alongside autism and so tempt, but they are defined by low intelligence and physical features, not by the social, communication and repetitive-behaviour triad. Older papers still say PDD where DSM-5 says autism spectrum disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Mental retardation (now intellectual disability) is defined by deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning beginning in the developmental period, not by the social and repetitive-behaviour triad, though many autistic children also have it.", "b": "Down syndrome is a chromosomal condition (trisomy 21, described by John Langdon Down in 1866) with typical facial features and intellectual disability; social interest is often relatively good, so it does not match the stem.", "c": "Cat's cry (cri du chat) syndrome is a deletion on the short arm of chromosome 5 (Jerome Lejeune, 1963) causing a high cat-like cry, a small head and severe intellectual disability; it is a genetic syndrome, not the autism triad."}}, {"q": "An individual who is fighting to climb the career ladder is striving to satisfy his:", "concept": "The answer is b: GMCH's own key marks personal motives. Some textbooks sort motives into three groups instead of two. Biological (physiological or primary) motives such as hunger, thirst, sleep and sex arise from bodily needs and keep the body in balance (homeostasis). Social motives such as affiliation, approval and power are learned from living with other people. Personal motives are a person's own learned goals, interests, habits and level of aspiration, the standard of future performance a person sets for himself or herself (a concept from Kurt Lewin's research group). Climbing the career ladder is the person's own aspiration, so in that scheme it is a personal motive. In the two-way scheme the same striving is a secondary (learned, acquired) motive, and David McClelland (The Achieving Society, 1961) would call it the need for achievement (nAch), measured by scoring stories told to Thematic Apperception Test pictures. Example: Riya already earns enough for food and a home, yet she takes a management course and applies for every promotion; no bodily need drives this, her own level of aspiration does. Option b is right because the paper uses the three-way scheme, where career ambition is a personal motive. Exam trap: primary (a) and biological (c) are the same family named twice, so they fail together. Key note: this mock used to mark d, secondary motives, the name in the two-way primary versus secondary scheme, which also describes this person. GMCH's own key for this paper marks b. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows GMCH; if a paper offers secondary motives but not personal motives, pick secondary.", "traps": {"a": "Primary motives are the unlearned, biological drives (hunger, thirst, sleep) based on bodily needs and homeostasis; a promotion at work satisfies no bodily need.", "c": "Biological motives is simply another name for primary motives (physiological drives such as hunger and thirst), so it fails for the same reason as a.", "d": "Secondary motives is the two-way scheme's name for all learned motives, and career striving does fit it. GMCH's key prefers the narrower three-way term, personal motives, for an individual's own aspirations, so d is the runner-up here."}}, {"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, the S-O-R concept was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Robert S. Woodworth (1869 to 1962) of Columbia University rejected the plain stimulus-response (S-R) formula and proposed S-O-R, stimulus-organism-response. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Woodworth argued that the same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism (O): its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, in which inner states stand between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth was a functionalist and is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot samosas (S) makes hungry Kabir reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), his state of hunger. Option a is right because the S-O-R concept is Woodworth's, introduced to correct the S-R connectionist view. Exam trap: Edward B. Titchener (1867 to 1927) (c) tempts because he is another early founder working in America, but he was a structuralist who analysed conscious experience into elements by introspection, not a critic of S-R bonds; and Thorndike is the connectionist the stem rejects, not the reformer.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology (collective unconscious, archetypes, introversion and extraversion); he worked on the psyche, not on reforming the S-R formula of learning.", "c": "Edward B. Titchener founded structuralism at Cornell, breaking consciousness into sensations, images and feelings by trained introspection; he had nothing to do with S-O-R.", "d": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis (unconscious conflict, id, ego and superego); his model of behaviour is driven by instincts, not framed as stimulus, organism and response."}}, {"q": "Which term was not part of Pavlov's theory of classical conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is d: of the four terms, reinforcement is the best pick for the one not part of Ivan Pavlov's (1927) theory of classical conditioning, because textbooks teach it as the central term of operant conditioning. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, explained conditioning through processes in the cerebral cortex. Excitation is the active state of a cortical centre that produces a response; when a conditioned stimulus (a bell) is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (food), a link of excitation forms, so the bell alone brings salivation. Inhibition is the opposite active process that suppresses a response: external inhibition (a novel stimulus disrupts the response), internal inhibition (as in extinction, when the bell keeps coming without food) and conditioned inhibition. Irradiation is the spread of excitation or inhibition across the cortex from its starting point, Pavlov's explanation of stimulus generalisation; concentration is its later narrowing back to the point of origin, which underlies discrimination. Reinforcement in the sense of a consequence that strengthens the response that produced it comes from Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) operant conditioning. Example: Meera's dog salivates at the pressure cooker whistle because the whistle always comes before dinner; any similar whistle now sets it off too, which Pavlov would explain by irradiation. Option d is right because excitation, inhibition and irradiation are Pavlov's cortical constructs, while reinforcement is the signature term of operant learning. Exam trap: irradiation (b) sounds like an odd, non-psychological word, so students often pick it; it is in fact Pavlov's own term for the spread of excitation. Key note: GMCH's own key for this paper marks b, irradiation. That key is wrong: irradiation is Pavlov's own term, and his Conditioned Reflexes (1927) devotes lectures to the irradiation and concentration of excitation and inhibition in the cortex. Strictly, Pavlov also used 'reinforcement' for pairing the conditioned stimulus with the unconditioned stimulus, and the word entered English through that 1927 translation, so no option is perfectly clean. d stays because reinforcement is the term textbooks file under operant conditioning (Thorndike, Skinner), while irradiation belongs to Pavlov alone.", "traps": {"a": "Excitation is Pavlov's term for the active cortical process that produces a conditioned response; it is one of the two basic processes in his theory.", "b": "Irradiation is Pavlov's term for the spread of excitation or inhibition across the cortex, his explanation of stimulus generalisation; it sounds unusual but is genuinely his.", "c": "Inhibition (external, internal or extinctive, and conditioned) is Pavlov's active suppressing process, the partner of excitation, and it explains extinction and discrimination."}}, {"q": "The presence of two mutually opposing forces at the same time, in which the stronger inhibits the weaker, is referred to as the:", "concept": "The answer is d: when two mutually opposing responses are present at the same time and the stronger one inhibits the weaker, this is the principle of reciprocal inhibition. The idea began in physiology. Charles Sherrington (1906) showed that when one muscle of an opposing pair contracts (the biceps), the nervous system automatically inhibits the other (the triceps), so a limb can move smoothly. Joseph Wolpe (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958) carried the principle into behaviour therapy. He argued that if a response incompatible with anxiety can be made to occur in the presence of anxiety-provoking stimuli, and it is strong enough to suppress the anxiety wholly or partly, the bond between those stimuli and anxiety is weakened. Deep muscle relaxation, assertive responses and sexual responses are all incompatible with anxiety. This is the basis of systematic desensitisation: the deeply relaxed client imagines the steps of a fear hierarchy, from least to most frightening, and at each step relaxation outweighs the small dose of anxiety. Example: Arjun fears dogs. Relaxed in the therapist's chair, he first imagines a picture of a puppy, then a dog across the road, and so on; because relaxation is stronger at every step, the fear weakens. Option d is right because the stem gives the definition of reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible states together, the stronger blocking the weaker. Exam trap: reactive inhibition (c) also contains 'inhibition', but it is Clark Hull's (1943) fatigue-like tendency not to repeat a response just made; reciprocal determinism (b) shares the word 'reciprocal' but is Albert Bandura's (1978) model of person, behaviour and environment influencing one another.", "traps": {"a": "'Principle of posterity' is not a recognised principle in learning or therapy; it is a made-up distractor.", "b": "Reciprocal determinism is Albert Bandura's view that personal factors, behaviour and environment all influence one another; it is about mutual causation, not one response suppressing another.", "c": "Reactive inhibition is Clark Hull's fatigue-like tendency, built up by responding, not to repeat a response; it fades with rest and involves no opposing response."}}, {"q": "A unique pattern of personal behaviours and characteristics by which each of us strives for perfection is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: in the individual psychology of Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), the unique pattern of behaviours, traits and habits through which each person strives for perfection (superiority) is the style of life. Adler, who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911, held that everyone begins life feeling small and inferior, and that the master motive is striving for superiority, later called striving for perfection or completion: the push from a felt minus towards a plus. Each child works out an individual way of pursuing this goal, formed largely in the first 4 to 5 years from family experiences, birth order and the way inferiority was handled. That consistent pattern is the style of life (lifestyle), and it shows in how the person meets the three tasks of life: work, friendship and love. Adler's related terms are easily confused. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal ('if I become a doctor, everyone will respect me') that guides behaviour, an idea he took from Hans Vaihinger's (1911) philosophy of 'as if'. The creative self is the power each person has to shape their own style of life, rather than being moulded by heredity and environment alone. Example: Kabir, a sickly child teased for being weak, strives to excel at study, chooses friends who admire his intelligence and avoids sport; this whole consistent pattern is his style of life. Option d is right because the stem describes an individual pattern of striving, which is exactly the style of life. Exam trap: striving for superiority (b) echoes the stem's wording ('strives for perfection'), but it is the universal motive itself; the style of life is each person's unique way of pursuing it.", "traps": {"a": "The creative self is Adler's term for the person's active power to build their own personality; it is the force that creates the style of life, not the pattern itself.", "b": "Striving for superiority (perfection) is Adler's universal master motive, shared by everyone; the stem asks for the unique pattern by which each person pursues it.", "c": "Fictional finalism is Adler's term, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger, for the imagined future goals that guide behaviour; it is the goal, not the pattern of behaviours used to reach it."}}, {"q": "Which psychological theory best explains the persistence of phobias in the absence of continuous exposure to the feared object?", "concept": "The answer is b: the persistence of a phobia, even when the person is rarely exposed to the feared object, is best explained by operant conditioning, through negative reinforcement of avoidance. O. Hobart Mowrer (1947) set this out in his two-factor theory. Factor one is classical conditioning: a neutral object is paired with a frightening event and comes to trigger fear, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's (1920) Little Albert study. Classical conditioning alone, however, predicts that the fear should extinguish when the object keeps appearing without anything bad happening. Factor two explains why it does not. Each time the person avoids or escapes the object, their fear drops, and this relief negatively reinforces the avoidance (operant conditioning). Because the person always avoids, they never stay long enough to learn that the object is harmless, so extinction never takes place and the phobia can last for years. This is why exposure therapies, which block avoidance, work. Example: Meera was bitten by a dog at 7. Now, at 20, she crosses the road whenever she sees a dog; each crossing brings instant relief, so the habit grows stronger, and she never learns that most dogs are friendly. Option b is right because the stem asks about maintenance, and a phobia is maintained by avoidance being rewarded with fear reduction. Exam trap: classical conditioning (a) is the near miss because it explains how the phobia is first acquired; persistence is the operant half of Mowrer's theory.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov; John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's Little Albert) explains how fear of the object is first learned, but on its own it predicts extinction, not persistence.", "c": "Social learning theory (Albert Bandura) explains how a fear can be acquired by watching another person's fear (modelling), not why an already learned phobia keeps going.", "d": "Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957) is the discomfort of holding inconsistent beliefs or acting against one's attitudes; it concerns attitude change, not the maintenance of fear."}}, {"q": "In a study on conformity, participants often agreed with a clearly incorrect majority. Which of the following best explains this behavior?", "concept": "The answer is b: when participants agree with a clearly incorrect majority, the best explanation is normative social influence, conforming in order to be liked and accepted and to avoid rejection. Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955) separated two reasons for conformity. Informational social influence is conforming because we think others know better, so we privately accept their view; it is strongest when the situation is ambiguous. Normative social influence is going along with the group to fit in, often while privately disagreeing (public compliance). Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) showed this with a line judgement task. A real participant sat with a group of confederates, and on critical trials every confederate gave the same obviously wrong answer about which of three lines matched a standard line. Alone, people made almost no errors, yet with the group about 75 per cent conformed at least once, and about a third of all critical answers were wrong. Afterwards many said they knew the answer was wrong but did not want to stand out. When a single ally gave the right answer, conformity fell sharply. Example: in a college quiz team, Riya knows the answer is Delhi, but when all four teammates confidently say Mumbai, she nods along to avoid looking foolish. Option b is right because the answer was clearly wrong, so the group offered no useful information; the pressure came from the wish to be accepted. Exam trap: informational influence (a) is the near miss; it belongs to Muzafer Sherif's (1935) autokinetic study, where a still point of light in a dark room seemed to move, so people had no clear answer and used the group as a source of information.", "traps": {"a": "Informational social influence is conforming because others seem to know better, as in Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic study; with an obviously wrong answer, the group offers no real information.", "c": "Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957) is the tension of holding inconsistent cognitions, reduced by changing attitudes; it explains attitude change after behaviour, not yielding to a majority.", "d": "Groupthink (Irving Janis, 1972) is faulty decision making in a cohesive group that values agreement over critical thinking, as in the Bay of Pigs invasion; it concerns group decisions, not one person yielding in a perception task."}}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is primarily associated with the reward system and addictive behaviors?", "concept": "The answer is b: dopamine is the neurotransmitter most closely tied to the brain's reward system and to addictive behaviour. The core reward circuit is the mesolimbic dopamine pathway: dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the midbrain send their axons to the nucleus accumbens in the ventral striatum, with links to the prefrontal cortex. James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) found that rats would press a lever again and again to receive electrical stimulation of this system, the first evidence for 'pleasure centres'. Natural rewards such as food, sex and social contact release dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, and almost all addictive drugs raise it, directly or indirectly: cocaine blocks dopamine reuptake, amphetamine releases it, and nicotine, alcohol and opioids increase the firing of dopamine neurons. Wolfram Schultz (1997) showed that dopamine neurons signal reward prediction error, firing more when a reward is better than expected, which teaches the brain what to seek. Repeated drug use changes this system so that drug cues trigger intense craving ('wanting'), the incentive-sensitisation theory of Terry Robinson and Kent Berridge (1993). Example: Arjun's phone pings with a new like on his post; the unpredictable reward triggers a dopamine burst, and he checks the phone again and again. Option b is right because dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway is the common final path of natural reward and drug addiction. Exam trap: serotonin (a) is the usual near miss because it is famous for mood and depression; it modulates mood and impulse control, but reward and craving belong to dopamine.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin (5-HT), made in the raphe nuclei, regulates mood, sleep, appetite and impulse control and is the target of SSRIs for depression; it is not the main reward chemical.", "c": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, enhanced by alcohol and benzodiazepines to calm neural activity; some drugs act on it, but the reward signal itself is dopamine.", "d": "Glutamate is the brain's main excitatory neurotransmitter, central to learning and long-term potentiation; it supports drug-related learning but is not the classic reward transmitter."}}, {"q": "A researcher finds that stress is negatively correlated with academic performance. What does this mean?", "concept": "The answer is a: a negative correlation between stress and academic performance means that as stress increases, academic performance tends to decrease (and as stress falls, performance tends to rise). A correlation describes how two variables vary together. It is usually measured with Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment coefficient, r, which runs from minus 1 to plus 1. The sign shows the direction. Positive means the two variables rise and fall together (height and weight); negative means they move in opposite directions (hours of television and exam marks). The size, ignoring the sign, shows the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and values near 1 or minus 1 mean the points lie close to a straight line. So r = minus 0.70 is a stronger relationship than r = plus 0.30. Most important, correlation does not imply causation: stress might lower marks, poor marks might raise stress, or a third variable such as poor sleep or money worries might drive both. Only an experiment can show cause. Example: a survey of 200 college students finds r = minus 0.45 between stress scores and marks. Riya, with high stress, is likely (not certain) to have lower marks than Kabir, with low stress. Option a is right because 'negative' describes the direction of the relationship: the two variables move in opposite directions. Exam trap: option b turns a correlation into a cause, the classic error examiners test; option c describes a positive correlation, and option d confuses 'negative' with 'zero'.", "traps": {"b": "'Stress causes poor performance' is a causal claim; a correlation alone cannot show cause, because the direction could be reversed or a third variable could explain both.", "c": "Both variables increasing together describes a positive correlation, the opposite of what a negative correlation means.", "d": "No relationship is a zero (or near zero) correlation; 'negative' refers to the direction of a real relationship, not its absence."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of a secondary reinforcer in operant conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is c: money is a secondary reinforcer. In operant conditioning, a reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely to happen again (B. F. Skinner, 1938). Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy biological needs and work without any learning: food, water, warmth, sleep and relief from pain. Secondary (conditioned) reinforcers are stimuli that were once neutral and became rewarding because they were paired with primary reinforcers. Praise, grades, tokens, medals and the clicker sound in dog training are all examples. Money is the classic case: a paper note means nothing to a baby, but it gains its power because it can be exchanged for food, shelter and many other rewards. A secondary reinforcer linked with many primary ones, as money is, is called a generalised reinforcer, and it keeps working whatever need the person has at the moment. The token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) uses tokens as secondary reinforcers that patients exchange for privileges. Example: Meera's parents give her a sticker each day she finishes her homework, and ten stickers buy a trip for ice cream. The stickers start out meaningless, but because they lead to the treat they come to reinforce homework on their own. Option c is right because money is valuable only through learning, by its link with primary rewards. Exam trap: food, water and sleep (a, b, d) are all primary reinforcers, so the examiner is testing which reward had to be learned; sleep may seem an odd reinforcer, but it meets a biological need.", "traps": {"a": "Food is a primary (unconditioned) reinforcer: it satisfies hunger and works without any prior learning.", "b": "Water is a primary reinforcer: it meets the biological need of thirst and is effective from birth.", "d": "Sleep is a primary reinforcer because it satisfies a biological need; it does not have to be paired with anything to be rewarding."}}, {"q": "People attributing their own unwanted impulses and feelings to someone else is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: attributing one's own unwanted impulses and feelings to someone else is projection, an ego defence mechanism. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) described defence mechanisms as unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety caused by unacceptable id impulses or harsh superego demands, and his daughter Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) set them out systematically. In projection, a thought or feeling that would cause guilt if owned, such as hostility, jealousy or sexual attraction, is pushed out and seen in another person instead. 'I hate him' becomes 'He hates me'. Because the impulse now seems to sit outside, the person can criticise it in others without facing it in themselves. Projection is common in paranoid thinking, and George Vaillant (1977) placed it among the immature defences. Projective tests such as the Rorschach and the Thematic Apperception Test rest on a related idea: people read their own needs into ambiguous material. Example: Arjun feels attracted to a colleague but believes this is wrong; instead he keeps telling friends that the colleague is always flirting with him. Option c is right because the stem defines projection exactly: one's own unacceptable feeling is attributed to another person. Exam trap: displacement (b) also moves a feeling, but it redirects a feeling the person still owns onto a safer target (shouting at a younger brother after being scolded by the boss), whereas projection changes who seems to have the feeling.", "traps": {"a": "'Rejection' is not a classical defence mechanism; it is an everyday word for refusing or turning away from someone, used here as a distractor.", "b": "Displacement redirects an impulse the person still owns from a threatening target to a safer one, such as kicking a door instead of hitting the boss; the feeling is not assigned to someone else.", "d": "Denial is refusing to accept a painful external reality, such as a heavy smoker insisting there is no health risk; it rejects a fact rather than relocating one's own impulse onto another person."}}, {"q": "Which research method is best suited to determine cause-and-effect relationships?", "concept": "The answer is b: the experimental study is the research method best suited to determining cause and effect. A true experiment has three defining features. First, the researcher manipulates the independent variable (IV), deliberately creating different conditions rather than just observing what happens naturally. Second, participants are randomly assigned to conditions, which spreads other differences (age, ability, mood) evenly, so the groups are equal at the start. Third, the researcher controls extraneous variables, keeping everything except the IV the same. If the groups then differ on the measured dependent variable (DV), the difference can be put down to the IV; this is high internal validity. The other methods lack manipulation and random assignment. They can show that two things go together, describe change or explore a rare case, but they cannot rule out third variables or settle the direction of cause, hence the rule that correlation does not imply causation. Example: to test whether sleep affects memory, a researcher randomly assigns 60 students to sleep 8 hours or 4 hours, then gives everyone the same word list. If the 8-hour group, which includes students like Kabir, recalls more, sleep can be said to cause better recall, because random assignment balanced everything else. Option b is right because only an experiment combines manipulation, random assignment and control, the conditions needed to infer cause. Exam trap: the longitudinal study (c) sounds powerful because it follows the same people over years and shows which came first, but without manipulation a third variable could still explain the link; time order alone does not prove cause.", "traps": {"a": "A correlational study measures how two variables vary together without manipulating either, so it cannot rule out third variables or show the direction of cause.", "c": "A longitudinal study follows the same people over time and shows developmental change and time order, but because nothing is manipulated it remains non-experimental.", "d": "A case study is an in-depth account of one person or a small group, such as Phineas Gage; it is rich and useful for generating hypotheses, but lacks control and generalisability."}}, {"q": "Which personality trait is most associated with openness to new experiences and intellectual curiosity?", "concept": "The answer is c: openness to experience is the Big Five trait most associated with curiosity, imagination and a willingness to try new things. The Big Five (five-factor model) grew out of the lexical approach: if a trait matters, languages will have words for it. Factor analyses of trait words by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961), Warren Norman (1963) and Lewis Goldberg (1981) kept finding five broad dimensions, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae built the NEO Personality Inventory (1985; NEO PI-R, 1992) to measure them. The acronym OCEAN lists them: Openness (imaginative, curious, artistic, open to ideas and values, versus conventional and practical), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable, self-disciplined), Extraversion (sociable, energetic, assertive), Agreeableness (warm, trusting, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious, moody, easily upset, versus emotionally stable). Costa and McCrae's six facets of openness are fantasy, aesthetics, feelings, actions, ideas and values. Openness is the Big Five trait most linked with creativity, and it correlates modestly with measured intelligence. Example: Riya reads about astronomy for fun, is learning Japanese, tries new cuisines and enjoys debating unusual ideas; she would score high on openness. Option c is right because intellectual curiosity and openness to new experiences are the defining features of this factor. Exam trap: extraversion (a) is the usual near miss because extraverts seek excitement and new social experiences, but extraversion is about sociability and positive energy; enjoying new ideas, art and intellectual exploration belongs to openness.", "traps": {"a": "Extraversion is sociability, assertiveness and positive emotion (the E factor of both Hans Eysenck's model and the Big Five); extraverts like stimulation, but the trait is not defined by intellectual curiosity.", "b": "Agreeableness is warmth, trust, kindness and cooperativeness versus antagonism; it describes how a person treats others, not curiosity about ideas.", "d": "Conscientiousness is organisation, dependability, self-discipline and achievement striving; it predicts school and job performance, but it is about self-control, not novelty."}}, {"q": "Which type of reinforcement schedule is most resistant to extinction?", "concept": "The answer is d: the variable ratio schedule is the most resistant to extinction. Extinction is the weakening of a learned response once reinforcement stops, and resistance to extinction is how long the response keeps going. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (Schedules of Reinforcement, 1957) mapped the effects of different schedules. Continuous reinforcement rewards every response and extinguishes fastest. Partial (intermittent) schedules reward only some responses and make behaviour far more durable, the partial reinforcement extinction effect (Lloyd Humphreys, 1939). The four basic partial schedules come from two choices: ratio (reward depends on the number of responses) or interval (reward depends on time passed), and fixed or variable. Fixed ratio (every 10th response) gives a high rate with a pause after each reward. Fixed interval (the first response after 5 minutes) gives a scallop: little responding just after a reward, faster responding as the time nears. Variable interval (after unpredictable times) gives a moderate, steady rate. Variable ratio (after an unpredictable number of responses around an average) gives the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance to extinction, because the next response might always be the winning one, so long runs without reward feel normal. Example: Kabir plays a mobile game that pays coins after an unpredictable number of spins; even after the coins stop entirely, he keeps spinning for days. Slot machines and lotteries work the same way. Option d is right because an unpredictable reward tied to the person's own responding makes the end of reinforcement hardest to notice. Exam trap: variable interval (c) is the near miss because it is also unpredictable and also resistant, but its rate is only moderate and it comes second; the fixed schedules (a, b) extinguish faster because the switch to no reward is easy to detect.", "traps": {"a": "Fixed interval reinforces the first response after a set time (a weekly salary, a scheduled test) and gives a scalloped pattern; it is predictable, so the end of reward is noticed and extinction comes quickly.", "b": "Fixed ratio reinforces after a set number of responses (piece-rate pay) and gives high rates with pauses after each reward; because it is predictable, it extinguishes faster than the variable schedules.", "c": "Variable interval reinforces the first response after unpredictable stretches of time (checking for messages); it is resistant to extinction but gives only a moderate rate and ranks second to variable ratio."}}, {"q": "When someone attributes their successes to internal factors and their failures to external factors, they are exhibiting:", "concept": "The answer is a: taking credit for successes (internal causes) and blaming failures on outside factors (external causes) is the self-serving bias. Attribution theory, begun by Fritz Heider (1958), studies how people explain the causes of behaviour, either internally (ability, effort, personality) or externally (luck, task difficulty, other people). The self-serving bias, reviewed by Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975), is a motivated error: it protects and enhances self-esteem. After a good result we say 'I worked hard' or 'I am talented'; after a bad one, 'the paper was unfair' or 'the umpire was biased'. It shows up in students, athletes, managers and couples. It tends to be weaker in collectivist cultures that value modesty, and people with depression often show the reverse pattern, blaming themselves for failure. Example: when Arjun scores 92 in mathematics he says he is good at the subject, but when he scores 48 in physics he blames the teacher's confusing questions. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly this split: internal causes for success, external causes for failure. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (b) and the actor-observer bias (c) are also attribution biases, but they concern explaining other people's behaviour by their dispositions, or one's own behaviour by the situation and others' by their traits, whatever the outcome; only the self-serving bias depends on whether the outcome was a success or a failure.", "traps": {"b": "The fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational ones when explaining other people's behaviour; it is not about one's own successes and failures.", "c": "The actor-observer bias (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) is explaining one's own behaviour by the situation but other people's by their traits; it does not split successes from failures.", "d": "The just-world hypothesis (Melvin Lerner) is the belief that people get what they deserve, which leads to blaming victims; it is about explaining others' fate, not one's own outcomes."}}, {"q": "A parent who reinforces a child's behaviour every time it occurs is using which type of reinforcement schedule?", "concept": "The answer is a: reinforcing a behaviour every time it occurs is continuous reinforcement (CRF), sometimes written as a fixed ratio 1 schedule. A schedule of reinforcement is the rule that decides which responses are rewarded. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (Schedules of Reinforcement, 1957) divided schedules into continuous, where every correct response is reinforced, and intermittent (partial), where only some are. Intermittent schedules come in four basic forms: fixed ratio, variable ratio, fixed interval and variable interval. Continuous reinforcement is the fastest way to establish a new behaviour, because the link between the act and the reward is clear and immediate, so it is ideal for shaping and early learning. Its weakness is that behaviour learned this way extinguishes quickly once rewards stop, because the change is easy to notice. Intermittent reinforcement builds behaviour more slowly, but the behaviour lasts much longer (the partial reinforcement extinction effect). The practical rule is to start with continuous reinforcement, then thin it to an intermittent schedule. Example: each time three-year-old Meera puts her toys back in the box, her mother praises her and gives her a hug. Meera learns the habit quickly; later her mother praises her only now and then, which keeps the habit going for longer. Option a is right because 'every time it occurs' is the definition of continuous reinforcement. Exam trap: fixed ratio (b) tempts because CRF can technically be written as FR 1, but in exam usage fixed ratio means a reward after a set number of responses greater than one; intermittent (d) is the opposite of the stem, the umbrella term for reinforcing only some responses.", "traps": {"b": "Fixed ratio reinforces after a set number of responses (every 5th correct sum); continuous reinforcement is technically FR 1, but in exam usage fixed ratio means rewarding fewer than all responses.", "c": "Variable interval reinforces the first response after unpredictable periods of time, as with a parent who praises tidying now and then at random moments; it does not reward every occurrence.", "d": "Intermittent (partial) reinforcement is the umbrella term for rewarding only some responses, covering the four ratio and interval schedules; it is the opposite of reinforcing every time."}}, {"q": "Which type of memory is primarily affected in individuals with Alzheimer's disease?", "concept": "The answer is c: episodic memory, memory for personal events tied to a time and place, is the system primarily affected in Alzheimer's disease, especially memory for recent events. Endel Tulving (1972) divided declarative (explicit) long-term memory into episodic memory (what happened, where and when: yesterday's lunch, last week's wedding) and semantic memory (general facts and word meanings: Delhi is the capital of India). Non-declarative (implicit) memory includes procedural memory, the skills and habits shown by doing, such as cycling. Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), is a degenerative dementia marked by amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles. The damage usually begins in the medial temporal lobe, in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus, the structures that form new episodic memories. So the earliest signs are forgetting recent events and conversations, repeating questions and misplacing things, while memories from long ago are relatively preserved at first. As the disease spreads into the temporal and parietal neocortex, semantic memory (word finding, naming, general knowledge) declines too, followed by language, visuospatial skills and judgement. Procedural memory, which depends more on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, is spared until late. Example: Kabir's grandmother cannot remember that her daughter visited this morning and asks the same question five times, yet she still knits and sings old bhajans perfectly. Option c is right because hippocampal damage first and most severely impairs the recording of new personal episodes. Exam trap: semantic memory (b) also declines in Alzheimer's disease and is the near miss, but later and less sharply; procedural memory (a) is spared until late, and its early loss points instead to Parkinson's or Huntington's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Procedural memory (skills and habits such as cycling or knitting) depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum and is preserved until late in Alzheimer's disease; it is impaired early in Parkinson's and Huntington's diseases.", "b": "Semantic memory (facts, word meanings) does decline in Alzheimer's disease as damage spreads into the temporal neocortex, shown in word-finding problems, but later and less sharply than episodic memory; it is the main early loss in semantic dementia.", "d": "Working memory (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974) holds and manipulates information for seconds and depends on the prefrontal cortex; it is affected in Alzheimer's disease, but it is not the primary early deficit."}}, {"q": "An individual who constantly worries about health despite multiple reassurances from doctors may be exhibiting:", "concept": "The answer is c: a person who constantly worries about having or getting a serious illness, despite repeated medical reassurance, may have illness anxiety disorder. DSM-5 (2013) replaced the older diagnosis of hypochondriasis with two disorders. Somatic symptom disorder is for people with distressing bodily symptoms plus excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviour about them. Illness anxiety disorder is for people whose main problem is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, with no bodily symptoms or only mild ones. Its DSM-5 features are: preoccupation with having a serious illness; somatic symptoms absent or mild; high anxiety about health and easy alarm about personal health status; excessive health-related behaviour (checking the body, searching symptoms online) or maladaptive avoidance (avoiding doctors and hospitals); and a duration of at least 6 months. It has a care-seeking type and a care-avoidant type. Normal test results and doctors' reassurance bring only brief relief, and the fear soon returns or shifts to another illness. ICD-11 keeps the name hypochondriasis and places it with the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Example: Riya has had three normal ECGs and two normal blood reports, yet every small chest twinge convinces her she has heart disease, and she searches her symptoms online every night. Option c is right because health worry that persists despite reassurance is the defining picture of illness anxiety disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder (b) also involves constant worry, but GAD worry spreads across many everyday areas (work, family, money), while illness anxiety fixes on one theme, having a serious disease; panic disorder (a) can include a fear of dying, but that fear is tied to the attacks themselves.", "traps": {"a": "Panic disorder is recurrent unexpected panic attacks followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks or their consequences; the health fear is tied to the attacks, not a lasting belief in a hidden disease.", "b": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters for at least 6 months; its worry is broad, not fixed on having a serious illness.", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive, unwanted obsessions with compulsions performed to reduce anxiety (washing, checking); health fears can occur, but repeatedly seeking medical reassurance about one disease is the picture of illness anxiety."}}, {"q": "The bystander effect can best be explained by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the bystander effect is best explained by diffusion of responsibility, the sense that when many people are present, each one is less personally responsible for helping. The bystander effect is the finding that the more people witness an emergency, the less likely any one of them is to help, and the slower help comes. It was prompted by the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, where newspapers reported that 38 neighbours watched and none called the police (a figure later shown to be exaggerated). John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968) staged an emergency: students talking over an intercom heard another participant apparently having a seizure. When students believed they were the only listener, about 85 per cent went to help before the seizure ended; when they believed four others could also hear, only about 31 per cent did. Each person assumed someone else would act. Their later decision model (The Unresponsive Bystander, 1970) lists five steps: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, assume responsibility, know how to help, and decide to help. Diffusion of responsibility blocks the third step; pluralistic ignorance, where everyone looks calm so no one reads the event as an emergency, blocks the second. Example: on a crowded Mumbai platform a man collapses; Meera sees dozens of people nearby and assumes one of them, perhaps a doctor, will help, so she walks on. Option a is right because spreading responsibility across many witnesses is the core explanation Darley and Latané gave. Exam trap: social loafing (c) also involves responsibility spread across a group, but it is reduced effort on a shared task, not failure to help in an emergency.", "traps": {"b": "Increased empathy raises helping, as in C. Daniel Batson's (1991) empathy-altruism hypothesis; it cannot explain why more witnesses lead to less help.", "c": "Social loafing (Bibb Latané and colleagues, 1979) is people putting in less effort on a group task when individual output cannot be identified; it resembles the bystander effect but concerns effort, not emergency helping.", "d": "Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957) is the discomfort of inconsistent beliefs or actions; it might explain justifying a failure to help afterwards, but not the drop in helping as the crowd grows."}}, {"q": "A psychologist who studies how people learn, remember and think would be classified as a:", "concept": "The answer is c: a psychologist who studies how people learn, remember and think is a cognitive psychologist. Cognitive psychology is the study of internal mental processes: attention, perception, memory, language, thinking, problem solving, decision making and learning. It treats the mind as an information processor that takes in, stores, transforms and retrieves information, rather like a computer. The field grew out of the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, when work such as George Miller's (1956) 'magical number seven' paper on short-term memory and Noam Chomsky's (1959) critique of B. F. Skinner's account of language showed that behaviourism could not explain mental life. Ulric Neisser named and defined the field in his book Cognitive Psychology (1967) and is often called its father. Cognitive psychologists mostly use laboratory experiments, reaction times and memory tests, and today brain imaging as well (cognitive neuroscience). Example: Kabir runs an experiment in which students learn word lists with or without a night's sleep and are tested the next day; because he is studying how memories are stored, he is working as a cognitive psychologist. Option c is right because learning, remembering and thinking are the core mental processes that define cognitive psychology. Exam trap: behavioural psychologist (d) is the near miss because behaviourists also study learning, but they study observable responses and their conditioning and deliberately set aside inner processes such as thinking and remembering; clinical psychologists (a) assess and treat mental disorders, and social psychologists (b) study how people influence and relate to one another.", "traps": {"a": "A clinical psychologist assesses, diagnoses and treats psychological disorders (Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic in 1896); the stem describes basic mental processes, not mental illness.", "b": "A social psychologist studies how people's thoughts, feelings and behaviour are shaped by others (conformity, attitudes, prejudice); thinking is studied only in its social context.", "d": "A behavioural psychologist, in the tradition of John B. Watson and B. F. Skinner, studies observable behaviour and conditioning; learning is covered, but remembering and thinking as inner processes are what behaviourism set aside."}}, {"q": "According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which of the following needs must be satisfied first?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Abraham Maslow's (1943) hierarchy of needs, safety needs are the lowest of the four levels offered, so they must be satisfied first. Maslow, a founder of humanistic psychology, arranged human needs in a pyramid of five levels, from bottom to top: physiological needs (food, water, sleep, air), safety needs (security, stability, protection, freedom from fear), love and belongingness needs (friendship, family, intimacy, acceptance), esteem needs (achievement, competence, respect from others and self-respect) and self-actualisation (realising one's full potential). The lower needs are prepotent: when they are unmet they dominate behaviour, and a higher need becomes the main motivator only once the lower ones are reasonably satisfied. The bottom four are deficiency needs (D-needs), felt when something is missing; self-actualisation is a growth or being need (B-need). In Motivation and Personality (1954) Maslow noted that the order is not rigid, and some people put ideals or creativity ahead of comfort. Example: Arjun has just moved to a new city for work. Once he has food and a roof, his first worry is a safe neighbourhood and a stable job; only after that does he look for friends, then for promotion, then for a creative hobby. Option c is right because physiological needs, the true first level, are not among the options, and safety needs come next. Exam trap: students who remember 'physiological first' may hunt for it and then guess; when it is missing, the lowest remaining level, safety, is the answer, while self-actualisation (d) sits at the top of the pyramid and is the last to be pursued.", "traps": {"a": "Esteem needs (achievement, recognition, self-respect) are the fourth level, above physiological, safety and belongingness needs, so they come later.", "b": "Love and belongingness needs (friendship, family, acceptance) are the third level and become the main motivator only after physiological and safety needs are met.", "d": "Self-actualisation, realising one's full potential, is the top level and a growth need; Maslow thought few people reach it, and it is the last, not the first, to be pursued."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core characteristic of borderline personality disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: chronic feelings of emptiness are a core characteristic of borderline personality disorder (BPD); the phrase appears word for word as criterion 7 in DSM-5 (2013). BPD is a Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, erratic) personality disorder: a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood. At least 5 of 9 criteria are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealising and devaluing; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substances, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour, threats or self-harm; affective instability from marked reactivity of mood; chronic feelings of emptiness; inappropriate, intense anger; and transient, stress-related paranoid ideas or severe dissociation. Marsha Linehan (1993) explained BPD through her biosocial theory and developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) to treat it. Example: Meera, 24, says she feels hollow inside; she cuts her arm after a friend cancels plans and swings from adoring to hating her boyfriend within a day. Option c is right because chronic emptiness is a BPD criterion exactly as written. Exam trap: each other option points to a different disorder: disorganised speech (b) is a psychotic symptom of schizophrenia, and repetitive, ritualistic behaviour (d) suggests OCD or autism. Key note: affective instability (criterion 6) is also a BPD feature, which is why persistent mood swings (a) tempt; but BPD mood shifts usually last a few hours and only rarely more than a few days, while 'persistent mood swings' points more to cyclothymia or bipolar disorder. Chronic emptiness is the criterion as written, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Persistent mood swings suggest cyclothymic or bipolar disorder, where mood states last days to weeks; BPD has affective instability, but its shifts are brief (hours to a few days) and reactive to events.", "b": "Disorganised speech (frequent derailment or incoherence) is a positive symptom of schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders, not a BPD criterion.", "d": "Repetitive, ritualistic behaviours belong to obsessive-compulsive disorder (compulsions) or autism spectrum disorder (restricted, repetitive behaviour), not to BPD."}}]});
